// Generated from Luther's "Activity Library: U6 to U16" (Claude Docs, October
// 2026) — 476 sourced activities, games and rule options by age. Regenerate
// rather than hand-edit: the drill ids are derived from each item's `key`, so
// editing a name here would orphan the drill a session plan points at.
// Decoded once by `ActivityLibrary.shared`.
import Foundation

extension ActivityLibrary {
    static let embeddedJSON = #####"""
{"version":1,"tabs":[{"age":"U6","title":"Activity Library: U6 and Under","guidance":["**Sources.** \"Mini\" is FIBA's [Mini-Basketball coaches manual](https://assets.fiba.basketball/image/upload/documents-corporate-wabc-coaching-manual-mini-basketball-eng.pdf); its [Chinese edition](https://assets.fiba.basketball/image/upload/documents-corporate-wabc-coaching-manual-mini-basketball-chn.pdf) has the same page numbers. \"Start Coaching\" is FIBA's [Start Coaching practice plans](https://assets.fiba.basketball/image/upload/documents-corporate-wabc-start-coaching-eng-practice-plans.pdf). \"Jr. NBA\" is the [Rookie practice plans](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/), which carry videos for some activities.","**Chinese names.** A Chinese name is shown only where FIBA's Chinese edition gives one, so coaches can find the page. Some of those names are poor translations; see the Sources and checks tab.","**Age guidance.** FIBA's youngest band is ages 5 to 7. It asks for games and guided discovery, with running, jumping, changing direction, throwing and catching ahead of technique (Mini p.2).","**Equipment.** FIBA gives a size 3 ball and a 1.83 m basket for U6 (Mini p.33). China's 小篮球规则 (Mini-Basketball Rules) allow baskets of 1.80 m or lower for kindergarten children.","**Running it.** Set each activity as a challenge and keep it short. FIBA says no activity should run too long (Mini p.10).","**Sorting.** The split between this tab and the U7 tab is mine, not FIBA's. The simpler activities are here. Those with named stops and pivots, a set defender or two skills in a row are on the U7 tab.","**Under 5.** No source opened lists activities for children under 5. For them, use only the Block 1 rows. That is a judgment, not a sourced rule.","**Stronger groups.** FIBA says to coach to each player's level, not the age label (Mini p.5). A strong U6 group can borrow from the U7 tab."]},{"age":"U7","title":"Activity Library: U7","guidance":["**Same FIBA band.** U7 players are still inside FIBA's 5 to 7 band, so games come first here too (Mini p.2). Source names are explained on the U6 and under tab.","**Ball.** Size 4 fits two sources: FIBA's [Dos and Don'ts](https://assets.fiba.basketball/image/upload/documents-corporate-mini-basketball-mini-basketball-dos-and-donts-eng.pdf) for ages 6 to 7, and China's 小篮球规则 (Mini-Basketball Rules) for age 8 and under. FIBA's manual instead names a softer size 5 for U8 (Mini p.33).","**Basket.** FIBA gives 6 to 8 feet (1.83 to 2.43 m) for ages 5 to 7 (Mini p.2; metres from p.33). China's rules give 2.35 m for ages 7 to 8.","**Teaching.** FIBA's method: start the game fast, stop after 4 to 5 minutes to ask questions, then change a rule. Technical points go in those pauses (Mini p.8–9).","**Defence.** FIBA asks only that a defender stay in front of the opponent. It cautions against teaching defensive footwork early (Mini p.30)."]},{"age":"U8","title":"Activity Library: U8","guidance":["**Age bands from here.** China's national competitions (U8, U10, U12) and most sources group players in two-year bands (Zhejiang's provincial league, with U7, U8 and U9 groups, is an exception), and no activity source opened is split by single years, so the tabs after U7 run U8 (age 8), U10 (ages 9–10), U12 (11–12), U14 (13–14) and U16 (15–16). Each tab adds to the ones before it. A group that is ahead can borrow from the next tab; FIBA says to coach to each player's level, not the age label (Mini p.5).","**What FIBA asks at 8–9.** Coordination, mobility and fitness still matter more than basketball-specific skills. Dribbling, passing, shooting and defending come in through games, \"without rigorous 'drilling'\" (Mini p.4).","**Where the rows come from.** Most new rows are from the Jr. NBA Rookie plans. Jr. NBA says its levels go by skill, not age; the Positive Coaching Alliance, which helped write the curriculum, describes Rookie as \"typically ages 8 and under\". Others are from USA Basketball's Introductory Level (its Fundamentals stage is about ages 6–9), Switzerland's J+S-Kids lessons for ages 8–10, FIBA, and the French federation's Bouge au basket sheets for primary pupils.","**Ball and basket.** The sources disagree. FIBA: a softer size 5 ball at U8 and no basket height given (Mini p.33). China's 2017 小篮球规则 (Mini-Basketball Rules): size 4 and 2.35 m for ages 7–8. NBA and USA Basketball: size 5 and 8 ft (2.44 m) for ages 7–8. German federation: size 4, 2.05–2.60 m ([Miniregeln](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf)). A Yanqing district explainer published for the 2026 national finals gives 2.60 m for U8 ([report](https://peking.bjd.com.cn/content/s6a72f464e4b0e45f3fd5a7c7.html)); it does not say the finals used it, and no CBA document confirming that height was found.","**Competition in China.** The 2026 中国小篮球 (China Mini-Basketball) national finals had a U8 mixed division playing four 6-minute quarters, and every player needed skill level 3 in the CBA's 篮球技能等级 (Basketball Skill Level) system ([Xinhua](https://www.news.cn/sports/20260518/662850cdc7e84c01b9cef24222e80151/c.html); [China Sports Daily via Sina](https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml)). Under-10s may enter the test at any level from 1 to 4 ([CBA measures, report](https://www.sport.gov.cn/n20001280/n20067662/n20067613/c28355010/content.html)). The test is a timed course; level 1 includes a low backward defensive slide and a bounce pass into a net, and each error adds 2 seconds ([launch report](https://t.m.youth.cn/transfer/index/url/sports.youth.cn/ssjj/202407/t20240715_15382084.htm)). The full item list for each level is not in any public text found. Changzhou's 2026 city rules play U8 as full-court 4 on 4 on a 12 × 15 m court ([regulations](https://tyj.changzhou.gov.cn/html/tyj/2026/FMAJJNIC_0706/25449.html)).","**Activity length.** FIBA's Start Coaching workbook: each activity runs 5–10 minutes, and no player should wait more than 20–30 seconds before being involved (Coach Workbook p.26). FIBA's Level 1 coaching course gives 7–10 minutes as a suggested maximum before a change of activity or a break ([WABC Level 1, 2.1.5](https://wabc-chn.fiba.com/?p=235)).","**Session length.** For ages 7–8 the [NBA and USA Basketball guidelines](https://youthguidelines.nba.com/) recommend one practice a week of 30–60 minutes, at most 3 hours of organised basketball a week, at least 2 rest days a week and at most 4 months a year. A 90-minute session is above that.","**Specialising.** The same guidelines say: \"Delay specialization in basketball until at least age 14.\" USA Basketball's Introductory Level asks for basketball once or twice a week plus daily activity in other sports. Training to be elite does not change this advice in any source opened.","**Defence.** Player-to-player only, one defender on the ball (FIBA \"No Double Team\", Mini p.34), and no taking the ball from a player's hands. FIBA stresses staying in front of the opponent over defensive footwork (Mini p.30); the Jr. NBA rows on this tab do teach a stance and slides.","**Injury-prevention warm-up.** No basketball programme for this age was found. FIFA 11+ Kids is a football warm-up for ages 7–12 with trial evidence; it is listed at the end of Block 1 as an option."]},{"age":"U10","title":"Activity Library: U10","guidance":["**Who.** Ages 9 and 10, China's U10 category. Everything on the U8 tab still applies.","**What FIBA asks.** At 8–9, motor abilities remain more important than basketball specifics, and dribbling, passing, shooting and defending come in without rigorous drilling (Mini p.4). From 10, more emphasis on fundamentals, contested activities followed by a return to uncontested ones, no specialist roles, and \"Often 'small sided' games such as 3 on 3 and 4 on 4 are better for children at this age\" (Mini p.5). The German federation's 2016 coaching guide, in its U10 section, puts it as \"Techniktraining ist Spieltraining\" (technique training is game training), with many ball contacts and decisions for everyone ([DBB guide, 2016](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2021/10/1.10-Leitfaden-fuer-das-Training-mit-Kindern-und-Jugendlichen.pdf) p.6–7).","**Where the rows come from.** Most new rows are from the Jr. NBA Starter plans, which the Positive Coaching Alliance describes as \"typically ages 9-11\". Others are from FIBA, from Switzerland's J+S-Kids lessons (ages 8–10) and from the Swiss Federal Office of Sport's Basketball at School material (ages 6–15).","**Ball and basket.** FIBA: 8 ft (2.43 m) at U10 (Mini p.33). China's 2017 rules: size 5 and 2.60 m for ages 9–10; a Yanqing district explainer published for the 2026 national finals gives 2.75 m for U10; it does not say the finals used it, and no CBA document confirms it. NBA and USA Basketball, ages 9–11: size 6 and 9 ft. German federation U10: size 5 (lighter models allowed) and 2.60 m. Research: boys aged 9–11 made more dribbles, passes and catches, and shot more accurately, with a 440 g ball than with the 485 g regulation ball of the same size (Arias 2012); children aged 9–12 made more shots at an 8 ft basket than at 10 ft, most clearly the 9–10-year-olds (Chase 1994).","**Competition in China.** The 2026 national finals had U10 boys and U10 mixed divisions, four 6-minute quarters, and required skill level 4. Applicants over 10 may start the skill test at level 5, and above level 5 must go up one level at a time. Changzhou's 2026 city rules play U10 as full-court 4 on 4, 12 × 15 m, 2.6 m basket, size 5 ball.","**Session length.** For ages 9–11 the [NBA and USA Basketball guidelines](https://youthguidelines.nba.com/) recommend two practices a week of 45–75 minutes, at most 5 hours of organised basketball a week, at least 2 rest days a week and at most 5 months a year. Basketball England's U10 session is 60 minutes, starting with 15 minutes of fun games ([Playbook](https://www.basketballengland.co.uk/media/7138/the-pdf-playbook.pdf) p.25). A 90-minute session is above both.","**Training and games.** USA Basketball (Learning to Train, about ages 8–12) recommends 70% training to 30% competition. Canada Basketball (Learn to Train, girls 8–11, boys 9–12) recommends 75:25 and warns that \"Too many competitions waste valuable training time\" ([ADM](https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf) p.36–38). Canada also says everyone should play every position at this stage.","**No screens.** The CBA's 2026 U12 camp plan says no on-ball screens under 12 (media report); Germany's mini rules ban screens and hand-offs up to U12; Ireland bans ball screens at U11. There are no screening activities on this tab.","**Injury-prevention warm-up.** The FIFA 11+ Kids option on the U8 tab covers ages 7–12."]},{"age":"U12","title":"Activity Library: U12","guidance":["**Who.** Ages 11 and 12, China's U12 category, the last mini-basketball age. Everything on the U10 tab still applies.","**What FIBA asks at 10–12.** More emphasis on fundamentals and more analytical feedback, but no specialist roles, and ideas for attack and defence that \"should be simple at the beginning\". Small-sided 3 on 3 and 4 on 4 are often better, with the aim of playing 5 on 5 \"in a slightly more organised way\" (Mini p.5). The German federation's U11/U12 goals add systematic technique training, basic defensive technique, and passing and moving as a group ([DBB guide, 2016](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2021/10/1.10-Leitfaden-fuer-das-Training-mit-Kindern-und-Jugendlichen.pdf) p.7–8). FIBA's Level 1 coaching course for under-12s: \"perfecting basketball fundamentals is not the most important aspect\", making practice fun is \"the most important consideration\", and \"all players should play all positions\" ([WABC Level 1, 3.2.2](https://wabc-chn.fiba.com/?p=268)). Basketball England's U12 list: more complex footwork, shooting on the move, defence, rebounding, pivoting and passing under pressure.","**Where the rows come from.** Jr. NBA Starter and All-Star (plus two MVP dribbling drills), FIBA's 4 on 4 chapter (Mini p.44–47), the CBA's 2026 U12 camp plan as reported in the media, and Switzerland's Federal Office of Sport. The Positive Coaching Alliance aims Jr. NBA All-Star at coaches of teenagers; using it at U12 is a judgment for players training toward elite level, in line with Jr. NBA's advice that elite teams start with All-Star or MVP.","**Ball and basket.** FIBA: size 5 at U12, no height given (Mini p.33). China's 2017 rules: size 5 and 2.75 m for ages 11–12; a Yanqing district explainer published for the 2026 finals lists 3.05 m for U12 (no CBA document confirms the finals' heights), and Changzhou's city rules use 3.05 m with a size 7 ball, a local choice. NBA and USA Basketball, ages 12–14: girls size 6, boys size 7, 10 ft. Germany: size 5 and 2.60 m up to U12. Ireland U12: size 5, regulation baskets.","**Competition in China.** The 2026 national finals had U12 boys and U12 girls divisions with 8-minute quarters and required skill level 5. Reported level 5 items: a one-handed pass into a net, behind-the-back and step-back dribbles, and lay-ups with the right and left hand, underhand and overhand; a two-handed pass where one hand is required counts as an error and adds 2 seconds ([launch report](https://t.m.youth.cn/transfer/index/url/sports.youth.cn/ssjj/202407/t20240715_15382084.htm)). Two Jr. NBA MVP dribbling rows for those moves are in Block 1. Changzhou plays U12 as full-court 5 on 5.","**Rules that shape this tab.** No on-ball screens under 12 (CBA camp plan, media report: \"12岁以下…不做有球掩护\", no on-ball screens under 12); Germany bans screens and hand-offs to U12. Player-to-player only (FIBA: no zone until U16, Mini p.34; Ireland). No double-teams (Ireland U12, Germany). Help defence is allowed (Ireland U12).","**What the CBA's 2026 U12 camps worked on.** As reported in the media: every session ran warm-up and activation, then technique, then team tactics, then stretching and recovery; training centred on drive-and-kick and pass-and-cut; the camps used 贝壳防守 (shell defence), 分割线防守 (help-line defence: weak-side defenders adjust to the ball and stop cuts), 5Y动态进攻 (5Y motion offence: continuous passing, cutting and refilling spots, no set plays) and the 维克森林训练法 (Wake Forest drill: transition that adds players each change of possession, 2 on 1 up to 5 on 5); one camp dropped its all-star game to put \"团队篮球优先\" (team basketball first) ([report](https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20260829A059G300); [camp summary](https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20260902A06XWC00)). The reports give no set-ups, so these are not rows; Shell Drill – 4 on 4, 3 on 1 Drive and Kick Reaction, 3-Person Pass, Cut and Replace and 3 on 1 Transition on this tab train the same ideas.","**Long shots.** Two rows include 3-point shots. FIBA counts shots from outside the 3-point line as 2 in mini-basketball because young players often lack the technique or strength for long shots (Mini p.33), and Basketball Ireland does the same at U12. FIBA's Start Coaching handbook adds: \"When young players shoot from \"beyond their range\" they will usually have poor technique\" (Facilitator Handbook p.97).","**Session length.** NBA and USA Basketball: ages 9–11, 45–75 minutes twice a week, at most 5 hours a week; ages 12–14, 60–90 minutes 2–4 times a week, at most 10 hours, at least 1 rest day a week, at most 7 months a year. Basketball England U12: 75–90 minutes. A 90-minute session is at the top of the 12–14 and Basketball England ranges, and 15 minutes over the 75-minute maximum for ages 9–11, which covers 11-year-olds.","**Teaching through games.** In a Spanish school study, 11–12-year-olds taught through tactical games outperformed a direct-instruction group in 3 on 3 on decisions, dribbling, shooting, spacing and off-ball defence (González-Espinosa 2021). In a Scottish study, 12-year-olds taught through games made more than twice as many good off-ball decisions, with no difference in skill execution (Gray and Sproule 2011). Both were short school units (10 and 5 lessons); the Spanish pupils had no formal basketball training.","**Injury-prevention warm-up.** SHRed, a basketball-specific programme tested in players aged 11–18, is the first row of Block 1."]},{"age":"U14","title":"Activity Library: U14","guidance":["**Who.** Ages 13 and 14, China's U14 category. Everything on the U12 tab still applies except the under-12 bans on screens and on 3-point scoring.","**What the federations ask.** German federation U13/U14: steer technique toward competition technique, make athletic training a major focus, consolidate defensive technique, learn simple team structures, and value creativity over rigid sequences; train players for a position \"but not one-sidedly\" ([DBB guide, 2016](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2021/10/1.10-Leitfaden-fuer-das-Training-mit-Kindern-und-Jugendlichen.pdf) p.8–9). Basketball England U14: shooting, on-ball defence, passing on the move and movement off the ball ([Talent Plan](https://www.basketballengland.co.uk/media/7137/talent-plan.pdf) p.19); 5 on 5 \"is introduced but is underpinned by concepts worked through 1v1 to 3v3\" ([Playbook](https://www.basketballengland.co.uk/media/7138/the-pdf-playbook.pdf) p.27). Canada (Train to Train, phase 1: girls 11–13, boys 12–14): no zone defence or zone presses, 4 practices to 1 game, and train through 1 on 1, 2 on 2 and 3 on 3 ([ADM](https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf) p.40–42).","**What FIBA's coaching course asks at 13–14.** \"Coaches must go into more depth concerning the development of technical fundamentals and individual tactical decisions (the decisions taken on the 1x1, 2x2, 3x3, etc.)\", without going too fast. More experienced players \"must use their non-preferred hand\"; all players should be able to fast break from any position and be introduced to perimeter and post skills; and success can be defined by the right process, not only points ([WABC Level 1, 3.2.2](https://wabc-chn.fiba.com/?p=268)). FIBA's older book for this age (Basketball for Young Players, 2000, since replaced by FIBA's newer coaches manual) adds: \"Exclusively individual defense must be trained\", at most 25% new material in any practice, and tall players do the same drills as the others; it prints 24 complete 120-minute sessions for 13–14-year-olds ([chapter 7, Iceland federation copy](<https://kki.is/library/Skrar/06.CAPITULO%206%20(Ingles).pdf>)).","**Where the rows come from.** Jr. NBA All-Star (the Positive Coaching Alliance aims it at coaches of teenagers), the more basic Jr. NBA MVP items and two Jr. NBA Starter screening activities, plus Switzerland's Federal Office of Sport and two training studies.","**Ball and basket.** 10 ft (3.05 m). NBA and USA Basketball, ages 12–14: girls size 6, boys size 7. Basketball New Zealand U14: boys size 6, girls size 5. Basketball Ireland U14: size 6. China's junior high league (players 15 and under): boys size 7, girls size 6. Changzhou U14: 5 on 5, 3.05 m, size 7. Research: in 51 U14 boys, a 2.80 m basket in competition produced more fast breaks and longer set attacks; shooting improved but not significantly (Ortega-Toro 2021).","**Competition in China.** The 全国U14青少年篮球联赛 (National U14 Youth League) is run by the CBA and the China Student Sports Federation; its 2026 final stage is 23–29 November in Yanqing, Beijing, and Zhejiang and Hangzhou girls' teams qualified ([report](https://www.sohu.com/a/1079772830_138481)). The 2026 中国初中篮球联赛 (China Junior High League) plays 4 × 8 minutes and splits each 12-player roster into groups A and B for the first two quarters ([Shaanxi notice](https://finance.sina.com.cn/wm/2026-05-25/doc-inhzchzh3486712.shtml)). No U14 regulation text with shot clock or defence rules was found.","**Defence rules differ.** Zone: FIBA's manual says not until U16 (Mini p.34), and FIBA's Start Coaching workbook says it \"is not recommended for players under 14\"; Ireland and New Zealand ban it at U14; Canada bans it in phase 1; USA Basketball allows all defences at 12–14. Double-teams: banned at U14 in Ireland, allowed in the USA. This tab has no zone or trapping rows. Full-court pressure is allowed in both.","**Session length.** NBA and USA Basketball, ages 12–14: 60–90 minutes, 2–4 times a week, at most 10 hours a week (players near that limit should not play another organised sport at the same time), at least 1 rest day a week and at most 7 months a year. Basketball England U14: 90 minutes. The 90-minute session fits.","**Specialising and growth.** NBA and USA Basketball: \"Delay specialization in basketball until at least age 14.\" Puberty starts on average at 12.5–13 for boys and 11–11.5 for girls (Canada Basketball).","**Strength work.** The German federation makes athletic training a major focus at U13/U14. The 2014 international consensus and the NSCA position statement support resistance training for young players when it is designed and supervised by qualified professionals, technique first, 2–3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days ([Lloyd 2014](https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2013-092952); Faigenbaum 2009). That is separate from the 90-minute court session."]},{"age":"U16","title":"Activity Library: U16","guidance":["**Who.** Ages 15 and 16. Everything on the U14 tab still applies, except its no-zone and no-double-team rules (see Rules below).","**What the federations ask.** German federation U15/U16: more intense technique training, position-specific and under game-like conditions; basketball-specific athleticism; the start of strength training; more complex team tactics; and coordinating sport and school loads ([DBB guide, 2016](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2021/10/1.10-Leitfaden-fuer-das-Training-mit-Kindern-und-Jugendlichen.pdf) p.9–11). Basketball England U16: more speed, consistency and accuracy, \"early position specific work\" and a broad range of finishes ([Talent Plan](https://www.basketballengland.co.uk/media/7137/talent-plan.pdf) p.19), and 5 on 5 \"underpinned with the concepts of 3v3 and 4v4\" ([Playbook](https://www.basketballengland.co.uk/media/7138/the-pdf-playbook.pdf) p.28). Canada (Train to Train, phase 2: girls 13–15, boys 14–16): the regulation game with a shot clock, and zone defences and presses only once players use their basic skills well in competition; late maturers must still get chances to play ([ADM](https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf) p.46).","**What FIBA asks at 15–16.** FIBA's Level 1 course: keep the long-term view but measure each player's needs in more detail, \"what are they missing?\" ([WABC Level 1, 3.2.2](https://wabc-chn.fiba.com/?p=268)). FIBA's 2000 book (Basketball for Young Players, since replaced): training \"more analytical and individualised\"; prepare 15–16-year-olds for \"progressively, higher loads of physical work\"; every player, guard or forward included, learns to play with the back to the basket in the low post; and practice built around decision-making ([chapter 8, Iceland federation copy](<https://kki.is/library/Skrar/07.CAPITULO%207%20(Ingles).pdf>)).","**Where the rows come from.** Mostly Jr. NBA MVP, the level at which \"players will also begin to specialize and fit into more specific roles and positions\"; plus one Jr. NBA All-Star game (Circle Trap 3 on 3), one FIBA decision game (Low-post decision 3 on 3) and the SHRed warm-up. Position rows are marked BIGS or GUARDS as Jr. NBA labels them. Basketball England's ages-and-stages summary still asks for an \"all-round\" player at 15–18 for women and 16–18 for men ([summary](https://www.basketballengland.co.uk/media/3802/summary-of-our-basketball-development-model-ages-and-stages.pdf)), so every player should get both.","**Rules.** Full FIBA rules: Ireland from U15, USA Basketball for high school (four 10-minute periods, 24-second shot clock with a 14-second reset after an offensive rebound, boys size 7, girls size 6). Zone: FIBA allows it from U16 (Mini p.34), but Basketball New Zealand still bans it at U16. FIBA's 2000 book says that at 15–16 \"it will be enough to start with one simple alignment (2-1-2, 2-3)\", built from part drills up to 5 on 5, with other alignments and specific press defence left until 17–18. Its zone drills depend on diagrams that could not be read, so this tab has no zone rows; that is a gap.","**Competition in China.** National U15 and U17 leagues exist (the 2026 U15 boys' final was 14–20 August in Jinjiang); no U15, U16 or U17 regulation text was found. The 2026 National Youth Three-Ball Games have under-18 groups only. Zhejiang's 2026 Provincial Games (Quzhou) include 5-a-side and 3x3 basketball; their age groups were not found.","**Session length.** NBA and USA Basketball, high school: 90–120 minutes, 3–4 times a week, at most 14 hours a week, at least 1 rest day a week and at most 9–10 months a year. Basketball England U16: 90 minutes. USA Basketball's Training to Compete stage (about 14–17) moves to 50% training and 50% competition.","**Strength and warm-up.** The German federation starts strength training at U15/U16; see the U14 tab for the consensus guidance. In 275 Slovenian male club players aged 13–17, a coach-led neuromuscular warm-up of about 14 minutes went with injury prevalence of 10.9% against 23.3% in teams without it ([Paravlić 2024](https://doi.org/10.1080/02640414.2024.2415215)). SHRed (U12 tab) is a ready-made basketball version."]}],"items":[{"key":"U6|routine|Walks and runs with the ball","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Walks and runs with the ball","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Walk on heels and on tiptoes. Move forward, backward and sideways holding the ball high, low or behind the back.","source":"Mini p.10, items 1–2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Heel flicks and high knees","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Heel flicks and high knees","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Run so the heels touch the ball held behind the back. Run so the knees touch the ball held in front.","source":"Mini p.10, items 3–4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Ball around the body","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Ball around the body","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"While walking or running, pass the ball around the head, body and legs, or toss it from hand to hand. Eyes stay up on the coach.","source":"Mini p.10, item 5","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Music stop","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Music stop","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Move with the ball while music plays and freeze when it stops. Next step: the same while dribbling freely with one or two hands.","source":"Mini p.10, items 6–7","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Which part of the hand?","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Which part of the hand?","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Bounce the ball with a fist, the side of the hand, the back of the hand, then palm and fingers. Ask which gave the most control.","source":"Mini p.11, item 26","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|High and low","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"High and low","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Dribble high, very high, low and very low. Sitting down, dribble with one finger, then two, up to five.","source":"Mini p.12, Body Orientation items 10–11","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Toss, clap, catch","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Toss, clap, catch","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Throw the ball high and catch it after clapping in front, behind the back or under the legs. Then touch the floor or turn around before the catch.","source":"Mini p.12, Spatial-Temporal item 4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Line explorers","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Line explorers","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Walk, run, jump and dribble along the lines and circles of the court.","source":"Mini p.12, Body Orientation items 1 and 4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Obstacle roll","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Obstacle roll","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Scatter cones, bottles or bags on the court. Players move around them rolling the ball with one hand, left then right. Then two hands, then feet, and last dribbling.","source":"Mini p.12, Spatial-Temporal item 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Clap the rhythm","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Clap the rhythm","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The coach claps a rhythm and the players dribble it, standing first and then on the move.","source":"Mini p.13, Spatial-Temporal item 12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Jack-Hammer Game","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Jack-Hammer Game","nameZh":"气锤游戏","howItRuns":"A leader dribbles high, low, slow and fast. The others copy.","source":"Mini p.17, item 8","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Car Game","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Car Game","nameZh":"汽车游戏","howItRuns":"Everyone dribbles to the coach's calls: start your engine (on the spot), first gear (walk), second gear (jog), top gear (sprint), reverse, red light, green light, left or right, pit stop (sprint back to the start).","source":"Mini p.17, item 9","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|routine|Hot Ball (Taps) and Around The World Wraps","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Hot Ball (Taps) and Around The World Wraps","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Tap the ball fast from hand to hand at the waist, in front of the face and overhead. Then wrap it around the waist, head and knees, ten times each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/1/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/1/"},{"key":"U6|routine|Follow the Leader","age":"U6","block":"routine","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Follow the Leader","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players line up behind a leader, a ball each, and copy the leader's action. The leader changes action every 5 to 15 seconds and hands over after one minute.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/"},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Pac Man","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Pac Man","nameZh":"吃豆人","howItRuns":"A tag game in which taggers and runners may move only along the court lines.","source":"Mini p.12, Body Orientation item 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Tiger Tail","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Tiger Tail","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player tucks a ribbon into the back of the shorts and tries to take other players' ribbons. On the whistle everyone freezes, and anyone who moves gives the tails they have collected to the nearest player. A lost tail can be replaced with one collected. Most ribbons wins. Add dribbling to make it harder.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 9","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Tail Game","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Tail Game","nameZh":"尾巴游戏","howItRuns":"Every child has a ball and a ribbon tail and dribbles inside a marked area. One child with no tail tries to grab tails for a set time. The player who grabs the most tails wins.","source":"Mini p.17, item 10","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Dribble Tag","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Dribble Tag","nameZh":"运球贴标签","howItRuns":"Three or four taggers dribble and chase the other players for a set time. Next step: everyone dribbles.","source":"Mini p.17, item 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|1-2-3 Stop","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"1-2-3 Stop","nameZh":"1-2-3停","howItRuns":"One player at half court faces away and calls \"1-2-3 Stop\" while the others dribble in from the baseline. On \"Stop\" the caller turns, and anyone seen moving goes back to the baseline. First to half court wins.","source":"Mini p.19, item 17","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Wolf and Sheep","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Wolf and Sheep","nameZh":"狼与羊游戏","howItRuns":"Everyone has a ball. On \"the wolf isn't here\" the sheep leave the key and dribble anywhere. On \"here comes the wolf\" the wolf may tag any sheep outside a key. Tagged sheep become wolves.","source":"Mini p.19, item 16","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Fisherman","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Fisherman","nameZh":"渔夫游戏","howItRuns":"The fish dribble from one baseline to the other past a fisherman at half court. Anyone tagged becomes a fisherman.","source":"Mini p.19, item 14","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Four Corners","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Four Corners","nameZh":"四角落游戏","howItRuns":"Four players stand at the corners of a half court and one in the middle. On \"go\" the corner players move to the next corner while the middle player tries to get to one first. FIBA allows running in place of dribbling.","source":"Mini p.18, item 13","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|smallGames|NBA Tag Game","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"NBA Tag Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players each pick one of two or three team names. The coach stands in the middle and calls a team, and that team runs to the far baseline without being tagged. Tagged players sit or kneel where they were tagged. Next step: everyone dribbles.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/"},{"key":"U6|smallGames|Transfer the Treasure","age":"U6","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Transfer the Treasure","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A coach at half court faces away, with cones around them as treasure. Players dribble out from the baseline, take a cone and dribble it back. If the coach turns and sees a player moving toward the treasure, the coach may chase and tag them; a tagged player loses the cone. First to three cones wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/"},{"key":"U6|compete|Target throw","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Target throw","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Throw balls of different sizes into bins, hoops and baskets at different heights, at marks on a wall, or to knock down pins.","source":"Mini p.21, items 1, 3 and 4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Throw up to a target","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Throw up to a target","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Sitting on the floor, throw into a bin placed on a bench, so the ball has to travel upward.","source":"Mini p.21, \"Vary the Target\"","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Every touch scores","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Every touch scores","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Free shooting in which hitting the ring, net or backboard also counts. Team version: 2 points for a basket, 1 for the ring, half a point for the backboard; first team to a set score wins.","source":"Mini p.21, items 2 and 6","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Stop on the signal","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Stop on the signal","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Run and stop at once on a signal. Then the same while dribbling. Then stop and stand on one foot.","source":"Mini p.13, Balance items 5 and 8","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Jump Stop Game","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Jump Stop Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Coach's arm up means go; arm down means jump stop. A player who is late or off balance takes three steps back. First across the court wins. Next step: with a ball.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/1/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/1/"},{"key":"U6|compete|Partner swaps","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Partner swaps","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs sit face to face with a ball each and swap balls by rolling, bouncing and throwing.","source":"Mini p.14, Coordination item 21","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Clap before the catch","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Clap before the catch","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"In pairs, A passes to B, and B must clap before catching.","source":"Mini p.14, Coordination item 25","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Knock the coin","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Knock the coin","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs bounce-pass to each other, trying to knock a coin on the floor toward the partner.","source":"Mini p.14, Coordination item 18","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Lay-up footwork","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"ballMovement","group":null,"kind":"activity","name":"Lay-up footwork","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players jump to high-five the coach. Then, from the block, they take one step with the foot away from the baseline and jump at the basket. Then add a ball: a tennis ball or a size 3 for young children. Use both sides and more than one basket so nobody waits long.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Touch game","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"games","group":"Compete","kind":"game","name":"Touch game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with a ball each dribble and try to touch the partner's ball, knee or back. Most touches in a set time wins.","source":"Mini p.13, Coordination item 4; p.16, Speed item 3","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Dribble races","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"games","group":"Compete","kind":"game","name":"Dribble races","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Race dribbling over 15 to 20 metres, in and out of cones, or as a relay.","source":"Mini p.15, Endurance items 1–3","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|Crocodile","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"games","group":"Compete","kind":"game","name":"Crocodile","nameZh":"鳄鱼游戏","howItRuns":"Players shoot from a spot close to the basket. A miss sends the shooter to the baseline. Each make rescues one player from there. If everyone ends up on the baseline, the crocodile wins.","source":"Mini p.22, item 9; Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|compete|All Together to the Basket","age":"U6","block":"compete","type":"games","group":"Compete","kind":"game","name":"All Together to the Basket","nameZh":"全体投篮","howItRuns":"Two teams stand back to back at half way, a ball each. On the signal everyone dribbles to the basket they face and shoots, with up to three tries, then dribbles back. Most makes wins.","source":"Mini p.23, item 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|Hoop Ball","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Hoop Ball","nameZh":"篮圈球","howItRuns":"Two small teams, 3 to 7 a side. A teammate stands in a floor hoop at each end, and a team scores by getting the ball to that teammate. Dribbling and passing are both allowed. Change the player in the hoop often.","source":"Mini p.31","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|More attackers than defenders","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"More attackers than defenders","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Uneven teams play keep-away. A team scores by making a set number of passes, or by passing the ball into the key.","source":"Mini p.16, Anticipation and Choice item 3; p.25","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|3 on 3 across the court","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 across the court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A small-sided game played across the court on side baskets, with attack and defence at both ends.","source":"Mini p.33, p.35, p.41","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|No grab","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options","kind":"rule","name":"No grab","nameZh":"“不抓球”规则","howItRuns":"Defenders may not take the ball out of a player's hands. They may intercept passes.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|No double team","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options","kind":"rule","name":"No double team","nameZh":"“无夹击球队”","howItRuns":"Only one defender may guard the player with the ball.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|Ring, net or backboard scores","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options","kind":"rule","name":"Ring, net or backboard scores","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A shot that hits the ring, net or backboard earns points. Use it when the basket cannot be lowered.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|Everyone plays","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options","kind":"rule","name":"Everyone plays","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player plays in each half.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U6|game|Light whistle on travels","age":"U6","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options","kind":"rule","name":"Light whistle on travels","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Stop play, tell the player what happened and give the ball back. FIBA writes this for U10; applying it below U10 is a judgment.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Signal dribble","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Signal dribble","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Dribble in the middle of the court and respond to the coach's calls: change speed, change direction, change hand, go backwards.","source":"Mini p.17, item 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Dribble lines","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble lines","nameZh":"运球线","howItRuns":"Dribble along the court lines. On meeting another player, change hand, reverse or change direction.","source":"Mini p.17, item 7","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Statue Game","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Statue Game","nameZh":"雕像游戏","howItRuns":"Everyone dribbles and stops on the coach's signal, using the stop the coach names: jump stop or stride stop. A late stop or the wrong stop loses a point.","source":"Mini p.17, item 6","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Stop and face","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Stop and face","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Free dribbling. On the signal, stop and turn to face a basket, a sideline or the baseline, as called. The coach may also name the pivot foot and a forward or reverse pivot.","source":"Mini p.18, item 11","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Ball swap","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Ball swap","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone dribbles around the court. When two players come close, each takes over the other's ball without picking it up.","source":"Mini p.20, item 22","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Marker course","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Marker course","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 3 to 5 start on the baseline, with markers at both free-throw lines and at half court. At each marker the dribbler does the skill the coach has set: change hand, change speed, change direction, retreat dribble or jump stop.","source":"Mini p.20, item 24","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Dribble Chicken","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble Chicken","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two players dribble straight at each other, then change hands to go past. They keep dribbling throughout and may stop moving to avoid other pairs.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Copy Cat","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Copy Cat","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs. The dribbler faces the coach. The partner has their back to the coach and stays in front of the dribbler. The coach points, and the dribbler goes that way at any speed.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Weak hand, two balls","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Weak hand, two balls","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"For players who are ready: dribble with the non-preferred hand, or dribble two balls alternately, standing and then moving.","source":"Mini p.5; p.13, Coordination item 5","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Lay-up footwork, moving","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Lay-up footwork, moving","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Run in from the 3-point line, take the ball from the coach at the block, take one step and jump. Next step: dribble in from the 3-point line.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 3","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|1-2 Step Lay-Ups","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"1-2 Step Lay-Ups","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball. The shooter starts 3 to 6 feet (about 1 to 2 m) from the basket with no dribble, takes one or two steps and lays the ball off the square on the backboard. The partner rebounds. Swap after one minute.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/"},{"key":"U7|routine|Parallel Relay Race","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Parallel Relay Race","nameZh":"并行接力赛","howItRuns":"Three or four small teams on the baseline. The first player dribbles to the far basket, stops and shoots, with up to three shots. They rebound, return and high-five the next player. The shot can be off a jump stop or a lay-up. Most baskets in a set time wins.","source":"Mini p.24, item 6","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Pair Shooting","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Pair Shooting","nameZh":"结对投篮","howItRuns":"One player shoots; the partner rebounds and passes the ball out. Three shots, then swap. Pairs can race to a set score.","source":"Mini p.29, item 8","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Around the World","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Around the World","nameZh":"环游世界游戏","howItRuns":"The coach sets 6 to 10 spots inside the players' range. Players shoot in turn. A make moves the shooter to the next spot; a miss passes the turn to the next player. After three misses at one spot the shooter moves on anyway. First to make one from every spot wins. Give every player a ball to cut waiting.","source":"Mini p.22, item 11; Start Coaching, Plan 3","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|routine|Pick Your Shot","age":"U7","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Pick Your Shot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Cones mark shooting spots, some close and a few farther out. Teams line up at half court, and one player at a time dribbles to a cone and shoots. A make wins that cone for the team. Most cones wins. If makes are rare, move the cones closer or count hitting the rim.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/1/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/1/"},{"key":"U7|smallGames|Dribble Gang","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Dribble Gang","nameZh":"运球连连转","howItRuns":"Dribble tag in which a tagged player joins the taggers.","source":"Mini p.17, item 4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|smallGames|Dog and Hare Game","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Dog and Hare Game","nameZh":"小狗与野兔游戏","howItRuns":"Two teams, a ball each. The dogs start in the centre circle and the hares anywhere on court. On \"go\" the dogs dribble and try to touch hares; a hare who is touched stops and sits. Most touches in a set time wins.","source":"Mini p.18, item 12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|smallGames|Four Corners, dribbling","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Four Corners, dribbling","nameZh":"四角落游戏","howItRuns":"As on the U6 and under tab, with the corner players sprint-dribbling to the next corner.","source":"Mini p.18, item 13","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|smallGames|Relay Race with Pins","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Relay Race with Pins","nameZh":"标杆接力赛","howItRuns":"Two teams, one ball each. The first player dribbles with one hand and knocks down the pins along the path with the other, then dribbles back. The next player dribbles and stands the pins up again, and so on.","source":"Mini p.20, item 20","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|smallGames|Swipers Game","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Swipers Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams. One team dribbles. When the coach calls \"Swipers\", the other team tries to knock balls away. No contact: swipers touch only the ball. A player who loses the ball holds it overhead until a teammate rolls a ball between their legs. Two minutes, then swap.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/"},{"key":"U7|smallGames|20 Passes","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"20 Passes","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, a ball each. After each pass the passer runs to a sideline. First team to 20 passes wins, and a dropped pass costs one point. FIBA's manual runs the same game with a rule against passing straight back, and with questions between rounds (Mini p.8).","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|smallGames|20 Passes, Keeping Off","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"20 Passes, Keeping Off","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One ball. The team without it tries to intercept passes and may not take the ball out of a player's hands. Each defender guards one player. A team that wins the ball starts its own count to 20. In Start Coaching the count resets to zero if the ball hits the floor.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 3; Mini p.9","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|smallGames|4x3 Pass Tag","age":"U7","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"4x3 Pass Tag","nameZh":"4x3传球贴便签","howItRuns":"Three taggers pass one ball among themselves and try to touch one of the four runners with the ball held in the hands, never thrown. Start Coaching adds that taggers may pivot but not travel; FIBA's manual lets the coach allow dribbling. Runners stay inside a marked area such as the 3-point line. Count the tags in a set time, then change taggers. Jr. NBA's team version has a video ([Rookie Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/6/)).","source":"Mini p.32; Start Coaching, Plan 7","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Pairs Passing","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Pairs Passing","nameZh":"结对传球","howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball. The receiver steps away, cuts back and catches with the stop the coach names, jump or stride. They pivot to face the basket, then pivot back and pass. The English manual misprints the name as \"Paris Passing\".","source":"Mini p.25, item 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Dribble, stop, pivot, pass","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble, stop, pivot, pass","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Relay. Dribble to a set point, jump stop, pivot and pass back to the line, then jog back.","source":"Mini p.28, item 5","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Dribble, Pivot, Pass","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble, Pivot, Pass","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of at least four. The first player dribbles out and jump stops. The second runs behind them and defends the pass back to the line. The passer uses fakes and pivots and does not throw high over the top.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Defender in the middle","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Defender in the middle","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two players pass with a defender between them. Then groups of three and five with one or two defenders.","source":"Mini p.16, Anticipation and Choice item 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Timed passing","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Timed passing","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 2 to 5 count their passes in a set time, first with no defence and then against a defending team. Players stop as they catch.","source":"Mini p.15, Endurance item 6","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Defend the Circles","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1","kind":"activity","name":"Defend the Circles","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"An attacker and a defender start in a corner. The attacker has 10 seconds to dribble into the no-charge circle, the free-throw circle or the centre circle. The defender wins by keeping them out and does not need to take the ball.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|\"GO\"","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1","kind":"activity","name":"\"GO\"","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"An attacker and a defender pass the ball to each other while moving across the court. At any moment the attacker can go and drive to the basket. The defender tries to keep them out of the key.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 7","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|1 on 1 from every spot","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 from every spot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"FIBA lists 1 on 1 games from all positions on the court and gives no further detail.","source":"Mini p.16, Anticipation and Choice item 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Signal shots","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and finishing contests","kind":"activity","name":"Signal shots","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Stand with the back to the basket and pivot to shoot on the signal. Or start with the ball on the floor, pick it up and shoot on the signal.","source":"Mini p.22, items 13–14","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Dribbling and Shooting Relay Race","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and finishing contests","kind":"activity","name":"Dribbling and Shooting Relay Race","nameZh":"运球和投篮接力赛","howItRuns":"Two groups. The first player dribbles toward the basket, stops and shoots, with up to three tries, then returns. Most baskets in a set time wins.","source":"Mini p.23, item 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Dribble Slalom Relay Races","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and finishing contests","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble Slalom Relay Races","nameZh":"弯道运球接力赛","howItRuns":"Teams start at half way, dribble around cones and shoot. Most baskets in a set time, or first to a set score. In Start Coaching the players change hands at each cone and finish with a lay-up.","source":"Mini p.23, item 4; Start Coaching, Plan 6","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|compete|Swish Lay-up","age":"U7","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and finishing contests","kind":"activity","name":"Swish Lay-up","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The team tries to make lay-ups in a row that go in off the backboard without touching the ring. FIBA's target is 10. Players jump up at the basket, not forward toward the baseline.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 4","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Munnsy Hoops","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Munnsy Hoops","nameZh":"Munnsy（穆尼西）篮圈","howItRuns":"Teams of 3 to 5 and three floor hoops. A team scores by passing to a teammate standing in any hoop, and nobody may stand in a hoop for more than 2 seconds. The scoring team keeps the ball. The defence wins it only by intercepting a pass or stealing from a dribbler.","source":"Mini p.31","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|5 Point Passing","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 Point Passing","nameZh":"5分传球","howItRuns":"Two equal teams. Each completed pass earns 1 point, and a pass to the teammate in the marked 5-point zone earns 5. Defenders try to intercept; after an interception the passing team gets the ball back. Play for a set time and rotate the player in the zone.","source":"Mini p.32","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Pass and Win","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Pass and Win","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Equal teams. One team starts in the centre circle and scores by passing to a teammate inside the no-charge circle. Dribbling in does not score. Each defender stays with one player and may intercept passes but not take the ball from a player's hands.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Tallyball Touchdown","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Tallyball Touchdown","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Equal teams. A team must make 6 passes and then pass to a teammate in the key to score a point. The count resets if the ball touches the floor.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 3","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|5 Possessions","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 Possessions","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Equal teams. The offence earns 1 point for getting the ball into the key and 2 points for a made shot from inside the key. After five possessions the teams swap. Each defender stays with one player, and the coach may limit dribbles.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|3 on 3 or 4 on 4 across the court","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 or 4 on 4 across the court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"As on the U6 and under tab. With four a side the passes are shorter. With only a half court, FIBA's stand-in for transition is that every player touches half way after each change of possession before rejoining play.","source":"Mini p.41, p.44","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Pass before a shot","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"Pass before a shot","nameZh":"“投篮前传球”","howItRuns":"The team must make 1 to 3 passes before it may shoot.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Points for a pass","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"Points for a pass","nameZh":"“传球得分”","howItRuns":"1 point for a pass to a player in the key, or 1 point when a shot follows a catch.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Inside counts more","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"Inside counts more","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A shot from inside the key is worth 3 points and from outside it 2.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Limit dribbles","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"Limit dribbles","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Restrict the number of dribbles to push passing.","source":"FIBA Dos and Don'ts","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Ball into the key first","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"Ball into the key first","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"No shot is allowed until the ball has gone into the key.","source":"FIBA Dos and Don'ts","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|No zone defence","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"No zone defence","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Player-to-player defence only. FIBA, the NBA with USA Basketball, and China's rules all say this for young players.","source":"Mini p.34; Sources and checks tab","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U7|game|Travel leeway","age":"U7","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U7","kind":"rule","name":"Travel leeway","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The 2018 China Mini-Basketball League U8 rules allowed 3 to 4 steps and 1 to 2 double dribbles outside the key. Beyond that the referee called the violation, explained it to the player and gave the ball to the other team. Inside the key the normal rules applied.","source":"Sources and checks tab","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|routine|Low, Middle, High Dribble","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Low, Middle, High Dribble","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each, starting on the sideline. Dribble low with the right hand for 20 seconds, eyes up, the left arm protecting the ball, then with the left hand. Repeat at middle height, then high (about shoulder level). The coach holds up fingers and players call out the number. Next step: partners face each other and one mirrors a leader who changes hand and height to \"lose\" them. Competitive version: partners shoulder to shoulder on opposite sides of a line, both dribbling with the outside hand, try to get two feet over the line.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/), 7","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Stationary Crossover Low, Middle, High","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Stationary Crossover Low, Middle, High","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Knees bent, swing the ball side to side low and quick below the knees for 20 seconds, then at knee level for 20 seconds, then higher for 20 seconds. Next step: a partner mirrors a leader who changes level; then add 1, 2 or 3 dribbles before each crossover.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/); Starter Plan 1","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Dribble Balance","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble Balance","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Stand on the right leg, knee slightly bent, and dribble with the right hand; then the left leg and left hand. 3 x 20 seconds each leg. Next step: a partner balancing on one leg leads changes of height and hand, and the other mirrors.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Cone Dribbling","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Cone Dribbling","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Even lines, five cones in a straight line from sideline to sideline for each line. Zig-zag through the cones, switching hands at each change of direction, and stop past the last cone. The next player starts when the one ahead is halfway. Then come back the other way. Next step: one or two players stand in place of cones as defenders; or run it as a relay.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/), 10; Starter Plan 1","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Two balls","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Two balls","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"In pairs with two balls, swap balls on the coach's signal, first by handing over, then by taking over the dribble. Then both partners dribble two balls each and swap on the signal. Next step: one player dribbles 4 balls.","source":"Mini p.13, Coordination items 7, 8","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|routine|Forward and back in pairs","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Forward and back in pairs","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with a ball each. A dribbles forward while B dribbles backward, and they change on the coach's signal. The coach uses a spoken signal sometimes and a visual one at other times, so players keep their heads up. Next step: A picks any direction and B goes the opposite way.","source":"Mini p.14, Coordination item 14","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|routine|Lay-Up High Five Drill","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Lay-Up High Five Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line, no ball. The coach shows a right-hand lay-up: step with the right foot, second step, jump off the left foot, extend the right hand. Each player does the two steps saying \"1, 2\" and high-fives the coach's raised hand. Then the left side with the opposite footwork. 3 minutes each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Lay-Up Skips Drill","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Lay-Up Skips Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone on the baseline moves together on the coach's \"1, 2, jump\"; the coach says which foot steps first. Jumping off one foot, players reach up with the opposite hand. Down and back twice.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Perfect Shot No Basket Drill","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Perfect Shot No Basket Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"No basket. Knees bent, elbow under the ball, shoot up and hold the follow-through. A perfect shot goes up, bounces in front and comes back into the hand without the hand moving. First to two perfect shots wins; 3 x 8 attempts. Harder: a dribble move, or a squat, push-up or sideline run, before each shot.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/); Starter Plan 7","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Block to Block Shooting Drill","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Block to Block Shooting Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners at a basket. The shooter shoots from one block, runs to the other block and gets the ball back from the rebounder. 30 seconds, then switch. Partners count makes to beat their last score. Aim for the square on the backboard.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 11](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/11/), 6; Starter Plan 8","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/11/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Self-Toss Rebound Drill","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Self-Toss Rebound Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three lines at each basket. Toss the ball up with two hands, move to it, jump and catch it at its highest point, land, take one shot, rebound and return to the line. 3 x 8. Competitive version: no balls; the coach shoots off the backboard, the first three in line go for the rebound and the winner gets an unguarded shot.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/), 7","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Shooting Line Game","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Shooting Line Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each line at a shooting spot is a team. Shoot once, get your own rebound, pass to the next player and go to the back. First team to the set number of makes (the plan uses 8) wins, then lines change spots. Variation: spell a word, one letter per make.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Run to That Line","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Run to That Line","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The coach walks the court with the players, naming spaces such as half court, the 3-point line and the sideline, and players repeat each name. The coach then calls a movement and a line, for example \"High knees to the baseline!\", for five lines. Movements: forward, backward or lateral march or skip, high knees, quick feet, and actions from other sports such as swimming arms or skating. Next step: players choose their own movements, or move as a chain of partners holding hands.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|routine|March, High Knees, Quick Feet","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"March, High Knees, Quick Feet","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The movements used in Run to That Line. March: walk with high knees, toes pulled up and arms at 90 degrees, then add backward and lateral marching. High knees: drive the knee up with the foot flexed, alternating legs, elbows at 90 degrees. Quick feet (also listed as Side Push): in an athletic stance, move sideways by pushing off the back foot, the left foot to go right; push rather than step or pull.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Smacks","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"More dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Smacks","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player holds a ball in front with both hands and smacks it with each hand in turn for 20 seconds, to get comfortable with the ball and with contact on it.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/"},{"key":"U8|routine|One Hand Moving Rolls","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"More dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"One Hand Moving Rolls","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Start on the baseline in a good stance with the ball on the floor and one hand behind it. Walk to half court rolling the ball with the hand kept on it, then turn and come back the same way. Repeat with the other hand.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Dribbling in a Linear Path","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"More dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribbling in a Linear Path","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player starts in triple threat with the ball at waist level on the shooting-hand side and walks in a straight line, dribbling with the shooting hand slightly behind the top of the ball. Painted floor lines help keep players straight. Heads stay up: players call out the number of fingers the coach holds up at the far end. The ball stays at or below the waist and is not slapped. Next step: walk, then jog, then sprint.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.47","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|routine|Two balls backwards, figure 8","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"More dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Two balls backwards, figure 8","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Walk backwards dribbling two balls, then run. Players keep \"chin to shoulder\" to see behind them, or work in pairs with the partner walking forwards and making sure the dribbler does not run into anything. Figure 8: dribble a figure 8 with the legs apart, then dribble figure 8s in pairs.","source":"Mini p.13, Coordination items 6, 9, 10","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|routine|Form shooting progression","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"More shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Form shooting progression","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four stages, each first with the shooting hand only and then with the guide hand on the side of the ball. On one knee (the knee opposite the shooting hand), raise the ball to shoulder level, pause to check form and shoot it up to oneself. Standing, add the legs by rising onto the balls of the feet without jumping. Against a wall from about five feet (1.5 m), aiming at an exact spot. On a hoop, which can be lower at first, with the middle finger last to touch the ball. Good mechanics matter more than makes.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.68–75","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|routine|1-Step Form Drill","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"More shooting","kind":"activity","name":"1-Step Form Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines face each other across the lane. The first player pushes off the foot nearer half court to cover as much of the lane as possible, lands on the leg nearer the basket and swings the outside leg round into a shooting stance, arms as if holding a ball. They join the opposite line, whose first player goes next. After a minute, each push finishes with a jump as if shooting, landing on the same spot with the follow-through held up. Next step: toss a ball to the player crossing, and add the shot if appropriate.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|routine|Between-the-legs and ankle-lift shots","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"More shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Between-the-legs and ankle-lift shots","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players face the basket, a ball each. On the coach's signal they either bounce the ball from behind them between the legs, catch it and shoot; or, holding the ball between the ankles, jump to lift it into the air, catch it and shoot. For the ankle lift, catch after one bounce at first, then before the ball touches the floor.","source":"Mini p.22, items 15 and 17","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|routine|FIFA 11+ Kids","age":"U8","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Injury-prevention warm-up (option)","kind":"activity","name":"FIFA 11+ Kids","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"About 15 minutes, replacing the usual warm-up, one ball per player. Seven exercises in order: running game (stop on a call or a signal), skating jumps, single-leg stand with ball throws, push-up, single-leg jumps, \"Spiderman\" (its levels include tapping the ball with a foot, crawling and dribbling) and side rolls (falling technique). Each exercise has 5 levels: start at level 1, do not skip levels, and move up when all players can do it correctly. In a trial with 3,895 football players aged 7–12, teams asked to use it at least twice a week had an overall injury rate about half that of control teams (hazard ratio 0.52). It was designed for football, not basketball.","source":"[Rössler 2018](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-017-0834-8), coach manual in supplementary file 1","sourceUrl":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-017-0834-8"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Skip Tag","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Skip Tag","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Inside set boundaries such as half court. One player or the coach is \"it\". Everyone, the tagger included, may only skip. At each tag play pauses, and the tagged player also becomes \"it\" and helps tag. Next step: everyone dribbles while skipping, or runs if the coach prefers.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Freeze Tag","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Freeze Tag","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Inside set boundaries such as half court, one player or the coach is \"it\". A tagged player freezes until an unfrozen player touches them. The game ends when everyone is frozen; add taggers if needed. Two minutes. Next step: everyone except the taggers dribbles a ball. Competitive version: two teams; one team freezes the other as fast as possible, then roles switch, and the faster time wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Robbers and Police (Räuber und Polizei)","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Robbers and Police (Räuber und Polizei)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The robbers have a ball each; the police have none. Police touch robbers. A touched robber becomes a prisoner and stands still with arms out until a robber slaps their hand. Police score 1 point per touch and robbers 1 point per freed prisoner. Then swap roles.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2011/02/Basketball_1_Dribbeln_1_d.pdf) Lesson 1","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2011/02/Basketball_1_Dribbeln_1_d.pdf"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Lions and Tigers","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Lions and Tigers","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs of similar speed face each other 3 metres apart in the middle of the court, one ball per pair; one side are lions, the other tigers. On \"Lions\", the tigers chase and try to tag the lions, and tagged players go back to their starting spots. The coach can call \"right foot\", \"left foot\" or \"two feet\" for a balanced stop, then \"Go\". Keep a safety line well away from the wall. Next step: on \"change\", chasers and chased swap roles; side shuffles or skips; dribbling with either hand. Competitive version: 1 point per tag, then rotate to new partners.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Snake Dribbling","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Snake Dribbling","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone dribbles inside the 3-point arc. One player, the snake, starts without a ball. On \"Go\" the snake tags dribblers. A tagged player joins the snake by holding its shoulder with one hand and tags with the free hand. The snake grows until one player is left.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Four Corners, tag version","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Four Corners, tag version","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"As on the U6 and under tab, but the middle player tries to tag a player before they reach a corner. A tagged player stops where they are until another corner player touches them; then both dribble to the corner. It works best with at least 9 players.","source":"Mini p.19, item 15","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Mascot Tag","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Mascot Tag","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A half court is split by a line of cones from baseline to half court, and the sidelines are the goal lines. Two teams, every player with a ball, each named after the NBA mascot it thinks is fastest, line up at the cones facing each other. The coach tells a made-up story; when it names a team's mascot, that team dribbles back to its sideline while the other team chases to tag. One point per tag, and players keep their own score. Reset at the cones for a new story.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 11](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/11/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/11/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Coach, What Time Is It?","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Coach, What Time Is It?","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players start on one baseline; the coach stands near the far baseline, facing away. Players call together \"Coach, Coach, what time is it?\" The coach answers with a number from 1 to 12 (\"10 o'clock!\") and players take that many big steps forward. On \"Game time!\" the coach may turn and tag, and players run back to their baseline to be safe; anyone caught joins the coach. Next step: everyone dribbles a ball for each step, and on \"Layup time!\" all dribble to a hoop and try a lay-up before being tagged.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Dribble Chaos","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Dribble Chaos","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two players from each team start in the key, each with a ball. They dribble and try to knock opponents' balls out of the key. A player whose ball is knocked out, or who double dribbles, steps out and a teammate takes the ball into the key. Players outside may knock at opponents' balls but may not step into the key. Dribblers keep their heads up and push the ball hard.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 3","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Up or Down Game","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Up or Down Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, \"Up\" and \"Down\", a ball each. Cones are spread around the gym, as many standing up as face down, with at least one cone per player. Everyone dribbles around; Team Up stands cones up and Team Down turns them face down, using only their hands. Players keep moving and may not guard cones. At the end of the time, count the cones to find the winner. Next step: a set dribble move before turning a cone, such as 5 crossovers in a row; after a mistake the player goes to a different cone. Competitive version: all players against the coaches.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/), 4, 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Capture the Cones Game","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Capture the Cones Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four teams, one in each corner, every player dribbling a ball. Players take cones from the other corners and bring them back to their own; they may not guard their own corner. Coaches move around and tag players carrying cones, and a tagged player returns the cone where it came from. Players dribble at all times and should go for diagonal cones. After 4 minutes the team with the most cones wins. Next step: dribble with the right hand going to a cone and the left hand bringing it back.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Tic-Tac-Toe Dribbling Game","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Tic-Tac-Toe Dribbling Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, X and O, line up on opposite sides of a tic-tac-toe board made with cones, hoops or tape in the middle. On \"Go\" the first player in each line dribbles to the board, stands in any square, passes the ball back to their line and makes an X or an O with their arms. The next players do the same. First team with three in a row wins; after a tie, play again.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|The Ball Carts (Die Ballwagen)","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"The Ball Carts (Die Ballwagen)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams and two ball carts, with balls of different sizes. Each player takes a ball and dribbles it into the other team's cart. The first team with an empty cart wins.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-4_d.pdf) Lesson 4","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-4_d.pdf"},{"key":"U8|smallGames|Over/Under Relay","age":"U8","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Over/Under Relay","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Even groups on the baseline. The first player dribbles three times with the right hand, then passes back over the head or under the legs. Each next player does the same, passing the opposite way from how they got the ball. The last player runs to the front and the group repeats with the left hand; done when the ball reaches the last player a second time. FIBA's version: roll the ball between the teammates' legs, and the last player dribbles it to the front; first team whose starting player is back at the front wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/); Mini p.25, item 5","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Dribble starts","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble starts","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Start the dribble from different positions: standing, sitting, kneeling, lying on the back or on the tummy.","source":"Mini p.16, Speed item 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|First dribble record","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"First dribble record","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player with a ball dribbles a set number of times, then catches the ball in triple threat. The coach counts successful repetitions, and players try to beat their own record. Controlled dribbling, no slapping. Next step: later in the level, the non-dominant hand, using the finger pads and keeping the ball at or below the waist.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.45–47","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Dribble Balance Push","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble Balance Push","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Even lines, a ball each. The first player stands on the right leg, knee slightly bent, dribbling with the right hand. On \"go\" they push forward, switch to the left hand and dribble to the foul line, where they stop on the left foot and dribble left-handed until the next \"go\". Then they push off again, switching hands, and so on to half court, the far foul line and the far baseline. The next group starts when the first reaches the foul line.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|compete|5-5-5 Creative Dribble","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"5-5-5 Creative Dribble","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player has a ball in open space. Dribble with the right hand for 5 seconds, the left hand for 5 seconds, then any way they like for 5 seconds; repeat for a minute. Next step: dribble around the court and, on the whistle, race to find a partner and do the 5-5-5 together. Competitive version: a relay in which partners on the sideline both dribble to the far sideline, do the 5-5-5 and dribble back; the first pair finished wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/12/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Relay past standing opponents","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Relay past standing opponents","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams race from one end of the court to the other. Opponents stand on the court, and each dribbler gets past them with dribble moves and finishes with a lay-up. The standing players must stay still but may try to knock the ball away. The next player starts once the dribbler crosses half way.","source":"Mini p.29, item 10","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|1.2.3. Triple Threat","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Triple threat, stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"1.2.3. Triple Threat","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone dribbles around the half court. When the coach says \"1, 2, 3!\", players answer \"Triple Threat!\" and drop into a triple-threat stance. On \"Go!\" they dribble on. Next step: dribble with the off hand or crossover while moving; in the stance, \"rip\" the ball left to right, low below the knees or high above the head.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Forward Pivot Basic; Reverse Pivot Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Triple threat, stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Forward Pivot Basic; Reverse Pivot Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player holds a ball in a good stance on the baseline. On the coach's \"Pivot!\", players pivot on the left foot in quarter steps; after 4 pivots they face forward again. Relax, then repeat with the right foot as the pivot foot. Forward Pivot Basic uses forward quarter steps and the Reverse Pivot Drill reverse quarter steps.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/), 7","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|compete|The Locomotive (Die Lokomotive)","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Triple threat, stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"The Locomotive (Die Lokomotive)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball. Odd numbers are locomotives and even numbers are wagons. On the signal the locomotives dribble freely and the wagons follow, dribbling. On a signal everyone jump stops; on the next, everyone starts again. Then swap roles.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-4_d.pdf) Lesson 4","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-4_d.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Under the arms, pivot, pass","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Triple threat, stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Under the arms, pivot, pass","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs 3 to 4 metres apart. One player stands still with legs apart and arms stretched out. On the coach's signal the partner dribbles under the arms, circles the player and dribbles back to the start, then stops, pivots (forward and reverse) and passes to the standing player, who then does the same.","source":"Mini p.28, item 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|Corner Passing","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Triple threat, stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Corner Passing","nameZh":"角落传球","howItRuns":"Teams line up at the four corners of a half court, one ball per corner. On the signal the first player dribbles to the half circle, stops, turns, passes to the next player in the line and runs back. Most passes in a set time wins. Next step: pass to the next corner's line and run to that line; pivot left or right, forward or back; pass with either hand. In Start Coaching (Dribble and Pass), players dribble to the free-throw line, jump stop, pivot forward or reverse as the coach calls, pass to the next line and join it.","source":"Mini p.28, item 6; p.29, item 7; Start Coaching, Plan 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|Red Light","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"footwork","group":"Triple threat, stops and pivots","kind":"activity","name":"Red Light","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players start on one baseline with a ball each; the coach faces them from the far baseline. \"Green light\": dribble and jog or run toward the coach. \"Yellow light\": walk while dribbling. \"Orange light\": walk backward while dribbling. \"Red light\": a quick jump stop into triple threat. Anyone the coach sees still moving after \"Red light\" goes back to the baseline. The first player to reach the coach wins and leads the next round.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/), 8","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Stationary Wall Passing – Chest","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing and catching","kind":"activity","name":"Stationary Wall Passing – Chest","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player stands a short distance from a wall with a target on it. Step toward the target with the lead foot, snap the thumbs down and extend the arms; let the ball bounce, catch it and repeat, 3 sets of 10 passes. Next step: dribble before passing, change the distance, or hit whichever target the coach or a partner calls. USA Basketball's Catch Own Pass Off Wall starts with a two-hand bounce pass, then a chest pass, catching with the hands in a \"W\" behind the ball.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/); [USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.59–60","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Make the Connection Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing and catching","kind":"activity","name":"Make the Connection Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 4 or 5. All but one have a ball and dribble in place. The connector, without a ball, moves to each player in turn, shows hands and calls that player's name; the player makes a bounce or chest pass to the connector, who passes it back. On \"switch\", a new connector. Next step: the connector shows hands without calling names, and players pass on eye contact; or players do 3 crossovers before passing.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/3/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Pepper Passing","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing and catching","kind":"activity","name":"Pepper Passing","nameZh":"热身传球","howItRuns":"Players stand in a circle with a passer in the middle. The ball goes from the outside to the middle player, who passes to the next player on the outside, and so on round the circle. Teams race to get round the circle quickest. Next step: two balls passed back and forth (2 Ball Pepper Passing · 双球热身传球).","source":"Mini p.26, items 6–7","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|Square Passing","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing and catching","kind":"activity","name":"Square Passing","nameZh":"方形传球","howItRuns":"Players stand in a square and pass the ball among themselves as quickly as possible. On the coach's signal they change direction, clockwise or counterclockwise. Next step: on the move, each passer follows the pass to join the line at the next corner.","source":"Mini p.26, item 11","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|Gates Passing Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing and catching","kind":"activity","name":"Gates Passing Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Gates of two cones 2 to 5 feet (about 0.6 to 1.5 m) apart are spread across a half or full court, with at least as many gates as partners. Partners share a ball and start on the baseline. They dribble to any gate and throw a bounce or chest pass through it. The pair with the most passes through gates in one minute wins. Next step: dribble with only the left or right hand before passing, or use other passes such as overhead. Competitive version: first pair to 25 passes, counting out loud.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Basketball Tennis","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing and catching","kind":"activity","name":"Basketball Tennis","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Rectangles about 10 by 20 feet (about 3 by 6 m), marked with cones and split in half. Two players share a ball, one on each side. They bounce pass across the middle line, trying to make a pass the opponent cannot catch after one bounce, so the opponent has to move. Scoring is like tennis: 15, 30, 40, game. Ball fakes and pivots are encouraged.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Who Is Ready Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Who Is Ready Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 3 in a triangle with one ball. The passer faces away while the other two decide which of them will make eye contact. The passer jumps and turns, finds the player looking with hands up, takes a quick step and passes. The receiver becomes the passer. Next step: a fourth player defends one receiver, and the passer finds the one not defended; then a defender on the passer as well, with pass fakes encouraged.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Partner Pass or Shoot Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Partner Pass or Shoot Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners about 10 feet (3 m) apart with one ball. The partner without the ball gives a cue: open hands means pass; arms down at the sides means shoot the ball to the partner as if at a basket. Next step: add a defender to each pair, who either crowds the ball handler (pass around the defence) or gives space (shoot to the partner).","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|compete|The Ball Thief (Der Balldieb)","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"The Ball Thief (Der Balldieb)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The attackers may not move and pass among themselves. One defender tries to intercept as many passes as possible in 30 seconds, then the next defender has a turn. When everyone has defended, the trainer announces the steals, and the team with more interceptions wins. Next step: no passing back; change the numbers of attackers and defenders to suit the level.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2011/02/Basketball_3_Passen_1_d.pdf) Lesson 3","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2011/02/Basketball_3_Passen_1_d.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|2 on 1 Shooting Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 1 Shooting Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"At each basket, two attackers stand at the elbows facing the basket and a defender stands under the basket with a ball. The defender passes to one elbow player and runs out to defend. The attackers stay where they are: the receiver shoots if open or passes to the other attacker, who shoots if open. After a shot or a steal, one attacker rotates to defence and two new attackers come in. Next step: the defender rolls the ball or makes low passes; move the attackers to the wing and baseline.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Open & Decide Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Open & Decide Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three players across the key: one on each block and one under the basket with the ball, with a defender behind the ball-handler. On \"go\" the ball-handler dribbles to the free-throw line, jump stops and pivots to face the basket. The defender guards one block player, and the ball-handler decides quickly whom to pass to for the shot. The passer becomes the shooter, the shooter the defender, and the defender goes to the back of the line. Keep a tally of makes over 3 minutes. Next step: a second defender follows the dribbler, and after the pass it is 3 on 2.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Keep Away Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Keep Away Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of five: four cones in a square and a fifth to one side as a rest station. Three attackers stand at cones, so one cone is empty, with a defender in the middle. The ball may be passed only left or right, never across. Players without the ball either receive the pass or fill the empty cone. The defender has 30 seconds to steal the ball; then everyone rotates, the defender going to the rest station. Competitive version: live 2 on 2 using all four cones, with two dribbles allowed; a steal or tipped pass changes possession, and the first team to 20 passes wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Recognizing Spots on the Floor","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Recognizing Spots on the Floor","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Five areas (baseline, wing, top of the key, low post, high post) each have a ball. Five players start on the baseline, run to an area and stand by the ball, and repeat until everyone has been to every spot. Then the coach calls spots, and players fill them, pick up the ball and get into triple threat facing the basket. Players stay on the spot, not between spots. Next step: one spot, building to all five in a row; sprint to the spot, pick up the ball and shoot; last, the coach calls a spot for the pass and the receiver shoots.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.78–79","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Proper Floor and Player Awareness","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Proper Floor and Player Awareness","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Start with 3 players inside the lane. On command they spread out along the half-court line, 12 feet (about 3.7 m) apart. The coach passes to one player, who passes to the properly spaced teammates. Next step: 4, then 5 players; on command players switch places with a teammate, the passer signalling with a raised hand or by calling \"change\"; air passes, then bounce passes; the coach calls a number of passes, then the player with the ball shoots.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.77–78","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Spacing","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Spacing","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Cones or discs are set about 12 to 15 feet (about 3.7 to 4.6 m) apart across the court. On command players sprint to a marker, then to a new marker on the next command. Variation: players in a line on the baseline each place a marker about 12 to 15 feet from the last one, then pass to the nearest teammate and then to one much farther away, to show that short passes are easier.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.80–81","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Pass & Cut Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Pass & Cut Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line at the top of the key with a few balls at the front, another line on the wing. The top player passes to the wing and cuts to the basket. The coach either defends the cutter or lets them cut freely. If the cutter is open, the wing passes to them; if the cutter is defended, the wing drives and shoots a lay-up.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Give & Go Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Give & Go Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players start on the 3-point line with the ball on the wing; the coach stands at the ball-side elbow. Pass to the coach, cut at once toward the basket and take the return pass for a lay-up. Use several baskets; with a second line, the coach moves elbow to elbow to feed both. Next step: the coach holds the ball at the right or left hip to show which side to cut; fake the other way first; then a defender on the cutter, and the passer fakes toward the defender before cutting off the coach.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Transition from Defense to Offense","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Transition from Defense to Offense","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Five lines at one end; four cones at the top of the key at each end mark five lanes (two outside, two inside, one middle). Without a ball, the front player of each line sprints to the far baseline, staying in their lane; players rotate until everyone has run every lane. Then add balls: the middle lane dribbles and the outside lanes pass; then the middle and inside lanes pass while the outside players dribble. Last, the middle player dribbles to the top of the key while the other four run their lanes, and chooses whom to pass to.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.81","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Drive & Kick Competition Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Team play: spots, spacing and cutting","kind":"activity","name":"Drive & Kick Competition Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines side by side at half court on one side, with one cone on the 3-point line above the elbow and one on the 3-point line at the wing. The first two players high-five. The ball-handler dribbles to the elbow cone and goes left or right round it, while the cutter runs to the wing cone and reacts. If the ball-handler goes left, the cutter cuts to the middle of the key for a pass and lay-up; if right, the cutter cuts to the short corner for a jump shot. Players rebound and switch lines. Competitive version: two groups race to 10 made shots.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/10/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Windows Defensive Slide Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Defence","kind":"activity","name":"Windows Defensive Slide Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners with one ball. Two cones about 6 to 8 feet (about 1.8 to 2.4 m) apart make a \"window\". One partner stands between the cones in a defensive stance; the other tries to roll the ball through the window, and the defender slides the feet sideways to stop it. Switch after 1 to 2 minutes and see who gets the most deflections. Next step: the attacker tries to dribble through the window, and the defender slides to cut them off.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/4/"},{"key":"U8|compete|One-on-One Tag Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Defence","kind":"activity","name":"One-on-One Tag Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One attacker without a ball and one defender, in the space from the top of the key to the baseline between the lane lines. The attacker tries to reach the baseline between the lane lines without being touched, and the defender stays between the attacker and the basket. Next step: the coach dribbles from the top of the key to the basket while the defender stays in front in a good stance; then 2 on 2 or 3 on 3, each attacker with a ball on a spot, one attacker at a time dribbling to the basket on the coach's command while their defender stays between ball and basket.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.75–77","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Tracing the Ball Breakdown Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Defence","kind":"activity","name":"Tracing the Ball Breakdown Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 3 with one ball. Two players face each other a little more than the lane width apart, one with the ball; the third starts in the middle. The third player closes out on the ball-handler, stays low, calls \"ball\" and traces the ball with the ball-side hand. The ball-handler moves the ball on one side of the body for 3 seconds, then passes, follows the pass, closes out and traces. Next step: the ball moves on both sides of the body, so the tracer switches hands; then add a pivot; then one dribble to gain a passing angle, after which the tracer uses both hands.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Pass and Shot (Pass und Wurf)","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Pass and Shot (Pass und Wurf)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two columns, A and B, and marker cones. Everyone has a ball except the first player in column A, who runs to a marker, takes a pass from the first player in B and shoots. The shooter rebounds and switches columns; the passer, now without a ball, runs to the other marker and the sequence repeats. Next step: vary the shooting positions; finish as a competition.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-5_d.pdf) Lesson 5","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-5_d.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Pass and Go (Passe et va)","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Pass and Go (Passe et va)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with a ball at a basket, and six chalked zones 3 steps apart. Each partner starts in one of the two zones farthest from the basket. Pass to the partner, then run to the next zone closer to the basket. Partners take turns shooting once each and repeat until the minute ends; most baskets in one minute wins. Next step: the pair plays against another pair.","source":"[FFBB Bouge au basket](https://eduscol.education.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/document/30-dactivite-physique-bouge-au-basketpdf-102369.pdf) sheet 6","sourceUrl":"https://eduscol.education.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/document/30-dactivite-physique-bouge-au-basketpdf-102369.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|1, 2, 3","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"1, 2, 3","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A basket and three chalked zones one step apart, numbered 1 (closest) to 3. Shoot from zone 1, then 2, then 3, and repeat until the minute ends. A basket from zone 1 scores 1 point, from zone 2 scores 2 and from zone 3 scores 3; add up the points. Next step: play against an opponent on three new zones, or move the zones farther back.","source":"[FFBB Bouge au basket](https://eduscol.education.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/document/30-dactivite-physique-bouge-au-basketpdf-102369.pdf) sheet 4","sourceUrl":"https://eduscol.education.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/document/30-dactivite-physique-bouge-au-basketpdf-102369.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Free Throw (Lancer franc)","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Free Throw (Lancer franc)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A start zone and a shooting zone 10 steps away. Shoot twice, dribble to the line, dribble back and repeat. One basket scores 1 point and two baskets score 3. Most points after one minute wins. Next step: 1 v 1, or with a partner against another pair, the partner passing after each shot.","source":"[FFBB Bouge au basket](https://eduscol.education.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/document/30-dactivite-physique-bouge-au-basketpdf-102369.pdf) sheet 5","sourceUrl":"https://eduscol.education.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/document/30-dactivite-physique-bouge-au-basketpdf-102369.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Shooting in Pairs (Wurf zu zweit)","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Shooting in Pairs (Wurf zu zweit)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs at the halfway line with one ball. Player 1 picks a side and shoots once, rebounds, dribbles back to the halfway line and passes to the partner, who shoots from the other side. One point per basket; first pair to 10 baskets wins.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2011/02/Basketball_2_Werfen_1_d.pdf) Lesson 2","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2011/02/Basketball_2_Werfen_1_d.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Shooting competition","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Shooting competition","nameZh":"投篮比赛","howItRuns":"Teams shoot freely from in front of the basket. Each basket is 1 point, and the team with the most baskets in a set time wins. Next step: change the distance, shoot from either side, with or without the backboard. FIBA also lists a free-throw competition, individual or in teams.","source":"Mini p.22, items 7–8","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|compete|Wrap Battle","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Wrap Battle","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams line up side by side at the free-throw line, at as many baskets as possible; the first player in each line has a ball. The first two tap balls together, wrap the ball round the waist three times, then shoot. The first to make a shot or a put-back scores a point for the team. They pass to the next two, and play goes on for 3 minutes; most points wins. Next step: wrap round the head, one leg or the ankles; or, as an easier version, dribble in for a lay-up after wrapping.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/), 9","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Make For a Cone Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Make For a Cone Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two to four teams of at most five, each at a spot such as the wing, elbow or corner, with a line of cones beside it, one per player. The first player in each line shoots and gets the rebound. A make lets them take a cone from any other team. Make or miss, they pass to the next player in line. The team with the most cones at the end wins. Next step: shoot from the 3-point line, or start with a drive and kick to the shooter.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Snatching the Basketball","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"layups","group":"Rebounding and lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Snatching the Basketball","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players jump and grab the ball with both hands at full overhead extension, then pull it down hard to the chin with elbows out. Knees bend for a high jump. Next step: a standing jump, then one step and jump.","source":"[USAB Curriculum](https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf) p.63","sourceUrl":"https://cdn1.sportngin.com/attachments/document/1e20-2167909/USA_Basketball_Youth_Coaching_Guide___Part_2.pdf"},{"key":"U8|compete|Toss & Get It Drill","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"layups","group":"Rebounding and lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Toss & Get It Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players line up in the paint, and the coach throws the ball off the backboard. The first player rebounds, chins the ball, tosses it back to the coach and goes to the end of the line. Next step: after the rebound, one shot to make a basket. FIBA's version: a player or the coach throws the ball against the backboard, and a teammate rebounds, lands and shoots.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/), 11; Mini p.22, item 18","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Lay-Up Bowling","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"layups","group":"Rebounding and lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Lay-Up Bowling","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams on opposite baselines, each with a ball and a spare ball near its sideline, and 3 cones 2 to 3 feet (about 0.6 to 0.9 m) apart near half court, toward each team's sideline. The first player dribbles to the far end for a lay-up, rebounds, dribbles back, jump stops and passes to the next. After a make they bowl the spare ball at the cones, retrieve it and return it; after a miss they go to the back of the line. First team to knock down all 3 cones wins. Next step: the coach at half court shows 1 (lay-up) or 2 (crossover, then lay-up on the other side).","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Rock, Paper, Scissors Game","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"layups","group":"Rebounding and lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Rock, Paper, Scissors Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three cones on the half-court line. Two teams with balls, each in 3 lines on its baseline facing the cones. The first player in each line dribbles to meet an opponent at a cone and, still dribbling with one hand, plays rock, paper, scissors with the other. The winner pivots and dribbles to their basket for a lay-up while the other player follows to apply pressure. A made lay-up scores 1 point; first team to 10 wins. Players then join a different line. In the key, players may jump stop and shoot.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/10/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Chase lay-ups","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"layups","group":"Rebounding and lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Chase lay-ups","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners with one ball. The ball handler starts at a spot such as the right wing; the partner starts about 2 strides behind at a cone. On \"Go\" the ball handler dribbles in for a lay-up and the partner runs to catch up and contest. Both go to the back of the line and switch roles. Change spots, and change the gap to suit ability. Next step: extra points for scoring in set ways, such as underhand, weak hand or off two feet; or add a second attacker and defender, so a ball handler who is cut off can pass.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/9/"},{"key":"U8|compete|Frequent Flyers","age":"U8","block":"compete","type":"layups","group":"Rebounding and lay-ups","kind":"activity","name":"Frequent Flyers","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"About 5 cones round the perimeter, each named after a city. Attackers outnumber defenders (3:1, 4:2, 5:2 and so on). Each attacker has a ball and starts at a cone; the defenders start in the lane. Attackers dribble in and try to score in the paint while the defence tries to stop them, with one shot per drive. A make from a cone \"stamps the passport\" there, and the player then tries the cones not yet scored from; after a miss they may start from any of those. The first to score from every cone wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/), 8, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|game|Points Game","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Points Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3 to 5, with six cones spread around the court. Passes may go only to a player at a cone or to a player in the key. A pass to a cone scores 1 point, a pass in the key 3 points, and a dribble into the key 1 point. If the defence gets the ball, it becomes the offence. Defenders must see both their opponent and the ball, and may not take the ball from a player's hands; they must intercept passes.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 2","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Into the Key","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Into the Key","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Equal teams. Players pass and dribble to try to get into the key. They stay 2 to 3 metres apart, lead into the key and then move back out to the perimeter, and after a pass cut to the basket and back out. The coach can play on offence to shorten passes, and cones can mark where players may stand on the perimeter.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 1","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Cone Knockdown Game","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Cone Knockdown Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A rectangle marked with cones, or half a court, with one cone at each end; each of two teams is given one of the cones. One ball. Teams start in the middle, one on offence, which passes the ball toward its cone and scores by knocking it down with a pass. A steal or deflection changes possession. Defenders may not guard the cones, and a stuck team may pass to the coach. Play to a set score or time. Easier version: allow 2 or 3 dribbles before a pass.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|game|5 Pass Knockout","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 Pass Knockout","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams of 3 to 6 in a space marked by court lines, one ball; use substitutes if teams are uneven. The offence tries to complete 5 passes, using pivots and cuts, with no dribbling. If the defence taps the ball, the ball goes out, or a travel is called, the defence picks it up and goes straight on offence. Easier version: passes off a pivot score 2 and others 1, or remove one defender. Competitive version: count how often a team reaches 5 passes; use the key or the 3-point area as the space; or require a score after the 5 passes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|game|Advantage Numbers Game","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Advantage Numbers Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two even teams on opposite sidelines, every player numbered. The coach stands at the free-throw line facing the offence, holding the ball in the hand farther from the basket. The coach calls up to 3 numbers; those attackers run out, take the ball from the coach's hand and try to score. Defenders with those numbers must first high-five the coach's empty hand. Play until a score, defensive rebound or steal, then switch. Play a set number of possessions or a set time. With an odd number of players, one player has two numbers. Next step: every attacker touches the ball before a shot.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/), 3, 4","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/2/"},{"key":"U8|game|2 on 2 Numbers Game; 3 On 3 Numbers Game","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"2 on 2 Numbers Game; 3 On 3 Numbers Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two players share each number, and everyone stands on the baseline. The coach throws the ball onto the court and calls two numbers, and those players race for the ball. The pair that gets it attacks and the other pair defends, for one possession and one shot. Mix the numbers evenly. First team to 5 points, counting by ones. In the 3 On 3 Numbers Game three players share each number; its target score is unclear in the source (3 in the heading, 5 in the text).","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/7/"},{"key":"U8|game|Scramble Game","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Scramble Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two even teams. Three attackers start outside the 3-point line at both wings and the top, and three defenders each match up about 6 feet (about 1.8 m) away. The coach throws to one attacker, and play is live on the catch. That attacker's defender must first run to touch the half-court line, then come back to defend, so the offence starts 3 on 2. Teams alternate: the attackers become defenders and three new attackers come in. First team to 5 baskets wins. Next step: at most 3 dribbles each time a player gets the ball.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/), 9","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/8/"},{"key":"U8|game|2 on 2 Surprise","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"2 on 2 Surprise","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 2 or more, at several baskets. One attacker with the ball starts at the top of the key on the 3-point line, guarded. A second defender stands under the basket facing the ball, with the second attacker behind them, who cuts to an open wing for a pass. Then it is live 2 on 2, and the defender under the basket closes out on the ball. Offence and defence switch after each possession; first team to 3 wins. Competitive version: other teams wait on the baseline and rotate in on defence after a score or a stop; first to 5.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/10/"},{"key":"U8|game|Coach Assisted 2 on 2 Small-Sided Game","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Coach Assisted 2 on 2 Small-Sided Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The half court is split in two. On each side two attackers and two defenders line up at the wing and corner, with a coach at the elbow. The offence passes to the coach and cuts to the basket against live defence. The coach passes back to the cutter or a teammate; if not, the cutter returns outside the 3-point line. Play runs until a score or stop, or until the coach calls \"Live\" for a 2 on 2. Then the defenders attack and two new defenders come in. Competitive version: 1 point for a live 2 on 2 basket, 2 for a score off a give-and-go pass.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/5/"},{"key":"U8|game|3v3 Champions Court","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3v3 Champions Court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3. One team starts on defence, and the next team waits at half court by the coach; waiting teams stand off the court passing a ball to each other. The offence high-fives the coach and attacks until it scores or the defence gets a steal or rebound. A scoring team keeps the ball, sprints back to high-five the coach and attacks again. A defence that wins the ball sprints to high-five the coach and becomes the offence. The losing team passes to the next team and goes off. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Rookie Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/rookie/page/12/"},{"key":"U8|game|Four 6-minute quarters","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Four 6-minute quarters","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The game length used at the 2026 China U8 national finals.","source":"[China Sports Daily via Sina](https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml)","sourceUrl":"https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml"},{"key":"U8|game|Outside shots count 2","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Outside shots count 2","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A shot from outside the 3-point line is worth 2 points, not 3. FIBA adds that it could count 1 point or nothing, to discourage these shots further.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Five passes, three points","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Five passes, three points","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A made shot after 5 passes is worth 3 points.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Free throws for non-scorers","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Free throws for non-scorers","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"At each quarter break, every player who has not scored takes one or two free throws, and makes count for the team.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Pass out after an offensive rebound","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Pass out after an offensive rebound","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"After an offensive rebound the ball must go to a teammate outside the key before the team can shoot.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Fouled shooter","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Fouled shooter","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A fouled shooter gets 1 point, the team gets the ball at half way, and there is no free throw.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Substitution options","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Substitution options","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two ways to get every player into each half (U6 tab): a compulsory substitution halfway through each quarter, or substitutions on the fly.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|10-point substitution","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"10-point substitution","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A player who scores 10 points comes off and may come back on later.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|No foul-out","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"No foul-out","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players are not removed at 5 fouls; the referee may substitute a player who is too rough.","source":"Mini p.33","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Play 2 periods, sit 2","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Play 2 periods, sit 2","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Germany's mini rules: every player plays at least 2 periods and sits out 2, with substitutions only at breaks.","source":"[DBB Miniregeln](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf"},{"key":"U8|game|No visible score","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"No visible score","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Canada Basketball does not recommend a visible score at this stage, and suggests creative scoring such as a point for every completed pass.","source":"[Canada Basketball ADM](https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf) p.32","sourceUrl":"https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf"},{"key":"U8|game|No throw-in","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"No throw-in","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Instead of a sideline throw-in, the player with the ball stands at mid-court and must pass without dribbling.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U8|game|Don't guard the throw-in","age":"U8","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U8","kind":"rule","name":"Don't guard the throw-in","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The defender stands beside the thrower, not in front. Once the ball is passed, both play normally.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|routine|Line Hops","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Line Hops","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four parts. 1: balance on one leg while dribbling and hop in as many directions as possible for 20 seconds, then switch legs. 2: the same on both feet. 3: in pairs, one partner calls out a letter and the other tries to spell it out by hopping on one leg. 4: in pairs, both stand on one leg while dribbling and try to push the partner off balance. The plan gives 2 x 20 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/"},{"key":"U10|routine|Tuck Back Drill","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Tuck Back Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball, or pretends to, and faces forward in triple threat. On \"tuck back!\", players say \"strong!\" while dropping the right foot and placing the ball on the right hip. On \"over!\", they step forward with the right foot while turning 90 degrees. On \"triple threat!\", they return to the triple-threat stance until the coach says relax. The plan gives 2 x 6 each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/), 7","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U10|routine|Retreat Dribble Drill","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Retreat Dribble Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players start on the baseline with a ball. They dribble with the right hand to the extended free-throw line, then take two retreat dribbles back, keeping the shoulder between an imaginary defender and the ball. They square up, dribble forward and repeat at half court, the far extended free-throw line and the far baseline. Repeat with the other hand. Next step: after the retreat, add a crossover or one between-the-legs dribble to attack the next spot. The plan gives 3 x 15 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/"},{"key":"U10|routine|Spin Dribble Drill","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Spin Dribble Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball and starts on the sideline. Take 2 dribbles with the right hand and spin to the left, then 2 dribbles with the left hand and spin back to the right. Continue down the court and back. The second dribble is a hard dribble, and the feet almost reach a jump stop at the same time as it, ready to spin. 3 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/"},{"key":"U10|routine|Change of Speed Game","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Change of Speed Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners, each with a ball, line up along the sideline with the leader in front. The leader runs, jogs or walks in a straight line to the other sideline, and the partner mirrors, staying within 2 feet (about 60 cm) of the leader. Roles switch on the way back. The plan gives 3 times down and back.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/"},{"key":"U10|routine|Face to Face (Vis-à-vis)","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"passing","group":"Passing","kind":"activity","name":"Face to Face (Vis-à-vis)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs stand 3 m apart with one ball and pass back and forth in the form the coach sets. Next step: chest, bounce and overhead passes; two balls, or two pass types combined; a triangle in threes; groups of 3 to 5 who follow their pass. Competitive version: count completed passes.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.9","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|routine|Star Passing","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"passing","group":"Passing","kind":"activity","name":"Star Passing","nameZh":"星星传球","howItRuns":"Groups of 5 stand in a star formation. On the coach's signal they pass in a set pattern, shown in the manual's diagram. The team that makes the most passes in a set time wins. Next step, Double Star Passing · 双星传球: the players are split into two teams, each with a ball, and both teams pass at the same time.","source":"Mini p.26, items 8 and 10","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|routine|2 Ball Square Passing","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"passing","group":"Passing","kind":"activity","name":"2 Ball Square Passing","nameZh":"双球方形传球","howItRuns":"Players stand in a square and pass two balls in opposite directions. On the coach's signal both balls change direction.","source":"Mini p.26, item 12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|routine|Press Lay-up (Drück-Korbleger)","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Press Lay-up (Drück-Korbleger)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players spread over all the baskets. For a right-hander: press the ball to the floor while stepping with the left foot, take two ground contacts (right, left), jump off the left leg, pull the swing-leg knee up and shoot one-handed off the backboard. Teach the shot before the lay-up, and bend the knees deeply for a clear start. Next step: more advanced players learn both sides, right with the right hand and left with the left.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.7","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|routine|Star Lay-up","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Star Lay-up","nameZh":"星星上篮","howItRuns":"1 dribbles and passes to 2, 2 dribbles and passes to 3, 3 dribbles and passes to 4, and 4 passes to 5, who shoots a lay-up. 6 rebounds and passes, and the activity continues. Players move to the line they passed to and call for the ball. Next step: players lead for the ball instead of dribbling. Neither text gives the start positions; FIBA's manual shows them in its p.26 diagram. The manual's version differs: 5 dribbles in for the lay-up, 2 rebounds and the shooter moves to 2's spot.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 4; Mini p.26, item 9","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|routine|Spin Step-In Drill","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Spin Step-In Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line on the baseline, each player with a ball; use several baskets. Three cones go around the paint: just above the block, in the middle of the lane and just above the opposite block. At the first cone, spin the ball out in front, step forward and land on the inside foot. Once square and balanced, repeat at the middle cone. At the third cone, plant the inside foot and shoot. The shooter rebounds and goes to the back of the line; the next player starts when the one ahead reaches the middle cone. Work in both directions.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U10|routine|See-Through Ball (Der durchsichtige Ball)","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"See-Through Ball (Der durchsichtige Ball)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone dribbles an imaginary ball through the hall. On signal 1, players move into the shooting start position; on signal 2, they shoot and hold the finish. A further signal restarts the sequence. BASPO's cues: shooting hand on the nose, guide hand at the ear, ball at chest height; after release, the shooting hand's fingers point to the floor and the guide hand's to the ceiling. Next step: players correct themselves or each other; then use a real ball.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.6; p.2","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|routine|Through the Middle to the Marker (Durch die Mitte zur Markierung)","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Through the Middle to the Marker (Durch die Mitte zur Markierung)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player has a ball. Dribble through the centre circle, jump-stop onto a free floor marker in the shooting stance, shoot, collect the ball and dribble back through the centre circle. The coach sets one focus at a time: basic position, guide-hand fingers up, shooting-hand fingers down, backspin, high arc. Competitive version: a ring hit scores 1 point and a basket 3.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.6","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|routine|Partner Step-In Shooting Drill","age":"U10","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Partner Step-In Shooting Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball. One player stands inside the 3-point line facing the basket in a good stance, ready to catch; the other stands under the basket with the ball and passes. The shooter takes a 1-2 step into the shot and shoots. The passer rebounds and passes again until the shooter has made 10 shots, then the players change places.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Team Tag","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Team Tag","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams on a full or half court. The taggers hold a bib, shirt, cone or other object and try to tag players on the other team. A tagged player takes the object and becomes a tagger. Play continues until the coach whistles or calls time, and the players without a bib at the end win. Next step: everyone dribbles. Or every player holds a partner's hand throughout.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/), 9","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|The Chase Game","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"The Chase Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of four, each player with a ball, on a full court. Player 1 starts on the baseline, Player 2 (the chaser) at the free-throw line, Player 3 at half court and Player 4 on the far baseline. On \"go\", 2 tries to tag 3 before 3 reaches 4's baseline. If 3 gets there, 4 becomes the chaser and chases 2 toward 1's baseline. A chaser who tags the player being chased swaps roles with them at once. Players count their tags, and the top four catchers form a new team for the next game. Easier: no balls, or a shorter court. Harder: off hand only.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Jail Break","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Jail Break","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams. Dribblers start inside the key with a ball each; defenders stand along the 3-point line. Dribblers try to dribble past the defenders and over the 3-point line. Defenders may not steal but slide their feet to force changes of direction. A dribbler who gets past becomes a defender. Play until one dribbler is left, then start a new game with the roles switched.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/), 6, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Pivot Tag (Sternschritt-Fangis)","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Pivot Tag (Sternschritt-Fangis)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone has a ball except 2 to 4 catchers. A player touched by a catcher gives up the ball and becomes a catcher. A chased player who holds the ball in both hands and pivots correctly shakes off the catcher, who must then chase someone else; only one catcher at a time can be shaken off this way. The double-dribble rule is suspended. Next step: set the dribbling hand, or change the field size or the number of catchers.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.5","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Fir-Tree Tag (Tannenbaum-Fangis)","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Fir-Tree Tag (Tannenbaum-Fangis)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone has a ball and dribbles constantly; 2 to 4 catchers are marked with a band. A touched player becomes a \"fir tree\", feet wide and ball overhead, and is freed when someone dribbles a ball through their legs. Next step: change the field size or the number of catchers, set the hand, or free trees by crawling under their legs instead.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.5","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Rabbit Hunt (Hasenjagd)","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Rabbit Hunt (Hasenjagd)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three roles: rabbits, who have a ball; hunters, who have none; and stationary players, \"X\". Hunters chase rabbits. A rabbit under pressure can dribble away or pass to an X, who passes back quickly. A hunter who touches a rabbit swaps roles with them.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf) Lesson 6","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Ball Depot (Das Balldepot)","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Ball Depot (Das Balldepot)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, each with a ball cart. Pairs cross the court passing one ball between them and put it in the other team's cart. The first team with an empty cart wins. Next step: each team has a ball thief who intercepts passes and dribbles stolen balls to the other team's cart.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-9_d.pdf) Lesson 9","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-9_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|smallGames|Hit Relay (Treff-Stafette)","age":"U10","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Hit Relay (Treff-Stafette)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 4 or 5 under one basket; all but two have a ball, and there is a pile of \"treasure\" (bottle caps, small cones or cards). On \"go\", ball carriers do a dribbling task, for example moving a tennis ball between small cones, then shoot. Balls are rolled back through the legs of waiting players to a player without a ball. A basket earns the shooter 2 treasure pieces and a miss 1. When the treasure is gone, the group with the most pieces wins. Next step: change the shooting distance.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.8","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|compete|Defense, I Love It Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1: dribble and defend","kind":"activity","name":"Defense, I Love It Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players spread out in front of the coach. On \"Defense!\", they slap the floor, get into a defensive stance and say \"I love it!\", staying in the stance; repeat the call and response 5 times. The coach then points left or right and players slide that way; if the coach fakes a shot, they put one hand up to contest. Next step: groups of 3 or 4, each with a leader who gives the cues; switch leaders after 30 to 45 seconds. The plan gives 2 x 5.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Read the Hand Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1: dribble and defend","kind":"activity","name":"Read the Hand Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners with one ball. The ball handler takes 1 or 2 slow dribbles toward the partner, who acts as a defender and either holds one hand down low or holds an arm out at about chest height. Hand down: the ball handler makes a high, shoulder-height crossover and dribbles past. Hand up: a low crossover and past. The player then turns and comes back. 10 in total, then switch partners.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/9/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Shield Steal Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1: dribble and defend","kind":"activity","name":"Shield Steal Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball. On a half court, cones mark a square for each pair, just big enough for the two players. On \"Go\", the attacker dribbles as long as possible while the defender tries to steal, and the attacker counts how long they keep the ball. On a steal or the whistle, players switch roles. 4 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Middle Corridor (Mittelkorridor)","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1: dribble and defend","kind":"activity","name":"Middle Corridor (Mittelkorridor)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs play 1 on 1 in a side corridor. The coach first explains what is and is not a foul. The attacker dribbles up the corridor from end to end, then both run back through the middle on the less crowded side; the next pair starts when the one ahead has crossed half way. The defender slides in a basic stance one arm's length away. If the ball is lost, it goes straight back to the attacker. Defence at 50%: keep arm's length. At 75%: if too close, sprint ahead without contact. At 100%: defend without fouling.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.10","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|compete|Rooted 1 on 1","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1: dribble and defend","kind":"activity","name":"Rooted 1 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three lines at half court, a ball at the front of each, and one defender per line at the 3-point line. The attacker dribbles toward the defender and has five dribbles to score. The defender may not move until the attacker is right in front of them. Players keep their own points. The attacker becomes the defender, and the defender joins the back of a half-court line. 2 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/), 8","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Pass Fake Breakdown Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"Pass Fake Breakdown Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player has a ball, spins it to themselves from the side, gives a quick reverse pass fake at chest level, and repeats. Change sides, and add pivots if appropriate. The fake is quick and moves both the ball and the eyes. 2 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Pass Under Pressure Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"Pass Under Pressure Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three, one ball, in a small area marked with cones or lines. One attacker dribbles continuously inside the area for 30 seconds while a defender tries to steal. The other attacker moves around outside the area; when they raise their hands, the dribbler must pass to them within two seconds, and they pass back. The defence scores a point for each steal or deflection, and the passer and receiver a point for each completed pass. Switch roles every 30 seconds. The plan gives 6 x 30 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/"},{"key":"U10|compete|The Gates (Die Tore)","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"The Gates (Die Tore)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three gates, each made of two cones, and 3 balls. Three attackers try to get through one of the gates using passes only, while 2 defenders try to stop them. A point for each time the group gets through; most points in a set time wins.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf) Lesson 6","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|compete|Pirate Game (Piratenspiel)","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"Pirate Game (Piratenspiel)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three pirates (defenders) each guard their own \"sea\". Seafarers in pairs with one ball must cross each sea, and may dribble. A pair that gets across runs round the outside back to the start, the \"harbour\". If the ball is intercepted or leaves the sea, the player responsible replaces the pirate. Next step: limit the number of dribbles, or change the field size or the number of pirates.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.9","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|compete|Pairs passing up the court","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Pass and cut","kind":"activity","name":"Pairs passing up the court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players in two lines, or in pairs, pass the ball back and forth while running to the other end of the court. They may pass the full length of the court, or at the far end the player on the outside shoots. That player rebounds their own shot and the players move to the other side of the court. Players swap lines on the way back.","source":"Mini p.27, item 13","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|compete|Weaves","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Pass and cut","kind":"activity","name":"Weaves","nameZh":"波浪传球","howItRuns":"Three groups on the baseline with one ball. The middle player passes to an outside player, then sprints behind that player and moves in front of them. Each player who catches passes to a player on the opposite side and runs behind that player, making a weave. Finish with a shot, or by stepping off the court at the far end. Next step: 4 groups, where the first pass always goes to the closest player on the side with two players; or 5 groups, where the first pass can go to the closest player on either side and the passer then sprints past both players on that side.","source":"Mini p.27, items 14–15","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|compete|Dribble & Pass to Target Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Pass and cut","kind":"activity","name":"Dribble & Pass to Target Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Half the players start on the right baseline; the other half stand well behind a cone on the left wing beyond half court. Everyone has a ball. A baseline player takes two running or jogging dribbles and throws a running pass at the cone. On a hit, the cone player passes back and the passer banana-cuts toward the basket they started near for a lay-up, chased by the cone player; they then switch lines. On a miss, the passer defends and the cone player attacks them 1 on 1 at the basket nearest the passing line. Points for a cone hit, a made lay-up and a stop; play to a set score, then change sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/), 8, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Pass and Cut, 2 on 2","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Pass and cut","kind":"activity","name":"Pass and Cut, 2 on 2","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Attackers 1 and 2 play against defenders, with the coach as a passer. The start positions are shown only in the plan's diagram. 1 passes to the coach or to 2, then cuts to the basket. The attackers score by getting the ball into the key. The defence wins by getting the ball, or if the attackers have not got into the key within 10 seconds. When 1 passes to the coach, 2 waits until 1 has cut before cutting. Defenders stay between their opponent and the player with the ball, able to see both.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 7","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|compete|Pass Fake & Drive Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"Pass Fake & Drive Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line at the top of the key with a ball, one attacker on the wing, one defender in the key and a coach on the opposite wing. The first player in line passes to the coach, who passes back; on that pass the defender closes out and plays live. The attacker either pass fakes and drives to the basket, or passes to the wing player, with the defender at a disadvantage. Rotate: top to wing, wing to defence, and a new player in at the top. The plan gives 1 x 2 minutes each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U10|compete|The Kick Pass Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"The Kick Pass Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines, one on the wing with the ball and one in the weakside corner, each attacker with a defender. The defender on the ball starts one step behind. Play is live once the attacker moves. The ball handler drives and reads the weakside defender: if that defender leaves the corner player to help, kick out for an open shot; if the defender stays home, try a lay-up. Play until a score or the defence gets the ball; the attackers move to defence and two new attackers start. Next step: move the corner player to just outside the block, or add an attacker and a defender on the weakside wing for 3 on 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U10|compete|2 on 2 Drive and Kick","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 2 Drive and Kick","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners line up on the baseline for 2 on 2. One attacker starts at the top of the key with the ball and one on the wing. The defender on the ball gives the dribbler a slight advantage to drive toward the teammate's side. The dribbler drives and kicks the ball out, and the 2 on 2 is live for one shot. The defence then becomes the offence, the offence goes to the back of the baseline line, and a new pair defends. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/9/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Shot Fake Breakdown Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shot fakes, jabs and shooting decisions","kind":"activity","name":"Shot Fake Breakdown Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Players start on the baseline, each with a ball. Each player spins the ball to themselves, catches, shot fakes and takes one dribble forward with the right hand, repeating to half court; on the way back they dribble forward with the left hand. Stay low, show the ball as for a normal shot, and do not rush the fake. The coach gives feedback along the way. 2 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Jab Attack Drill","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shot fakes, jabs and shooting decisions","kind":"activity","name":"Jab Attack Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A player with a ball starts facing the basket where half court meets the sideline. Jab with the foot nearest the sideline, then step across the body with the jab foot, taking a big step and a big dribble forward at an angle the other way, and pick up the ball with a 1-2 stop as if to shoot. Reset, jab again and attack the middle. The goal is to be inside the 3-point line after 3 jabs and take a jump shot after the third. Always 1-2 step; no hopping onto two feet after the dribble. 3 times from each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Variation Shooting","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shot fakes, jabs and shooting decisions","kind":"activity","name":"Variation Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners, one shooter and one passer, with up to 4 pairs per basket. Round 1: the shooter takes 6 shots, all from different spots and ranges, and the passer passes from a different angle each time. Round 2: the same with a different stance: narrow, wide, off one leg, staggered. Round 3: after each pass the passer closes out, and the shooter shot fakes and takes one or two dribbles before the shot, changing spot on every catch. Switch roles after each round. The plan gives 3 to 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/), 9","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Triangle Decision Shooting","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shot fakes, jabs and shooting decisions","kind":"activity","name":"Triangle Decision Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Cones form three triangles, one at the free-throw line and one on each wing, tips pointing to the basket, with groups of 2, 3 or 4 at each. A defender starts at the tip; an attacker with the ball starts on the opposite side, facing the basket. The defender closes out to one side, and the attacker reads it and takes one or two dribbles toward the basket the other way for a jump shot. The shooter becomes the defender, and the defender joins a line at another triangle. After a set time, players count their makes; the highest total wins. The plan gives 4 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Decision 1 on 1","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shot fakes, jabs and shooting decisions","kind":"activity","name":"Decision 1 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with a ball at spots around the 3-point line; the coach stands in the lane and gives each pair a number. The defender stands just inside the 3-point line in a stance, facing the basket; the attacker stands behind, touching the ball to the defender's back. When the coach calls a pair's number, the attacker pulls the ball away and attacks the basket, and the defender must touch the floor with both hands before defending. If the coach stays out of the play, the attacker takes a lay-up; if the coach steps forward, the attacker pulls up for a shot. Possessions alternate, make or miss. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Blind 1 on 1","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shot fakes, jabs and shooting decisions","kind":"activity","name":"Blind 1 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners at any spot on the 3-point line, up to three pairs per basket. The attacker stands behind the defender, holding the ball on the defender's back. As soon as the attacker moves to dribble, the defender may play live. The attacker has two dribbles to score with a lay-up or jump shot. Next step: lay-ups only, then lay-ups with the non-dominant hand only. The plan gives 4 games.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/), 7","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/"},{"key":"U10|compete|3-Line Layups Add Variations","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"2 on 1 and 3 on 2","kind":"activity","name":"3-Line Layups Add Variations","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three lines facing the basket, one on each wing and one at the top of the key. The first in each line dribbles in for a lay-up, rebounds their own shot, passes to the next in that line and goes to the back. The wings shoot right- and left-hand lay-ups, the middle a lay-up without the backboard, and players also mix in finishes such as the Euro step. Rotate lines after 90 seconds. Next step: the middle line defends and one wing line attacks without a ball, making a live 2 on 1 with 10 seconds per possession and lay-ups only.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/2/"},{"key":"U10|compete|2-on-1 Fast Break Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"2 on 1 and 3 on 2","kind":"activity","name":"2-on-1 Fast Break Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines at half court, one with a ball, and a defender at the top of the key. The two attackers try to score in a 2 on 1 fast break. The player who shoots or turns the ball over becomes the next defender, and the others go to the back of the half-court lines. 3 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/), 10, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Full Court Transition Outnumbered Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"2 on 1 and 3 on 2","kind":"activity","name":"Full Court Transition Outnumbered Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two attackers inbound from the baseline and try to score at the far basket. One defender starts at the 3-point line on the inbound side and one at the far 3-point line, and each guards only their own half, so the attackers have a 2 on 1 in each half. A steal or the ball going out of bounds ends the game, and a new group rotates in: the attackers move to defence and two new attackers come on. Next step: add one attacker and one defender for 3 on 2 in each half. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U10|compete|3 On 2 Advantage","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"2 on 1 and 3 on 2","kind":"activity","name":"3 On 2 Advantage","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three attackers start anywhere outside the perimeter and two defenders in the key; one defender has the ball. Play starts when that defender passes to an attacker. Attackers may not cut and must pass the ball around to get a good shot. After a score or a stop, the two defenders join one more player on offence and two new defenders come in. Next step: allow one attacker to cut, who must go back outside the 3-point line if they don't get the ball; or attackers may hold the ball for only one second before passing, shooting or dribbling, or the coach calls a turnover. 6 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Shoot And Follow","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Shoot And Follow","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 3 with 2 balls at a basket; the coach sets the distance and type of shot. On \"start\", one player shoots and the second shoots right after. Shooters chase their own shot, rebound and pass to the open teammate, and the passer moves to a new spot for the next pass. After two minutes, groups change baskets, type of shot or side of the basket. Competitive version: teams count makes, and the most makes after a set time wins. The plan gives 3 x 2 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Hit Games (Treff-Spiele)","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Hit Games (Treff-Spiele)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups spread across the baskets, one ball per group, and repeat the shooting technique. The first group to 10 baskets wins. A gross technique error, such as a two-handed shot, costs the group all its points. Next step: change the distance; score 1 for the ring and 3 for a basket; change the number of baskets to win or the number of balls; move groups to the next basket after each round; shoot lay-ups.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.7","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|compete|21","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"21","nameZh":"21分游戏","howItRuns":"Two teams, as even as possible, each with two balls, lined up at a set spot. A basket from the spot scores 2 points. A player who catches the rebound before it bounces may take a second shot, worth 1 point. The next player starts once the one ahead has scored or used both shots. The first team to at least 21 points wins.","source":"Mini p.22, item 10","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|compete|Pressure Shot","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Pressure Shot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams race to make 10 shots that the coach chooses, within the players' range. A team on 3 that misses its next shot goes back to zero; a team on 5 that misses its next shot loses 1 point.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 8","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|compete|Free Throw Golf","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Free Throw Golf","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Use as many baskets as possible, with one player shooting at a time. Each player shoots 2 free throws at a time and rotates until they have shot 10. Nothing but net scores minus 2, any other make minus 1, and a miss plus 1. The lowest score after 10 free throws wins. Competitive version: partner groups compete, and partners may distract the shooter but not block or touch them.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Jump Stop Shooting Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Jump Stop Shooting Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Up to 4 groups at shooting spots on the wings and slots, the first player in each line with a ball, and a cone a few feet from the basket. The first player dribbles to the cone, jump stops and shoots, rebounds their own shot and passes to the next in line. The first team to the target number of baskets wins. Work on a good, balanced jump stop at game speed.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/12/"},{"key":"U10|compete|2-Bounce Shooting Game","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"2-Bounce Shooting Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four players at a basket, each with a ball, start outside the 3-point line. Each takes one dribble toward the basket and shoots, then must get the rebound before the ball bounces twice; if it bounces twice, the basket does not count. After the rebound the player goes back out to the perimeter and repeats. The first to make 5 shots wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/10/"},{"key":"U10|compete|Cone Game (Hütchen-Spiel)","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Cone Game (Hütchen-Spiel)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"At least 2 players per basket, a fixed shooting spot at each basket, and about half as many cones as groups in the middle. After making 5 press lay-ups, a group may take a cone, from the middle or from another group, and put it beside its marker; from then on it scores with shots from the cone, not lay-ups. Cones may not be fought over. A group that loses its cone must make 5 press lay-ups again before taking one. The first group to the agreed points total wins. BASPO's example: 22 players, 6 baskets, groups of 3 or 4, 2 balls per group, 3 cones, 10 points to win.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.7","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|compete|Shooting Ladders","age":"U10","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting contests","kind":"activity","name":"Shooting Ladders","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners share a ball, and several pairs can use one basket. Each basket has three spots: short (near the block), medium (near the free-throw line) and long (near the 3-point line). Partners take turns shooting at each spot, rebound their own shot and pass to the partner, and count how many times they complete the ladder. Move the spots closer or farther to suit the players. Next step: every shot off the dribble (pull-up, side step or step-back), and only a swish moves the shooter back to the next spot. Competitive version: waiting players or coaches contest the shots.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U10|game|Line Ball (Linienball)","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Line Ball (Linienball)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, one ball. A team scores by getting the ball behind the opponents' baseline using passes only. BASPO's school version plays across the hall in 2 to 4 fields with teams of 2 or 3: passing and pivoting only, no dribbling, and a point for placing the ball on the floor behind the line without travelling. Next step (school version): change the field size or team size, play a league or rotation tournament, or allow a set number of dribbles.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf) Lesson 6; [BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.9","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|game|Criss Cross Game","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Criss Cross Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3 on a half court, one ball. The defending team starts on the baseline; the attackers start outside the 3-point line on both wings and at the top of the key. The defenders pass along the baseline from far left to far right, and the far-right defender passes to the nearest attacking wing. The defenders then sprint out to guard the player diagonally opposite, the middle one taking the top, while the attackers reverse the ball from right to left. Play goes live once the ball reaches the far side. A team that scores stays on attack; a defence that gets a stop attacks next. Either way a new defence comes in. First to 3 points wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U10|game|Scrimmage Activity","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Scrimmage Activity","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, two halves. In the first half one team attacks at one end and the other defends. When the defence gets the ball, it goes to the other end and shoots, with no defence, until it scores; then the ball comes back for another contested possession. At half time the defending team is probably ahead, since it scored every time it had the ball. The teams swap in the second half. The coach can change how a contested possession scores, for example getting the ball into the key or a shot hitting the ring.","source":"Start Coaching, Plan 9","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|game|5 v 5 closing game (Spiel 5 gegen 5)","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 v 5 closing game (Spiel 5 gegen 5)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every J+S-Kids lesson ends with 15 minutes of 5 v 5. Where side baskets are available, play 3 v 3 across the width of the court instead.","source":"[J+S-Kids Basketball](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf) Lesson 6 (all lessons)","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2012/06/Basketball_Lektion-6_d.pdf"},{"key":"U10|game|Four 6-minute quarters","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Four 6-minute quarters","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The game length used at the 2026 China U10 national finals.","source":"[China Sports Daily via Sina](https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml)","sourceUrl":"https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml"},{"key":"U10|game|Equal time for three periods","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Equal time for three periods","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Equal playing time in periods 1 to 3; the coach decides in the 4th period and extra periods.","source":"[USA Basketball rules, ages 9–11](https://www.quickscores.com/downloads/mwrhuachucayouth_USA_Basketball_Youth_Guidelines__Rules_and_Standards.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://www.quickscores.com/downloads/mwrhuachucayouth_USA_Basketball_Youth_Guidelines__Rules_and_Standards.pdf"},{"key":"U10|game|Two different fives","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Two different fives","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Five players play the 1st quarter and a different five play the 2nd.","source":"[Basketball Ireland rules, U11](https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/NationalUnderageRules.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/NationalUnderageRules.pdf"},{"key":"U10|game|Full-court defence in the last period only","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Full-court defence in the last period only","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Player-to-player defence may extend full court only in the 4th period and extra periods. A team leading by 25 or more may not defend beyond half court.","source":"USA Basketball rules, ages 9–11","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|game|All field goals count 2","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"All field goals count 2","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"No 3-point shot; a basket from outside the line counts 2.","source":"USA Basketball rules, ages 9–11; Basketball Ireland U11–U12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|game|Travel called lightly","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Travel called lightly","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Travel and illegal dribble are applied sparingly. The referee gives feedback on footwork, may stop play to explain, then gives the ball back to the same team.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|game|Fast break and possession","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Fast break and possession","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A team that scores on a fast break keeps the points and gets the ball back at half way.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|game|No 8 second","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"No 8 second","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The 8-second violation is not called; the team keeps the ball and throws in from half way.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U10|game|Free-throw line 1 m closer","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Free-throw line 1 m closer","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Germany's U10 rule; players may not step over the line.","source":"[DBB Miniregeln](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf"},{"key":"U10|game|Keep score late in the stage","age":"U10","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U10","kind":"rule","name":"Keep score late in the stage","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Canada does not recommend a visible score early in Learn to Train and suggests keeping score towards the end of the stage.","source":"[Canada Basketball ADM](https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf) p.37","sourceUrl":"https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf"},{"key":"U12|routine|SHRed Injuries Basketball warm-up","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Injury-prevention warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"SHRed Injuries Basketball warm-up","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"About 10 minutes, before the basketball warm-up, at all practices and games. Four parts; each exercise has a Level 1 and a Level 2. Aerobic: forward run with backward zig-zag side shuffle (L2 carioca); skipping forward and backward (L2 sideways); forward run building speed. Agility: single-leg hops forward and backward (L2 side to side); squat jumps (L2 skate jumps). Strength: front plank (L2 with alternating leg lifts); side plank (L2 with torso rotation); Nordic hamstring curl; walking lunges with torso rotation (L2 with arm and knee lifts); 3-D lunges (L2 side lunges with arm circles). Balance: single-leg balance with torso rotation (L2 with ball roll); with squat pass (L2 and jump catch). Reps and times are on the printed handout. In Calgary players aged 11–18, ankle and knee injury rates were 36% lower in the season it was used (a before-and-after comparison, not a randomised trial).","source":"[University of Calgary SHRed page](https://live-ucalgary.ucalgary.ca/shred-injuries/all-sports/basketball); [Emery 2022](https://doi.org/10.2519/jospt.2022.10959)","sourceUrl":"https://live-ucalgary.ucalgary.ca/shred-injuries/all-sports/basketball"},{"key":"U12|routine|Dynamic Warm-Up Part 4","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Dynamic Warm-Up Part 4","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each movement once, travelling from the baseline to half court or from half court to the baseline: Leg Swings, Knee Hugs, Imaginary Dunks, Backwards Run, Trunk Twists, Explosion Leap. The page gives no further instructions; the movements are shown on video.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/), 7, 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Dynamic Warm-Up Part 5","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Dynamic Warm-Up Part 5","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each movement once: The Bear Hold, Ankling, Carioca, Pogo Jumps, Leg Swings, Calf Raises. The page gives no distance or further instructions; the movements are shown on video.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Defensive stance and slides","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"defence","group":"Defensive footwork","kind":"activity","name":"Defensive stance and slides","nameZh":"防守站姿与滑步","howItRuns":"Start from the defensive stance and slides: lateral slides, zig-zag backward slides, and slides linked into a sprint and a change of direction. The report gives no set-up, times or numbers.","source":"[CBA U12 camp plan (media report)](https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20260829A059G300)","sourceUrl":"https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20260829A059G300"},{"key":"U12|routine|Gauntlet Dribbling","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Gauntlet Dribbling","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Cones split a half court into 3 lanes, with 2 to 3 defenders in each lane who may only move side to side. One attacker per lane starts at half court, dribbles through the defenders and finishes with a lay-up or jump shot. The defenders try to steal the ball or knock it out of bounds. Then the defender nearest half court joins the attacking line, every defender moves up one spot, and the finisher takes the spot nearest the hoop. When everyone has gone, the first player starts again in the opposite direction. Next step: limit the dribbles, or defenders dribble their own ball.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/), 9","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Partner Decision Dribbling Game","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Partner Decision Dribbling Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball. The dribbler faces the hoop; the partner stands a few feet away with their back to the hoop and gives hand signals while the dribbler moves. \"Hands Up\" means pass, \"Hip Flip\" means drive to the basket, \"Step Backward\" means shoot. Next step: limit the dribbler to set moves, let the partner move side to side for new passing angles, or require a set shot (jump shot, lay-up or floater).","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Dribbling in the Counter-attack (Das Dribbling im Gegenangriff)","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Dribbling in the Counter-attack (Das Dribbling im Gegenangriff)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Full court. Two players start together, dribble the length of the court at the same time and finish (lay-up, one step or Eurostep). Then the next pair goes. The maximum number of dribbles depends on level; the source gives no number. Next step: limit the dribbles, then alternate right and left hand on each dribble.","source":"[BASPO Training Forms](https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691761148/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-den-angriff-situationsangepasst-und-zielstrebig-aufbauen.pdf) p.14","sourceUrl":"https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691761148/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-den-angriff-situationsangepasst-und-zielstrebig-aufbauen.pdf"},{"key":"U12|routine|Between Cross Behind","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Between Cross Behind","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each. On the coach's command, dribble the sequence between the legs, crossover, behind the back. A mistake sends the player back to start with \"between\". One full sequence is one rep; get as many reps as possible. 2 x 20 seconds. Saying the sequence aloud helps keep the order.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Open Step Back Breakdown Drill","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Open Step Back Breakdown Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs facing each other, one ball. Dribble with the right hand, wait until the bounce is nearly done, then push off the left foot to the side and back while collecting the ball. Pass to the partner, who does the same. Then the other direction. 3 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Chaos Layups","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Chaos Layups","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"As many players with a ball as possible, split evenly across the baskets. Players start outside the three-point line and dribble the whole time. They may drive, but may shoot a lay-up only inside a set area (inside the lane lines or the restricted area), and after each attempt they dribble back outside the three-point line. The coach adds \"chaos\": weak-hand finishes only, or a time limit to see who makes the most. Next step: limit the lay-up area to one side of the basket, or add up scores in teams of three or four.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|routine|1 on 1 Finishing Game","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 Finishing Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two players, each with a ball, on either side of the hoop, dribbling with a set hand. On the whistle both try to finish first, at the front of the rim or on the opposite side. Phase 1: only 1 dribble. Phase 2: finish on the opposite side of the rim. Phase 3: no backboard. Rotate quickly, bringing in two new players after a few rounds.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/10/"},{"key":"U12|routine|3-Person Shooting","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"3-Person Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three at one basket: shooter, passer and rebounder. The shooter takes 10 shots in a row. Then the shooter becomes the rebounder, the rebounder the passer and the passer the shooter. Continue until each player has taken the set number of shots; the plan prints 2 x 10 shots each.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/"},{"key":"U12|routine|2 Balls, 3 Shooters V-Cut Shooting","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"2 Balls, 3 Shooters V-Cut Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three with two balls. Two players spread out and one starts near the rim with a ball. Each player makes a V-cut before receiving a pass from the teammate who has just rebounded their own shot. Shooters rebound their own shot and always pass to the same teammate: 1 to 2, 2 to 3, 3 to 1. Shoot from a different spot each time. Play one minute at a time and count makes. Next step: one dribble into a pull-up jump shot after the catch, or the passer closes out and contests.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|routine|Progression Shooting Competition","age":"U12","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Progression Shooting Competition","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams spread across the baskets, one ball each. The first player takes the set shot, rebounds, passes to the next player and goes to the back of the line. Every make counts toward the team total at that spot. The progression is 8 lay-ups, 8 floaters, 8 mid-range catch-and-shoot, 8 one-dribble pull-ups starting at the 3-point line, and 8 catch-and-shoot 3s. The first team to make all shots from all spots wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|1 on 1 Dribble Duel","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"1 on 1 Dribble Duel","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs face each other at the elbows or the top of the key, each with a ball. Both complete a dribbling task, for example 5 between-the-legs dribbles, or 2 crossovers and 2 behind-the-back. On the coach's \"Go\", or when the sequence is done, both race to finish at the rim. First to score wins. Next step: finish on the opposite side of the rim from the start, or go around a cone before finishing.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|Line Battles","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Line Battles","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs or threes, one ball, playing inside the free-throw lane. The attacker starts on the baseline, guarded by a partner, and tries to reach the free-throw line as many times as possible in a set time or number of tries, staying inside the lane lines. Being forced out or losing the ball means restarting from the baseline. Reaching the free-throw line scores 1 point. Next step: weak hand only, or the defender starts a step ahead, shoulder to shoulder or trailing. Competitive version: a point counts only if the attacker goes into a scoring move after reaching the line.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/), 7, 10, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|1 on 1 – Cones","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"1 on 1 – Cones","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines at the top of the key, one nearer the wing, each with a cone on the baseline in front of it. The coach stands at the top with a ball. The first player in each line sprints to touch their cone, then sprints to the wing for 1 on 1. The player from the line nearer the wing attacks and the other defends. Maximum 3 dribbles per possession. Then the next two go. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|1 on 1 – Dribble Out","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"1 on 1 – Dribble Out","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines on the baseline near the sideline, one slightly nearer the top of the key, each with a cone in front of it between the three-point line and half court. The line nearer the sideline has the balls. On the coach's command the first two players go around their cones toward the middle, the ball handler dribbling the whole way, and then play 1 on 1. Then the next two go. Use the other side or other baskets for more turns. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|On the Block 1 on 1 Game","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"On the Block 1 on 1 Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines on the baseline, with a player standing on each block. The coach has the ball at the free-throw line and passes to one of them. That player has at most 1 dribble to take a shot and may not move backwards. The player who did not get the pass defends. Play until the defence gets the ball or the attacker scores. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/), 10; All-Star Plan 4","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|Elbows 1 on 1 Game","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Elbows 1 on 1 Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines on the baseline, with a player standing on each elbow. The coach has the ball at the top of the key and passes to one of them. That player has at most 2 dribbles to take a shot in the paint. The player who did not get the pass defends. Play until the defence gets the ball or the attacker scores. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/7/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|Full Court 2 on 2","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Full Court 2 on 2","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The full court is divided into two halves, each with a line on the baseline and a line at or near half court. A baseline attacker with the ball, guarded by one defender, tries to reach half court while staying within the lane. There a second attacker and defender join, and the 1 on 1 becomes 2 on 2. The second attacker starts with a different advantage each time: the defender on the hip, or a few steps behind. Next step: the attacker who starts with the ball must pass and try to score off a pass and cut. The plan gives no win condition.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|2 on 2 Around the Cone","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"2 on 2 Around the Cone","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A cone on the weak-side wing. One attacker starts in front of a defender under the rim; the second attacker is in the corner with their defender in help position. The ball handler dribbles around the cone with the defender trailing, which gives a live 2 on 2 with a slight advantage to the offence. After a few turns, move the second attacker from the corner to the wing. Next step: the second attacker may start anywhere inside the three-point line, a second cone goes in the opposite corner, and the ball handler chooses which cone to go around. The plan gives no win condition.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U12|smallGames|2 on 2 Slide Up (Nachrutschen)","age":"U12","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"2 on 2 Slide Up (Nachrutschen)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two 2 on 2 games side by side across the hall, with the other players on benches along the halfway line. The first team to score stays on. Two newcomers come on and new teams form, each winner with a newcomer. The losers go to the end of the benches and everyone moves up. A basket counts; if nobody scores, a hit on the rim or board may count. Next step: two or three hits to win, or 4 on 4.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.13","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U12|compete|Pull Back 1 on 1 Game","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1 with the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Pull Back 1 on 1 Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs, with three pairs spaced around the arc. The attacker pushes off the left foot and takes a long step forward on the right, swinging the ball low by the right foot to create space while the defender crowds without stealing. The attacker then pushes back off the right foot and pulls the ball back with one bounce, left foot as the pivot foot. Then the attacker shoots if there is space or attacks the basket, and it is live 1 on 1. Switch roles regularly and alternate pivot feet.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Pivot 1 on 1","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1 with the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Pivot 1 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs inside the three-point line, one ball. The defender starts a few steps away and passes to the attacker to start. The attacker may not dribble and must pivot to create space for a shot while the defender pressures; the attacker tries to finish with a lay-up or shot. Switch after a shot or a steal. After a few rounds, allow 1 dribble before pivoting. Next step: change the starting spot, set a time limit to score, or name the pivot (front, reverse, step-through).","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Three-quarter 1 on 1 (Dreiviertel 1 gegen 1)","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1 with the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Three-quarter 1 on 1 (Dreiviertel 1 gegen 1)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The court is halved lengthwise. Pairs with one ball start at the baseline, with space between groups. At about free-throw-line height the ball carrier hands or passes the ball to the partner and becomes the defender; the partner attacks the remaining three quarters. Three intensity levels: 50%, defend only up to the three-point line, then \"check\" and play 1 on 1; 75%, defend from the start and give the ball back after a steal; 100%, a steal ends the action. Next step: hand over only at half way, limit the dribbles, change direction, or play across to side baskets.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.11","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U12|compete|1 on 1 Half-court","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"oneOnOne","group":"1 on 1 with the ball","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 Half-court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs at or near half court, up to 4 pairs per half court. Against live defence, the attacker must dribble from half court to the baseline within 8 seconds, up and back 3 times. Round 1: the defender starts in front. Round 2: shoulder to shoulder. Round 3: slightly behind and a few steps to the side. Then switch roles. The attacker scores 1 point for each crossing within 8 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Call for and Receive the Ball on the Move (Den Ball aus der Bewegung heraus verlangen und annehmen)","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Call for and Receive the Ball on the Move (Den Ball aus der Bewegung heraus verlangen und annehmen)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Half court, with 2 to 3 players holding a ball and 6 to 7 without. Players without the ball move and offer a target to receive a pass. Progression: the quality of the stop and catch; then the timing of catch, pass, catch; then stop after the catch and start with a dribble; then start with a dribble after the catch and pass straight off the dribble.","source":"[BASPO Training Forms](https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691761148/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-den-angriff-situationsangepasst-und-zielstrebig-aufbauen.pdf) p.15–16","sourceUrl":"https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691761148/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-den-angriff-situationsangepasst-und-zielstrebig-aufbauen.pdf"},{"key":"U12|compete|Pass, Cut & Replace Drill","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Pass, Cut & Replace Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line on each wing and one player with the ball at the top of the key. The top player passes to a wing and cuts to the basket looking for the return pass. The wing without the ball replaces the cutter at the top, and the cutter goes to the back of the line on that wing. The wing with the ball passes to the top; that player reverses the ball to the opposite wing and cuts, and the sequence repeats.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Purposeful movement, 4 on 4","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Purposeful movement, 4 on 4","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"FIBA's teaching sequence for four players. Player 2 leads for the ball and faces the basket after the catch; player 1 cuts after passing. A defender is added, and 2 is encouraged to back cut when they cannot receive on the perimeter. After 2 back cuts, 3 replaces by cutting to the perimeter, and 2 moves to the spot 3 started from. Then 4 replaces 3, and 2 moves to where 4 started. Finish by reversing the ball from one side to the other. Start with no or few defenders, then let them play; give instructions on the run without stopping the activity, or stop for no more than a minute, as in a time-out. Start positions are shown in the manual's diagrams.","source":"Mini p.46–47","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U12|compete|Get Open 1 on 1 from Wing","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"Get Open 1 on 1 from Wing","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"An attacker and a defender on each wing, with a coach or passer at the top of the key; up to 2 groups per half court. The attacker passes to the coach, and the defender denies the return pass. The attacker must get open in 5 seconds or less and catch near or behind the 3-point arc. When the coach passes back it is live 1 on 1, with 3 dribbles to score after the catch and finishes in the paint only. The attacker then defends and the defender goes out.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|compete|3-Second Deny 1 on 1","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"3-Second Deny 1 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"An attacker and a defender on each wing, with a coach or player holding the ball at the top of the key; 2 groups per half court. The defender denies the ball for 3 seconds while the attacker cuts to the block and back out to the wing. After 3 seconds the passer tries to pass in, and it is live 1 on 1. The attacker then defends and the defender goes to the end of the line. Next step: 5 seconds to score after the catch. Competitive version: 1 point for a deflection and 3 for a steal, with each player keeping their own score.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|compete|3-Person Pass, Cut and Replace","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"3-Person Pass, Cut and Replace","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three: attackers on each wing and at the top of the key with the ball, each guarded by a defender who may not steal until the coach's whistle. The top player passes to a wing and cuts to the basket; the wing without the ball replaces at the top, and the cutter fills the empty wing. The offence repeats this while the defenders move with their players. On the whistle it is live. A score is 1 point, and that team stays on against a new defence. A steal or defensive rebound is a turnover, and a new defence comes in.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|compete|3 on 3 Deny","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Moving without the ball","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 3 Deny","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three, lined up outside the three-point line. Attackers may only dribble sideways around the arc and may not drive. Defenders deny every pass by overplaying the passing lanes. The offence may score only from a backdoor pass, and each possession lasts 5 to 7 seconds; once a backdoor cutter gets the ball, play is live. Each team gets 3 to 5 possessions in a row, then new teams rotate in. Scoring: a steal is 1 point, a lay-up off a backdoor cut 2 points, any other score 1 point.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Defending the 2-on-1 Drill","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing, drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"Defending the 2-on-1 Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One player at the top of the key with a ball, one in the short corner on the same side, and one defender starting at the block who must guard both. The top player either goes for a lay-up or passes. After a pass, the corner player must catch and shoot, and the defender works on contesting that shot.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/6/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Dunker Spot 2 on 1","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing, drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"Dunker Spot 2 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two attackers and one defender at each basket. The attackers start in the dunker spots, along the baseline just outside the lane but not quite in the short corner; the defender plays in the middle. The defender or a coach passes to one attacker to start, and the two play 2 on 1 to finish at the rim. Next step: 2 or 3 passes before a shot; no dribble or only one; or a third attacker near the free-throw line and a second defender for 3 on 2.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Skip Pass 2 on 1","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing, drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"Skip Pass 2 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines, one on each wing; one line has the balls, and one defender starts under the basket. The player with the ball makes a skip pass to the opposite wing, and the receiver attacks the basket on the catch. At the same time the defender runs to touch either block, and then it is live 2 on 1. The attacker reads the defender and either drives to score or drives and kicks to the opposite wing. Maximum 2 passes. Next step: a defender on the ball for the skip pass, who steps off after it. FIBA says skip passes \"may not be suitable at this age group\" and that reversing the ball achieves the same result (Mini p.43).","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|compete|2 on 1 Bunch Spacing Game","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing, drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 1 Bunch Spacing Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three, two attackers and one defender, all bunched together with one hand each on the ball. Play is live when an attacker grabs the ball. The offence must spread out at once, not lined up \"3 in a row\", then pass, move and look to score while keeping good spacing. The offence plays 3 or 4 possessions in a row, then roles change. Plan 11 plays it with no dribbling, so the offence must pass and cut. Next step: one side of the court only, or a second defender for 3 on 2. Competitive version: points for baskets or for keeping spacing for a set time.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/), 11, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/"},{"key":"U12|compete|3 on 1 Drive and Kick Reaction","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing, drive and kick","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 1 Drive and Kick Reaction","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three attackers around the three-point line (top and both wings). One defender in the middle holds a ball and passes to start. The offence passes until a wing drives to the basket, and the other two space themselves in reaction to the drive. The defender must take one of them away, and the driver passes based on the defender's position. For the first few reps the coach does not set the off-ball spacing. Change the set number of passes so the drive comes from a different spot each time. Next step: extra defenders for 3 on 2 or 3 on 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/), 4, 5","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Contested Shooting Game","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Contested Shooting Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"An attacker on each elbow and a line under the hoop; use as many hoops as possible. One elbow player shoots and the other contests. The first player under the hoop passes to the shooter, and as soon as the pass leaves the passer's hand the defender sprints to contest. Rotate through the roles quickly. After a few rounds, the shooter takes one stationary dribble before shooting, and the defender may go only on the catch. Then change the contest angle to the baseline, or the shot spot. Count makes, individually or as teams, for a set time or to a set number.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/10/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Hot Shot Shooting","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"Hot Shot Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three: shooter, defender and passer-rebounder. The defender starts facing the passer, with space between them and the shooter. The shooter moves one way to get free before the pass. The defender contests at the level the coach sets: guided (close out and contest without blocking) or live (may fully challenge the shot). Coaches may add one dribble before the shot, a drive on the catch, or a choice to shoot or drive. Bursts of five shots per player, with optional scoring such as 1 point per make.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/"},{"key":"U12|compete|1 on 1 Create Space","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting against a defender","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 Create Space","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs, up to 3 per basket. The attacker starts with the ball outside the arc or in the mid-range, with the defender within arm's distance. The attacker may use up to 2 dribbles to create space for a pull-up jump shot and may not enter the paint. The defender may not steal but may distract the shooter in any way. Switch after 3 reps. Next step: the defender may not move until the attacker dribbles, and a false start that fools the defender earns a bonus point; later, the defender may steal.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Jump to the Ball Drill","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts and help defence","kind":"activity","name":"Jump to the Ball Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line on the baseline, a player on each wing, and the coach at the top with the ball. The first baseline player sprints to close out on the coach. The coach passes to either wing, and on the pass the defender jumps to the ball in a good defensive stance. The receiver takes 1 to 2 dribbles toward the middle, sees the defender, retreat-dribbles and drives baseline for a lay-up. The defender fills the open wing, and the wing player goes to the back of the baseline line. A new player passes to the coach and closes out. 4 times each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|compete|2-Person Closeout Drill","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts and help defence","kind":"activity","name":"2-Person Closeout Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A coach with the ball on one wing, another coach or player on the other wing, and two lines on the baseline. The defender guarding the ball calls \"ball, ball, ball!\". When the ball goes to the other wing, that defender jumps to help, calling \"help, help, help!\", and a player from the other line closes out on the ball. When the ball comes back, the closeout player becomes the helper, a new player closes out, and the first defender leaves. On the whistle or \"LIVE\", the player with the ball looks to drive and score, and it is live 2 on 2 to a score or a stop.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Closeout 1 on 1 Drill","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts and help defence","kind":"activity","name":"Closeout 1 on 1 Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A passer at the top of the key, an attacker on the wing and a defender at the free-throw line; run several games at each hoop. The passer passes to the wing and the defender closes out. On the catch it is live, with 3 dribbles to score. The attacker scores two or three points for a basket; the defender gets one point for each stop (steal or rebound). Then the defender goes to the passing spot, the passer to the wing, the wing player off, and a new defender comes in.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Tracing the Ball 2 on 2 Game","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts and help defence","kind":"activity","name":"Tracing the Ball 2 on 2 Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two attackers stand 12 to 15 feet (about 3.5 to 4.5 m) from the basket and 5 to 6 feet (about 1.5 to 2 m) apart. Two defenders with balls start under the basket. The defenders pass to the attackers, close out and trace the ball. One attacker passes their ball to a coach, and it becomes live 2 on 2: the passer may cut at once, and the other attacker may dribble and attack or shoot. The offence has 5 seconds to score. Rotation as printed: the attackers become the defenders and two new defenders come in; the plan does not say who attacks next.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/10/"},{"key":"U12|compete|2 on 2 Closeout","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts and help defence","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 2 Closeout","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One attacker on the wing and one at the top of the key, two lines on the baseline across from them, and a coach with the ball. When the coach passes to one attacker, the first two baseline players close out to the ball or to help, and they play 2 on 2. Each team gets 1 shot. The attacking pair goes off, the defending pair attacks, and the next baseline pair closes out. Games to 5.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/10/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Shell Drill – 4 on 4","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts and help defence","kind":"activity","name":"Shell Drill – 4 on 4","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of four, one on offence and one on defence. The offence has only perimeter players and no post players. After 5 passes the ball is live and the offence may try to score. Switch teams every possession. Next step: the offence may score only from a drive and kick-out.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/"},{"key":"U12|compete|3-Second Box Out Game","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"rebounding","group":"Rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"3-Second Box Out Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball, placed on the floor. Players take four steps away from the ball, and the defender faces the attacker with their back to the ball. When the coach yells \"shot\", the defender makes contact and boxes out while the attacker tries to touch the ball; the defender tries to hold for 3 seconds. Switch roles. The defence gets 1 point for each 3-second hold; the offence gets 2 points each time it touches the ball. Play for 2 minutes, with players keeping their own score.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 11](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/11/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/11/"},{"key":"U12|compete|1-on-1 Box Out Game","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"rebounding","group":"Rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"1-on-1 Box Out Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three with one ball; several groups can share a hoop. A shooter shoots from random spots, and the other two, one attacking and one defending, battle for the rebound. The rebounder tries to finish against the defender, and play goes on until a score or the coach calls \"time\". Rotate roles after a few rounds. 1 point for a rebound and 1 for a basket; after a set time the top scorer at each hoop wins. Next step: add a player guarding the shooter, so it becomes live 2 on 2 off the rebound, with the shooter's teammate named first.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U12|compete|2 on 2 Box Out Game / 3 on 3 Rebounding Battle","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"rebounding","group":"Rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 2 Box Out Game / 3 on 3 Rebounding Battle","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams of two (Starter) or three (All-Star) start under the basket, moving in a circle, with no set offence or defence. The coach or a player throws the ball at the rim. Both teams box out, and the team that rebounds becomes the offence. If the other team steals or rebounds a miss, it must take the ball back behind the three-point line. Play until a score or the coach calls time, then two new teams come in. Each team gets 2 to 3 turns, and teams track scores to find the winner.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/), 7; All-Star Plan 4","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Square Rebounding","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"rebounding","group":"Rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Square Rebounding","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four attackers in a square (both blocks and both elbows), each matched by a defender; the coach has the ball at the top of the key or on the wing. Before shooting, the coach calls a rotation: \"across\", \"up and down\" or \"diagonal\", and the defenders rotate and box out in that direction. On the coach's shot, make or miss, play is live. If the offence rebounds, it plays live half court to a score, stop or turnover. If the defence rebounds, it goes into live full-court transition.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U12|compete|Chaos Rebounding","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"rebounding","group":"Rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Chaos Rebounding","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"2 on 2 or 3 on 3 in the half court, with extra players on the sideline to rotate in. The coach shoots, or passes to a player who must shoot at once; the defence does not contest this first shot. Once the shot goes up, play is live, and either team can rebound and try to score. Play to a score, or stay live on every rebound until a target such as the first team to two baskets. Next step: a 5-second shot clock, a pass before scoring, or the first shot from outside the lane. Competitive version: bonus points for offensive rebounds that lead straight to a basket.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/"},{"key":"U12|compete|3-Cone Game (3-Hütchen-Spiel)","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Transition","kind":"activity","name":"3-Cone Game (3-Hütchen-Spiel)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three on the baseline with one ball; cones in the middle and on each side. 3 on 0: the ball carrier dribbles to touch the middle cone while the other two touch the side cones (never the same one), then they attack the basket with at most two passes. 3 on 1: one defender from the next group may start defending; their two teammates join only after that defender wins the ball or a basket is scored. 3 on 2: two defenders. 3 on 3: all three defenders must first touch a side marker; a far marker gives a fast break, a close one a 3 on 3 game.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.16","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U12|compete|3 on 1 Transition","age":"U12","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Transition","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 1 Transition","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of four, each lined up in one of the four corners of the court; one ball. One player from each team starts: three on offence with the ball at half court, and one on defence guarding the hoop. The offence tries to score as quickly as possible. After the shot, the rebounder stays on offence and is joined by one player from each corner line on that side, and those three attack the other way. The shooter goes back on defence. After a score or turnover the drill resets with players from the corners. Competitive version: 1 point per steal or rebound; top scorer after a set time wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|game|3 on 3 Continuous","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 Continuous","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three, full court. One team starts at half court with the ball, and two teams start on defence, one at each end; extra teams line up on the baselines at both ends. The offence attacks one basket. A score earns 1 point and keeps the ball to attack the other way. A waiting baseline team replaces the defence that was scored on; if none is waiting, that defence stays on. Whenever a defence gets a stop, it takes the ball the other way. All-Star plays to 3 makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/9/); All-Star Plan 1, 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/9/"},{"key":"U12|game|3 on 3 – Defense Wins","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 – Defense Wins","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three; use both baskets with four or more teams. The offence starts with a live ball and players move and play freely. After every change of possession the ball goes back to the top of the key. If the offence scores, it moves to defence and a new offence comes on. If the defence gets a stop, it earns 1 point, stays on defence, and a new offence comes on. Games to 4, with no \"check\" so play stays quick.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|game|3 on 3 No Dribble Game","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 No Dribble Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three; use as many hoops as possible. One team starts on offence at spots around the arc. Play live 3 on 3 with no dribbling by the offence. After a made basket or a turnover, the ball is inbounded. There is a 30-second shot clock. A team that scores twice wins the round, and new teams rotate in. Next step: 1 point for a defensive stop.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/"},{"key":"U12|game|Post 3 on 3 Game","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Post 3 on 3 Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Attackers and defenders on the block, the wing and the top of the key; one ball. The wing passes into the post, and once the ball is in the post it is live 3 on 3. If the defence steals or rebounds, it must clear the ball behind the three-point line. Play to a score or a set time. First team to 5 points wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/"},{"key":"U12|game|Game on One Basket (Auf einen Korb)","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Game on One Basket (Auf einen Korb)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Half court, one basket; 2 on 2, 3 on 3 or 4 on 4 (4 on 4 uses one half court per game). Start with a \"check\": the attacker passes the ball low to the defender, who hands it back from below when ready. After a foul, violation, 3-second violation or out-of-bounds, the other team gets a check. After a basket play goes on, and the new attacking team must first pass or dribble outside the 3-point line. Defence is 1 on 1, no zone; the 3-second rule applies. Attackers stay at least 4 m apart and look for 1 on 1 drives first.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.14","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U12|game|4 on 4 alignments","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"4 on 4 alignments","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"4 Out: an open post with no set post player; a player may post up after cutting to the basket, then go back out. It works on pass and cut, flash cuts and back cuts. Low Post Triangle: a post player is introduced; it works on passing to the post, cutting off the post and back cuts. High Post Triangle: the post moves to the high post; it works on back cuts, post moves facing the basket, cutting off the post and driving from the wing. All players get turns in the post and on the perimeter. Positions are in the manual's diagrams.","source":"Mini p.47; p.44","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U12|game|4 on 4 Continuous with Constraints","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"4 on 4 Continuous with Constraints","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three teams of four (add substitutes to teams if numbers are odd), full court. One team starts at half court on offence; the other two defend, one at each end. After a score the offence quickly takes the ball and attacks the other basket; a defence that gets a stop becomes the offence and attacks the other basket. Constraints: a score within 7 seconds of gaining possession counts double; three defensive stops in a row win the game; once a team gains possession and crosses half court, it may switch baskets. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U12|game|4 on 4 No Dribbles","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"4 on 4 No Dribbles","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of four play 4 on 4 with no dribbling. Encourage players to cut and move without the ball. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/10/"},{"key":"U12|game|5 on 5","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 5","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of five, with substitutes if needed. Play until a team makes 3 shots, and encourage players to cut, move the ball and use the concepts they have learned. All-Star Plan 1 (\"5 on 5 Controlled\") plays 4-minute full-court games and, when a coaching point comes up, stops play, makes the point and restarts.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/), 5, 8, 12; All-Star Plan 1","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/3/"},{"key":"U12|game|5 on 5 With Constraints","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 5 With Constraints","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of five. Only two dribbles are allowed each time a player catches the ball; more than two is a turnover. After one or two games with this rule, change it to no dribbles at all. Play 3 to 4 games of 3 minutes each. Starter Plan 11 also plays the no-dribble game on its own in 3-minute games, a dribble being a turnover; All-Star Plan 3 (\"5 on 5 Dictated – No Dribbles\") plays it full court to 3 made baskets.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/), 11; All-Star Plan 3","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/4/"},{"key":"U12|game|5 on 5 Dictated – Minimum 4 Passes","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 5 Dictated – Minimum 4 Passes","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Full-court games to 4 made baskets. No shot may be taken until the team with the ball has made 4 passes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U12|game|5 on 5 Half Court","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 5 Half Court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of five (more if there are substitutes but not enough for three teams), half court. One team defends for 5 possessions; with a third team, the offence changes every possession. A possession ends with a made shot, a rebound or a turnover; a deflection out of bounds does not end it. Each team gets 5 possessions on defence, and the team with the most stops wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/"},{"key":"U12|game|8-minute quarters","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"8-minute quarters","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The quarter length used at the 2026 China U12 national finals.","source":"[China Sports Daily via Sina](https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml)","sourceUrl":"https://finance.sina.com.cn/jjxw/2026-05-15/doc-inhxytzf3301813.shtml"},{"key":"U12|game|Every player plays a full quarter","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"Every player plays a full quarter","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player plays at least one full continuous quarter and at most two; five play the 1st quarter and a different five the 2nd.","source":"[Basketball Ireland rules, U12](https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/NationalUnderageRules.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/NationalUnderageRules.pdf"},{"key":"U12|game|Press in the 4th quarter only","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"Press in the 4th quarter only","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pressing only in the 4th quarter, and not by a team leading by 15 points or more.","source":"Basketball Ireland rules, U12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U12|game|Score gap capped","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"Score gap capped","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The scoreboard does not show a difference of more than 20 points.","source":"Basketball Ireland rules, U12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U12|game|Help defence, defensive 3 seconds","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"Help defence, defensive 3 seconds","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Help defence is allowed; the defensive 3-second rule applies; player-to-player only, no double-teams.","source":"Basketball Ireland rules, U12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U12|game|No 3-point basket","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"No 3-point basket","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A score from outside the 3-point line counts 2.","source":"Basketball Ireland rules, U12","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U12|game|Bring in 3 seconds and backcourt step by step","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"Bring in 3 seconds and backcourt step by step","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"From age 12, introduce the 3-second rule and the backcourt rule progressively, at a 3.05 m basket with a size 5 or 6 ball, in teams of 3 to 5 for the basic 3 on 3 school game.","source":"[BASPO Basketball at School](https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf) p.13","sourceUrl":"https://www.mobilesport.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/2018/11/MT_11_18_Basketball_d.pdf"},{"key":"U12|game|Performance-league U12","age":"U12","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U12","kind":"rule","name":"Performance-league U12","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"In Germany, a state's top (performance-level) U12 league may play 5 on 5 with the time rules (3, 5, 8, 14/24 seconds) and one time-out per half, but keep the 2.60 m basket and compulsory playing time for every child.","source":"[DBB Miniregeln](https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://basketball-bund.de/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/07/DBB_Jugend_Miniregeln_2024.pdf"},{"key":"U14|routine|SHRed Injuries Basketball warm-up","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Injury-prevention warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"SHRed Injuries Basketball warm-up","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"As on the U12 tab: about 10 minutes before the basketball warm-up at every practice and game, Level 1 or Level 2 of each exercise.","source":"[University of Calgary SHRed page](https://live-ucalgary.ucalgary.ca/shred-injuries/all-sports/basketball)","sourceUrl":"https://live-ucalgary.ucalgary.ca/shred-injuries/all-sports/basketball"},{"key":"U14|routine|Explosion Leaps","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Explosion Leaps","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Start on the sideline in a low squat, knees bent, hips down and hands on the floor in front. On the coach's command, jump up and out as high and as far as possible, land balanced on two feet, and repeat to the opposite sideline. Once each way. The aim is height and distance with the body under control.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Closeout, Slide & Backpedal Drill","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"defence","group":"Defensive footwork","kind":"activity","name":"Closeout, Slide & Backpedal Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line on the baseline where it meets the lane line. The first player sprints to a closeout at the elbow, defensive-slides across the lane to the other elbow, then backpedals to the baseline and joins the back of the line. The next player starts when the one ahead reaches the elbow. 2 minutes each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Zig Zag Defensive Slides – with Dribbler","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"defence","group":"Defensive footwork","kind":"activity","name":"Zig Zag Defensive Slides – with Dribbler","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball, half the group in one baseline corner and half in the opposite corner. The defender starts in a stance with their back to the court. The dribbler goes side to side, changing direction at will, and the defender slides back at angles to cut them off for the length of the court. The next pair starts when the pair ahead reaches the free-throw line. Defenders push off the back foot and stay low with hands out; the coach sets the dribbler's speed to the group's level. 3 x down and back.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Contact Dribble","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Contact Dribble","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball. One partner dribbles in a stationary stance, the way the coach specifies. The other gives contact by fouling the dribbler on the arms, shoulders, lower body and so on, in a natural way that serves the drill. The dribbler plays through the contact and keeps control. Swap roles after the time; 2 x 30 seconds each.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Catch a Second Ball Series","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Catch a Second Ball Series","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with two balls. One player dribbles with one hand while the partner tosses the second ball to the free hand. The dribbler catches it under control with soft hands, not smacking it back, and tosses it back. Repeat with both hands, and both players take a turn. Next step: use a tennis ball, or make a dribble move before each catch. 4 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Rhythm Dribbling","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Rhythm Dribbling","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each, spread out on the court. For the set time, players work on rhythm dribbling: standing tall and relaxed, then dropping low to make a move; changing speeds; taking several steps within one dribble; or dancing while they dribble. 3 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|routine|2-Ball Jab Drill","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"2-Ball Jab Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two balls each (or share with a partner), spread along the baseline in a good stance, pounding both balls at once. The coach stands at the free-throw line and raises the left or right hand. Players quickly jab the matching foot out to the side and bring it back under them. The coach keeps switching hands for the set time. 2 x 30 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|routine|2 Ball Alternate & React","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"2 Ball Alternate & React","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two balls each (or share with a partner), starting on the baseline in a good stance and dribbling the balls alternately. The coach starts at the free-throw line and stays in front. When the coach raises the left or right hand, players take one big explosive step forward with that foot first, still dribbling both balls. At half court they turn and come back, until time runs out. 2 x 30 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Backdoor Pass Lay-Up Drill","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Backdoor Pass Lay-Up Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines, one at the top and one on the wing or in the corner, with the balls in the top line; two groups or four lines per basket, based on numbers. The ball handler dribbles toward the wing. The wing player times the cut, steps up, then cuts backdoor, and the ball handler snaps a bounce pass to the cutter for a lay-up. Players rebound and switch lines. 20 makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Filling the Corner Drill","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Filling the Corner Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line on each wing, with a ball in one line. The ball handler drives baseline side, looking to score. As they drive, the player on the other wing drops to fill the corner. Near the basket the driver passes to the corner player, who is ready with hands up and knees bent, and who catches and shoots. Players rebound and switch lines. The team keeps going until it reaches the set number of makes, then repeats the other way. Printed as 2 games to 8.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 9","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Reverse Pivot Series","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Reverse Pivot Series","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines on the wings with balls. The first player stands wide off the block facing the ball line, catches a pass, does the move, rebounds and goes to the back of the line; the passer is next. Then the other side. The eight moves: (1) reverse pivot rip for a lay-up; (2) reverse pivot rip opposite for a lay-up; (3) reverse pivot straight into a jab and shot; (4) reverse pivot opposite straight into a jab and shot; (5) reverse pivot, jab, attack opposite for a pull-up; (6) the same off the opposite foot; (7) reverse pivot, jab, attack opposite for a move of choice; (8) the same off the opposite foot. 2 each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Mixed Drills – Footwork Focus","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Mixed Drills – Footwork Focus","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball, within 15 feet (about 4.5 m) of the basket. The coach calls an action; players do three of it, then take a shot. The actions: 2 feet forward and backward; 2 feet side to side; 1 foot forward and backward; 1 foot side to side; 2-foot hop up and down (each foot); 1-foot hop up and down (each foot).","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Backboard Form Shooting","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Backboard Form Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each (partner up if needed), at as many backboards as possible. Players stand a short distance from the basket, to the side of the rim, and shoot at the backboard, aiming high so the ball touches the board on the way down. 3 x 8.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|routine|Full Court Transition Shooting Drill","age":"U14","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Full Court Transition Shooting Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"At least 9 players, in three lines on one baseline: one side, middle, other side. One player from each line starts on the opposite baseline with a ball, and one at half court. The half-court player runs toward a baseline, catches a pass from the baseline player there, shoots, rebounds and joins that line. The passer sprints the length of the floor, catches a pass at the other end and shoots, then rebounds and becomes the passer there. The coach can vary the shot. When a team reaches the set number of makes, teams rotate spots. 3 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/), 6","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U14|smallGames|Ball Tag Game","age":"U14","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Ball Tag Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Everyone starts and stays inside half court, with one basketball. One player is \"it\" and runs for the set time trying to tag others; after a tag, the tagger's turn is over. A player holding the ball cannot be tagged, so as the tagger comes close, the ball holder passes to the player about to be tagged. Coaches can adjust it to the team, for example 2 taggers or 2 balls.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/12/); [MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/12/"},{"key":"U14|smallGames|Breakout Dribble","age":"U14","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Breakout Dribble","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four defenders stand on the corners of a square, with one dribbler in the centre. The defenders stay on the edge of the square and move side to side only, to contain. The dribbler tries to break out, without contact or force, within 5 to 8 seconds. If time runs out, the coach calls a change and a new dribbler comes in; after a breakout, the dribbler rotates out. Defenders rotate every 2 or 3 possessions. Next step: a second attacker in the square, or defenders dribble while they contain. Competitive version: 1 point per breakout, or less time to break out.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|smallGames|Partner Running Pass Drill","age":"U14","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Partner Running Pass Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three, up to three groups at a time, starting on the baseline. Two passers face each other with a defender between them. Against live defence, the passers pass back and forth as they run the court and try to score a lay-up; they must complete a set number of passes first. Roles switch on the way back. Next step: one dribble allowed after each catch. Passers do not drop passes, call each other's names and lead the receiver.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/); [MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U14|smallGames|Around the World – Makes!","age":"U14","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Around the World – Makes!","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Five cones mark the shooting spots and players split evenly among them, the first in each line with a ball. Each player shoots, rebounds and goes to the back of the line. A team shoots from its spot until it makes 5, then rotates, until it has 5 makes at all five spots. Then the team must make a half-court shot. First team to finish wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|smallGames|25 Point Free Throw Game","age":"U14","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"25 Point Free Throw Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One shooter per basket, at as many baskets as possible. Each player shoots 5 free throws in a row. Each shot scores: 5 for a swish that comes back so the shooter can catch it with one foot still on the line; 4 for any other swish; 3 for a make that touches the rim or board once; 2 for two touches; 1 for three touches or rolling around the rim; 0 for a miss. Highest score wins, out of 25.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Hook Pass Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Using screens","kind":"activity","name":"Hook Pass Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line on the wing, a ball each, with a cone or chair on the wing as the ball screen and the coach on the wing, closer to the corner than the screen. Each player dribbles off the ball screen and throws a hook pass to the coach, then flashes back toward the ball for a catch and finish, gets the rebound and goes to the back of the line. 5 each hand.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Using a Screen Shooting Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Using screens","kind":"activity","name":"Using a Screen Shooting Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A passing line on the wing; on the opposite side, a shooter, a pretend defender and a screener. The shooter uses the screen to catch and shoot, then goes to the back of the line. Rotate: the defender becomes the shooter, the screener the defender, and the passer runs across to screen. The group works together to get many makes quickly. 10 makes each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Using a Screen Shooting Game","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Using screens","kind":"activity","name":"Using a Screen Shooting Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of four: passer, screener, shooter, defender. The shooter starts in the corner and the screener comes from the wing to set a pin-down. On each rep the defender plays the screen a different way: under it (the shooter steps back into open space), trailing (the shooter curls and keeps the defender behind), or jumping over it (the shooter fades to the corner or cuts behind the screen to the hoop). No dribbles. 1 point for an open shot, 1 more for a make. Next step: the shooter at the top of the key with a flare screen from the weak-side wing, or on the wing with a cross screen from the top of the key.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Using a Screen 2 on 1 Game","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Using screens","kind":"activity","name":"Using a Screen 2 on 1 Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A wing attacker and defender, a passer (player or coach) on the opposite wing, and a screener at the top of the key, at as many hoops as possible. The screener sets an off-ball screen for the wing attacker and may not receive the ball or score. The attacker has five seconds to use the screen, then plays live 1 on 1. Players then rotate roles. Scoring: open shot 1 point, made shot 2, a screen that leads to a shot 1; most points wins. Next step: 2 on 2 with a defender on the screener; the defence may not switch. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/5/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Screening Reaction Bursts","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Using screens","kind":"activity","name":"Screening Reaction Bursts","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Half court: at least three attackers (passer, screener, cutter) against matched defenders (3 v 3) or fewer (3 v 2, 4 v 3). A pass from the screener or cutter to the passer starts it. The screener screens the cutter's defender and the cutter uses the screen to get open for a shot. Each burst runs on a timer (for example 2 minutes) or to a target number of screen-and-read actions (for example 3 to 5). Rotate after each burst of 3 to 5 possessions. Next step: other screen types (down, back), or at least 2 screens before a shot. Competitive version: points for screen-and-score actions or wide-open shots; add a trailing defender, or the defence switches.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Post Pass & Screen Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"Post Pass & Screen Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line near the top of the key with a ball; players on the wing and the block. The first player passes to the wing, who passes into the post and then screens for the top player. The top player cuts toward the wing while the screener slips to the basket, and the post passes to the cutter for a shot (or, as an option, to the screener). Rotate: post to the back of the line, screener to post, shooter to wing. Repeat to a set number of makes. Competitive version: add three defenders and play live 3 on 3 once the post catches, with double points for a score off the screen after the post entry. 8 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA Starter Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/starter/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|compete|2 on 1 Post Passing Game","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 1 Post Passing Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three, at least two groups per hoop: one attacker on the post, one on the wing or top of the key, and one defender on the post. The post defender may play behind, front, or deny the pass from the side with an arm. The passer must get the ball into the post three times, and the post makes a scoring move based on the defence. Rotate after three sets. When everyone has played each role, the defender moves onto the passer and the game restarts. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U14|compete|2 on 2 With a Post","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 2 With a Post","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Four lines: one on each wing and one on each block. The first player in each wing and post line plays offence and the second plays defence. The wing starts with the ball and must make an entry pass to the post, and the post defender must play behind. Once the ball is in the post, play is live 2 on 2. Everyone takes turns on the wing and in the post. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Pound Pass Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"Pound Pass Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball, at least a lane width apart. The player with the ball pounds it, then makes a one-hand overhand pass with the hand behind the ball and fingers pointing up. The partner catches with one hand and passes back the same way. Work at an intense pace and change the dribble before the pass. 2 x 1 minute.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Pound Pass 1 on 1","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"Pound Pass 1 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners at least a lane width apart; the attacker faces the hoop and starts with the ball. The attacker pounds the ball and makes a one-handed pass, and the partner catches with one hand and passes back the same way. After 3 passes the attacker may attack the basket and play is live. Change the dribble before the pass. Switch attack and defence after 1 minute, and change partners after another. Next step: the attacker starts with two balls and dribbles both before passing. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/), 8","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Pressure Timing Bursts","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"passing","group":"Passing under pressure","kind":"activity","name":"Pressure Timing Bursts","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners: a passing line at the top of the key and a cutting line under the basket. At the top, one player dribbles in a set area while the partner pressures. Under the basket, an attacker and a defender. The cutter picks a side and cuts toward that wing, and the top player must time the pass while the defenders try for deflections. On the catch, the cutter has 3 dribbles to score. Groups play bursts of 3 to 5 possessions and keep score.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/9/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Ball Up Ball Down Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts","kind":"activity","name":"Ball Up Ball Down Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"An attacker with a ball in each corner and on each wing, with a matching defender on the baseline. Defenders close out. On the coach's call, attackers lift the ball overhead (\"ball up\") or lower it into a low stance (\"ball down\"). On \"ball up\" the defender pushes off the back foot and steps closer with proper hand position; on \"ball down\" the defender pushes back off the front foot. Defenders move without wasted movement. 3 minutes each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Closeout to Prevent Middle","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts","kind":"activity","name":"Closeout to Prevent Middle","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines on the baseline and one player on each wing. The first in each line passes to the wing and closes out. The attacker may dribble twice in any direction while the defender works to stop a drive to the middle. After two dribbles the attacker picks up the ball and the defender pressures it. Rotate: the defender becomes the wing attacker and a new defender steps up from the baseline. 3 reps each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Partner Closeout Shooting","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Closeouts","kind":"activity","name":"Partner Closeout Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners in open space within their 1–2-dribble range, up to three pairs per half court. The defender starts with the ball, passes to the attacker and closes out. If the defender leaves space, the attacker shoots. If not, the attacker takes 1 or 2 dribbles into a pull-up or lay-up; if the defender cuts off the first dribble, the attacker counters the other way with a crossover or another move. Rotate after 3 or 4 bursts. Next step: the attacker catches on the move, for example running from the wing to the top of the key, and attacks the closeout. 3–4 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Timing the Dribble","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"Timing the Dribble","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Guards in pairs with a ball in open court; bigs in pairs with a ball at the blocks. Guards: the defender matches the rhythm of the dribble and tries to steal as the ball leaves the dribbler's hand. Bigs: the attacker dribbles and bumps the defender for one or two dribbles to learn the timing; then the defender \"pulls the chair\" by stepping back, so the attacker loses balance, loses the ball or travels. Partners take turns. 3 x.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Hand on Top Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"Hand on Top Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with one ball, up to three lines at a basket (both wings and the top). As the attacker drives, the defender moves with them and puts an arm on their body. The attacker tries to get the inside arm on top of and in front of the defender's arm, drives past and shoots. Partners switch until the shot count is reached. 3 each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U14|compete|1 v 1: close the driving line (1:1 Durchbruchslinie schliessen)","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"1 v 1: close the driving line (1:1 Durchbruchslinie schliessen)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"1 v 1. The attacker starts, and the defender may act only after the ball first touches the floor. The defender tries to force a change of hand or direction and keep the attacker out of the key. The action ends with a steal, a rebound or a basket. Next step: 2 v 2, with an extra attacker in the weak-side corner and the defender in help position in the key; then 3 v 3, adding an attacker and a defender on the weak-side wing.","source":"[BASPO Training Forms](https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691764795/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-kompakt-und-situationsangepasst-verteidigendefensive-duelle-erfolgreich-bestreiten.pdf) p.6–8","sourceUrl":"https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691764795/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-kompakt-und-situationsangepasst-verteidigendefensive-duelle-erfolgreich-bestreiten.pdf"},{"key":"U14|compete|1 v 1 for five seconds (1:1 während fünf Sekunden)","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"1 v 1 for five seconds (1:1 während fünf Sekunden)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The coach has the ball, with one attacker and one defender. The attacker tries to get free, receive and play 1 v 1. The defender must deny the catch for at least five seconds; if they do, roles switch. If not, the attacker plays 1 v 1 to a finish. Next steps: catch on the wing (45°) with no backdoor cut; then with the backdoor; then in the corner; then anywhere in the quarter court except the key; then anywhere in the quarter court; then Winners' Ball (whoever wins the ball attacks next).","source":"[BASPO Training Forms](https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691764795/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-kompakt-und-situationsangepasst-verteidigendefensive-duelle-erfolgreich-bestreiten.pdf) p.9–10","sourceUrl":"https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691764795/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-kompakt-und-situationsangepasst-verteidigendefensive-duelle-erfolgreich-bestreiten.pdf"},{"key":"U14|compete|Deny Layup Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"Deny Layup Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line with balls on each wing, the coach at the top, one attacker and one defender on court. The defender denies the attacker for a few seconds while the coach tries to pass to the attacker. After deflecting the pass, the defender goes the other way for a lay-up, and the former attacker contests it. 3 minutes each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/10/"},{"key":"U14|compete|1 on 1 – 3 Stops","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 – 3 Stops","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A player on each wing and one at the top, with the defender starting in the paint and extra players at the end of a wing line. The defender must get three 1 on 1 stops: against one wing, then the top, then the opposite wing. Then rotate: the defender to the back of the wing line, the front of that line to the top, the top player to the opposite wing, and that wing player becomes the defender.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Full Court Man to Man","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"Full Court Man to Man","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs matched by skill and position, a ball per pair, half the pairs on each baseline. The court is split lengthways on the lane line and players may not cross the middle. The defender tries to force as many changes of direction as possible, sliding to stay in front or running to get back in front if beaten. The attacker tries to score at the far basket. The next pair starts when the one ahead reaches half court. At the far end, pairs join that line and swap roles. 3 x each.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|compete|2 v 2 and 3 v 3 without dribbling (2:2 und 3:3 ohne Dribbling)","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"1 on 1 defence and denying","kind":"activity","name":"2 v 2 and 3 v 3 without dribbling (2:2 und 3:3 ohne Dribbling)","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"2 v 2 in a quarter court. The attackers try to score without dribbling. If the defence wins the ball, it tries to score too. The defenders pressure the ball carrier, deny direct passes and break fast after a steal. Next steps: half court without fixed roles; then full court with transition. The title names 3 v 3, but the description covers 2 v 2 only.","source":"[BASPO Training Forms](https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691764795/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-kompakt-und-situationsangepasst-verteidigendefensive-duelle-erfolgreich-bestreiten.pdf) p.3–5","sourceUrl":"https://assets01.sdd1.ch/assets/lbwp-cdn/mobilesport/files/1691764795/mobilesport-basketball--trainingsformen-kompakt-und-situationsangepasst-verteidigendefensive-duelle-erfolgreich-bestreiten.pdf"},{"key":"U14|compete|Tag & Pursue Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Tag & Pursue Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of three at a basket (two attackers with one ball, one defender), at least two groups per basket. The defender guards the player without the ball from help position, calling \"help, help, help\". The ball goes to that player and the defender closes out calling \"ball, ball, ball\"; the ball goes back and the defender returns to help, calling \"help\". The attacker shoots, and the defender finds their own player, tags them and pursues the ball. In MVP Plan 4 the defender finds the shooter instead.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/); [MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/5/"},{"key":"U14|compete|4 on 4 Rebounding","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"4 on 4 Rebounding","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Lines on the perimeter inside the 3-point line and on the baseline at the post; the coach has the ball. The first player in each line defends and the next steps in on offence. The coach passes or shoots so the defenders adjust to the ball, and the defenders pass back to the coach. After a short time the coach shoots and the defenders box out, or tag and pursue. If the offence rebounds it may try to score, and the defence stays on; either way a new offence comes in. A defence must get 5 rebounds in a row before rotating off.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/), 6","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Rebound & Score","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Rebound & Score","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three players in the paint and a line on the baseline. The coach shoots and all three go for the rebound; whoever gets it tries to score and the other two defend. All shots are in the paint and \"limited fouls are called\". A player who scores 3 times comes out and the next in line jumps straight in. No pauses: the ball is always live, even as it goes through the net.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|compete|4 on 4 Shell to Rebound","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"4 on 4 Shell to Rebound","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of four, one team on offence and one on defence. The offence has only perimeter players, no posts, and may only pass and cut, with no screens. After 5 passes the ball is live and the offence may try to score, still without screens. A defensive rebound ends the possession. After each possession the defence steps off and the offence goes to defence. First team to 5 defensive rebounds wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Shell Drill 5 On 5 – with Post","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"Shell Drill 5 On 5 – with Post","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of five, one team on offence and one on defence. The offence has perimeter players and one post, and may pass and cut but not screen. After 5 passes the ball is live and the offence may try to score, still without screens. Switch teams every possession. The emphasis is post defence and how to help on a post player.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/), 11; [MVP Plan 11](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/11/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U14|compete|3 on 3 from Help the Helper","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and rebounding","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 3 from Help the Helper","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three; use both baskets with four or more teams. Each possession starts with a baseline drive: a help defender stops the ball and the third defender helps the helper. The driver must kick the ball out, then play is live. After every change of possession the ball goes back to the top of the key. A team that scores keeps the ball. No check, to keep it quick.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/), 9; [MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Half Court Spacing Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"Half Court Spacing Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Tape on the floor marks the offensive spots, for example 3 around 2, 4 around 1, or 5 out, with a line at each spot; groups of five and a ball. One group at a time passes, cuts and fills open spots, keeping all five spots filled. After a set time the coach calls for a shot; the group shoots and the next group rotates in. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U14|compete|5 on 0 Fast Break","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"5 on 0 Fast Break","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Five lines on the baseline: the middle, one each side of the middle, and two wide. The first two players in the middle line each have a ball. All sprint to the far basket. The middle player passes to the nearest player on the right, who passes to the player left of the middle line, who passes to the wide player on the right for a lay-up; that player swings through to switch sides with the left-wing runner. The middle player rebounds without letting the ball bounce, and the players nearest the middle line switch sides inside the paint. Repeat straight away coming back, then the next group goes. Players rotate through every starting spot. 5 x.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Balance the Floor Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"Balance the Floor Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The five lines of 5 on 0 Fast Break, numbered 1 to 5; one ball and the coach. The first group gives the coach the ball. The coach passes to one player and calls 2 or 3 numbers. Those players run the court calling out where the ball and their teammates are, so the floor is balanced; the catcher dribbles or passes to help. Once balanced, they take a quick shot or practise drive and kick, as the coach decides. 5 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|compete|3 on 0, 2 on 1","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 0, 2 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three lines on the baseline, with 2 balls in the middle line. The first three run the court passing, outside players wide: the middle player passes to one side and gets it back, to the other side and gets it back, then to the first side for a lay-up. The shooter sprints back to defend. The middle passer must get both feet in the paint, then attacks the other way 2 on 1 with the wing who did not shoot, who takes the rebound. After one shot, the next three start. 6 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U14|compete|3 on 2, 2 on 1 Modified","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 2, 2 on 1 Modified","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three attackers spread outside the 3-point line in the half court, one with the ball; two defenders near the basket. Play is live when the ball handler attacks the hoop: 3 on 2 until a score or turnover. The player who shot or turned the ball over goes back on defence on the opposite side, and the other two attackers become the new defenders. The two defenders bring the ball back and try to score 2 on 1. The next possession starts with three new attackers in new half-court spots. Next step: the 2 on 1 stays on one side of the court; crossing is a turnover. 4 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U14|compete|3 on 2, 2 on 1","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 2, 2 on 1","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Full court. Three lines on the baseline (two wings and the middle, with the balls) and two defenders at the far end. The first player in each line attacks 3 on 2, with only one shot. Whoever shoots or turns the ball over gets back on defence; the other two attackers stay as the new defenders. The two original defenders bring the ball back 2 on 1. The next three start from the baseline, and finished players go to the back of the lines.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 11](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/11/); [MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/11/"},{"key":"U14|compete|Line Up Transition Defense Drill","age":"U14","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Spacing and transition","kind":"activity","name":"Line Up Transition Defense Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams: five on the baseline, five on the extended free-throw line facing them; one ball and the coach. The coach passes to a baseline player, whose team attacks the far basket. The player on the free-throw line directly across from the ball handler sprints to the baseline in front of them, turns and sprints back into play. The defence must stop the ball and match up, playing 5 on 4 until that player gets back. Teams switch positions after each possession. First team to the set number of defensive stops wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/), 7, 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|game|3 on 3 Dictated","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 Dictated","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of three; use both baskets with four or more teams. The coach dictates the first action and players must start the possession that way; after it, the ball is live and players play freely. Set versions: every possession starts with a pass and cut or a pass and ball screen (All-Star Plan 2), or with a down screen (All-Star Plan 11; MVP Plans 3, 11). After every change of possession the ball goes back to the top of the key. A team that scores keeps the ball. No check. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/), 9, 11; [MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|game|3 on 3 Kings Court","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 Kings Court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3 or 4 (larger teams substitute), on as many baskets as possible, all games at once. One court is the Kings Court, where a win is worth 3 points. When the Kings Court game ends, every game ends. The team with the highest score among the other courts' winners then plays the Kings Court winner. Teams track their scores over a set period, such as 10 minutes. The page does not say what a win on the other courts is worth.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/12/"},{"key":"U14|game|3 on 2 Trailer Game","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 2 Trailer Game","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, full court. Each team always defends the same basket with two players and has a player or line on the sideline at half court. When the three attackers cross half court, the defending team's sideline player runs to the middle of half court, touches the floor and joins the defence. After a score or a stop, those three defenders attack the other way, and the other team's sideline player does the same as they cross half court. The aim is to score in the 3 on 2 before the third defender arrives. Games to 5 makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/6/"},{"key":"U14|game|11-Person Transition Drill","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"11-Person Transition Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two defenders wait at each basket, three attackers start near half court and the other players wait on the sidelines. The three attack one basket 3 on 2. After a shot or turnover, whoever gets the ball goes straight on offence with the sideline players on that half, dribbling or passing up the court to attack the other basket 3 on 2, and play continues. Players not in play fill open spots as the coach directs. Next step: start each possession with an outlet pass to a sideline player; at most two passes before a shot. Competitive version: each player scores 1 point for a rebound or live-ball steal and 1 when their team scores.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/), 11; [MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/7/"},{"key":"U14|game|5 on 1 Build Up","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 1 Build Up","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Five attackers in any alignment (for example 5 out, 4 out 1 in, or staggered); five defenders in a row behind the baseline; one ball. The first defender passes to any attacker and closes out. After each pass the next defender is released, so the game builds from 5 on 1 to 5 on 5; once the fifth defender is in, play is live. The offence moves the ball quickly to use its advantage before the defence recovers. Next step: keep the offence on for a 3-minute burst, changing alignment each possession; change the order the defenders enter; or the offence must finish on a paint touch.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U14|game|3 Trips & Flip","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 Trips & Flip","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two or more teams, full court. The offence starts a half-court possession and can keep trying to score off its own rebounds. After a score, a defensive rebound or a steal, play goes live for one possession the other way. Then the team that started with the ball brings it back to its starting basket, completing the three trips. The teams then flip, so the other team starts in the half court. The coach can dictate a play or action. Games to 5 makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U14|game|Half Court 5 on 5 – 3 seconds","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Half Court 5 on 5 – 3 seconds","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of five, half court, games to 5 makes. After the first pass, any player who holds the ball longer than 3 seconds commits a turnover. A team that scores keeps the ball.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U14|game|5 on 5 Situational Play","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 5 Situational Play","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams are given a specific game situation, for example: a team has the ball and leads by 1 with 45 seconds left; or a team has the ball and trails by 2 with 2:30 left. The situations teach game and clock management, and the coach reinforces key points as they come up.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/), 6; [MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/3/"},{"key":"U14|game|Small-sided ladder","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Small-sided ladder","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"1 on 1 to 5 on 5 on courts sized to the numbers: 15 × 6 m, 22 × 8 m, 24 × 11 m, 26 × 13 m and 28 × 15 m. Two bouts of 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 minutes respectively, with 2 minutes' rest. FIBA rules, except a foul gives loss of possession. In national-level U14 and U16 players, the smaller formats gave more technical actions per minute; 2 on 2 gave the most shots.","source":"[Clemente 2020](https://doi.org/10.2478/hukin-2019-0082)","sourceUrl":"https://doi.org/10.2478/hukin-2019-0082"},{"key":"U14|game|2 on 2 full-court bouts","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"2 on 2 full-court bouts","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Full court, player-to-player defence, 3 bouts of 4 minutes with 2 minutes' passive rest. In U15 players, 2 on 2 gave a higher heart rate and more dribbles, shots and rebounds than 4 on 4, with no significant difference in passes.","source":"[Conte 2017](https://doi.org/10.3390/sports5010003)","sourceUrl":"https://doi.org/10.3390/sports5010003"},{"key":"U14|game|Shot clock from the front court","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"Shot clock from the front court","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Start the shot clock only when the offence has the ball in its front court, for at most 32 seconds.","source":"Mini p.34","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U14|game|30-second shot clock","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"30-second shot clock","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The USA Basketball standard for ages 12–14.","source":"[USA Basketball rules](https://www.quickscores.com/downloads/mwrhuachucayouth_USA_Basketball_Youth_Guidelines__Rules_and_Standards.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://www.quickscores.com/downloads/mwrhuachucayouth_USA_Basketball_Youth_Guidelines__Rules_and_Standards.pdf"},{"key":"U14|game|No zone","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"No zone","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Player-to-player defence only.","source":"Mini p.34; [Basketball Ireland U14](https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/NationalUnderageRules.pdf); [Basketball New Zealand U14](https://nz.basketball/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/BBNZ-Regs-Bk-3-Competition-as-of-5-Mar-2026.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/NationalUnderageRules.pdf"},{"key":"U14|game|No double-teams","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"No double-teams","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Help defence allowed, double-teaming not.","source":"Basketball Ireland U14","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U14|game|Press, but not when far ahead","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"Press, but not when far ahead","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pressing allowed in all four quarters, but not by a team leading by 15 or more (Ireland) or 25 or more (USA Basketball).","source":"Basketball Ireland U14; USA Basketball rules","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U14|game|Groups A and B","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"Groups A and B","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Split the squad into two groups that each play half of the 1st and 2nd quarters, as China's junior high league does.","source":"[Shaanxi notice](https://finance.sina.com.cn/wm/2026-05-25/doc-inhzchzh3486712.shtml)","sourceUrl":"https://finance.sina.com.cn/wm/2026-05-25/doc-inhzchzh3486712.shtml"},{"key":"U14|game|Everyone plays a full quarter","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"Everyone plays a full quarter","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each player plays at least one full continuous quarter and at most three.","source":"Basketball Ireland U13–U14","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U14|game|Four 8-minute quarters","age":"U14","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U14","kind":"rule","name":"Four 8-minute quarters","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Used by USA Basketball (12–14), Basketball New Zealand U14 and China's junior high league.","source":"As above","sourceUrl":null},{"key":"U16|routine|SHRed Injuries Basketball warm-up","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Injury-prevention warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"SHRed Injuries Basketball warm-up","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"As on the U12 tab: about 10 minutes before the basketball warm-up at every practice and game, Level 1 or Level 2 of each exercise.","source":"[University of Calgary SHRed page](https://live-ucalgary.ucalgary.ca/shred-injuries/all-sports/basketball)","sourceUrl":"https://live-ucalgary.ucalgary.ca/shred-injuries/all-sports/basketball"},{"key":"U16|routine|Follow the Leader Warm-Up","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Follow the Leader Warm-Up","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"All players spread out on the baseline. Each player takes a turn leading the team in a stretch or active movement from the baseline to half court and back, once each. Encourage players to pick a movement that has not been used yet. The plan calls it a way to develop leadership and comfort having a voice among teammates.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Dynamic Warm-Up Part 11","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Dynamic Warm-Up Part 11","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each movement once: Bear Hold, Stationary A Skip, Pogo Jumps, Forward Walking Lunge, Lateral Chop, Carioca. The plan prints only the movement names, with a video, and gives no written description.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Dynamic Warm-Up Part 14","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"warmup","group":"Movement warm-up","kind":"activity","name":"Dynamic Warm-Up Part 14","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Each movement once, from the baseline to half court or the other way: Walking Hamstrings, Knee Hugs, Jump to Stick, Lateral Squat Hold, Quick Feet, 2 Foot Hops. The plan prints only the movement names, with a video.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Early-Cross Jab Series","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Early-Cross Jab Series","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs. The ball carrier dribbles at the partner, who acts as the defender, and switches hands before getting too close. Right after the switch, the carrier drops into a jab step that takes them past the defender in the opposite direction. Work both hands and swap roles.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Hard Step, Hard Pound Breakdown Drill","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Hard Step, Hard Pound Breakdown Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each, starting on the baseline. Players take two fast dribbles forward with the right hand, then stop hard, with a forceful right-hand dribble and a forceful step landing together. Work both sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|routine|2 Ball Resistance Dribbling","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"2 Ball Resistance Dribbling","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs with 2 balls, on the baseline. The dribbler holds a strong, firm stance and alternates the two balls while the partner applies resistance to the shoulders, hips and outside of the knees. Halfway through the time, the dribbler changes to dribbling both balls at the same time. The partner then moves in front, puts two hands on the dribbler's shoulders and resists as the dribbler drives forward dribbling. Then partners switch.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Over the Line Dribble Drill","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Over the Line Dribble Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each. Straddle a line, one foot on each side, knees bent in an athletic stance. Dribble with the right hand on the right side of the line, then control the ball so the next bounce lands on the left side, changing sides every dribble with one hand. Then switch hands.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/10/"},{"key":"U16|routine|In & Out Overs Drill","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"In & Out Overs Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs, one ball. One partner holds an arm straight out at about waist height. After 1 or 2 dribbles, the dribbler makes an in-and-out move over the arm, bounces the ball under it and brings it back to the starting hand. Keep going for 30 seconds, then switch hands and roles.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 10](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/10/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/10/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Figure 8 Dribble 4","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"Figure 8 Dribble 4","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each, lined up with space on the baseline. Players dribble a figure 8 using 4 dribbles: on the right side with the right hand, between the legs from right to left, on the left side with the left hand, then between the legs from left to right. Keep the pattern going for 2 x 20 seconds.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/"},{"key":"U16|routine|1-Ball Pound, 1-Ball Control","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"dribbling","group":"Dribbling","kind":"activity","name":"1-Ball Pound, 1-Ball Control","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two balls each, spread out facing the coach. One ball stays on the floor under one hand while the player holds a good stance and dribbles the other with eyes and chest up. The coach calls numbers 1 to 4, each a direction for moving the floor ball out and back: on \"3!\" players keep dribbling and move the floor ball to the 3 spot and back. Use both hands. The plan's text does not say which direction each number means.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/"},{"key":"U16|routine|In & Out Around the Lane","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"In & Out Around the Lane","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each, in one line on the baseline where it meets the lane line; use more baskets if needed. The first player dribbles up the lane line with a quick in-and-out move, repeating it 2 to 3 more times until past the elbow. Above the elbow they turn back toward the basket and attack for a lay-up. The next player starts when the one ahead is halfway up the lane. 3 each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Floater Series","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Floater Series","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Small groups, one basket and one ball per group. Each player shoots 3 reps of 4 types of floater. First, right hand off the left leg; once everyone has taken 3, left hand off the right foot. Then floaters off two feet: 3 with the right hand, then 3 with the left. 2 sets.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/), 10","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Jump Through the Hips & Finish","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Jump Through the Hips & Finish","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Lines, with a couple of balls at the front of each. Players dribble in as for a normal lay-up. Before reaching the basket they imagine a defender cutting in front, take a hard last dribble while jumping and twisting the hips toward the middle of the floor, land, and jump straight back up to shoot. 3 minutes each way, on both sides of the court.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Finishing Through Contact","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"layups","group":"Lay-ups and finishing","kind":"activity","name":"Finishing Through Contact","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line on the wing, each player with a ball; a coach with a pad. Each player makes a dribble move and drives for a lay-up. As the player goes up to finish, the coach makes contact, with a pad or otherwise. The player rebounds and goes to the end of the line. The plan's point: finish through contact by jumping off two feet to keep balance and strength. 3 each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Hard Step & Shoot","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Hard Step & Shoot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three lines at the top, each player with a ball. The first player in each line takes two hard, fast right-hand dribbles, makes a quick stop combining a hard dribble and a hard step, shoots, rebounds and goes to the back of the line. Work both sides, 4 minutes each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Loop Shooting Drill","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Loop Shooting Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines, 2 balls in each, and one shooter on the court at the block. The first player in a line passes to the shooter, who shoots, rebounds, gives the ball back to the line it came from and goes to the back of that line. The passer loops around the outside of the shooter to the far side of the lane, catches from the other line and shoots. Keep looping. The coach sets the shooting distances.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U16|routine|Up & Out Shooting","age":"U16","block":"routine","type":"shooting","group":"Shooting","kind":"activity","name":"Up & Out Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One line on the baseline under the basket; the first player has no ball and the next few have one. The first player runs to the top of the key, then makes a straight-line cut to the wing. The next player passes; the cutter catches and shoots, or takes another designated shot, rebounds, gives the ball to the next player without one and goes to the back of the line. The passer runs to the top of the key and cuts straight to the opposite side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/"},{"key":"U16|smallGames|1st to 50","age":"U16","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"1st to 50","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams of 5, one ball. The team with the ball may not dribble, screen or shoot; every pass counts toward 50, and the first team to 50 wins. A steal, turnover or deflection gives the other team the ball, and they start counting their passes. A team that wins the ball back carries on from its own count: a team that had 12 restarts at 12. The defence can pressure aggressively, so passes must be strong and accurate.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U16|smallGames|Circle Trap 3 on 3","age":"U16","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Circle Trap 3 on 3","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Three attackers in a circle on the perimeter with one ball; three defenders inside it. Two defenders trap and go for a steal, deflection or forced turnover; solo traps are not allowed. The third is the interceptor, reading passes. The attackers move the ball quickly; the two without it adjust their spacing for better passing angles. Any steal, deflection or defensive touch switches roles. With more than 2 teams, a new three comes on as offence, the offence moves to defence and the defence goes off. Next step: change the number of defenders, such as 4 v 3; add a shot clock or touch count; or, on a turnover or the coach's call, flow into live full-court 3 v 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA All-Star Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/all-star/page/8/"},{"key":"U16|smallGames|Beating 2 Defenders","age":"U16","block":"smallGames","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Beating 2 Defenders","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 3 with one ball, starting at the centre of half court. One player attacks; the other two defend from just above the 3-point line. The attacker has 10 seconds to make a move and get a shot off against both. Then the next group comes on. The three rotate every possession so each gets a turn on offence. Keys from the plan: attack the weaker defender, split the defenders if they leave space, and create space.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U16|compete|In & Out Crossover to Shot","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shots off the dribble","kind":"activity","name":"In & Out Crossover to Shot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Several lines at a basket; the first 1 or 2 players in each line have a ball. The first player makes an in-and-out crossover, shoots, rebounds and passes to the next player in line, who repeats. Work in both directions, 3 minutes each way. Competitive version: keep score.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Slide to Open Step Back","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"shooting","group":"Shots off the dribble","kind":"activity","name":"Slide to Open Step Back","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines on the wing with 2 balls in each line; use other baskets if needed. Each player takes 2 to 3 slide dribbles toward the baseline, chest facing the basket. After the last slide dribble they make an open step back or side step and shoot a jump shot from inside the 3-point line, rebound their own shot and pass to the next player. The two lines race to 4 makes, then switch sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Curl & Pop Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Screens","kind":"activity","name":"Curl & Pop Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines, one at the elbow and one on the same-side wing; the coach at the top; balls. The elbow player tosses a ball to the coach and goes to screen for the wing player. The wing curls hard, expecting the defender to go over the top. The screener expects the defender to help on the curl and pops back to the ball, and the coach passes to the screener for the designated shot. Switch lines and repeat to 15 makes, then switch sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Pick & Pop Shooting","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Screens","kind":"activity","name":"Pick & Pop Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball; posts start on the block and guards above the opposite wing; a coach passes and names the shots. The first post passes to the coach and sets a ball screen for a guard. The guard comes off the screen and passes to the popping post, who takes the coach's designated shot, rebounds and goes to the back of the post line. The guard relocates to a spot the coach chooses, catches from the coach, takes the designated shot, rebounds and goes to the back of the guard line. 20 makes each way, timed.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Pocket Pass & Shoot","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Screens","kind":"activity","name":"Pocket Pass & Shoot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball; posts start on the block and guards above the opposite wing; a coach. The first post passes to the coach and sets a ball screen for the guard. The guard comes off the screen and makes a pocket pass to the big, who catches, finishes in the paint, rebounds and goes to the back of the post line. The guard relocates to a spot the coach names, catches from the coach, takes the shot the coach names, rebounds and goes to the back of the guard line. 20 each way.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Slip Pass Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Screens","kind":"activity","name":"Slip Pass Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A passing line at the top of the key, a screening line on the wing and a shooting line on the same baseline; a coach defends the screener. As the screen is set, the coach either drops back to defend the screen or steps up to jump the shooter coming off it. If the coach drops, the passer hits the shooter coming off the screen; if the coach steps up, the screener slips to the basket for the pass. Rotate passer to screener to shooter to passer. A player can take the coach's role.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/), 2, 5","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Side Ball Screen Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"teamPlay","group":"Screens","kind":"activity","name":"Side Ball Screen Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Guards, paired by ability, in two lines high on each wing near half court; bigs, paired, in two lines where the lane line meets the baseline. On one side the first guard pair and first big pair take offence and defence. The big sets a ball screen when the guard signals, and the four play one 2 on 2 possession off the side screen. Then the other side goes. Players rotate sides and swap offence and defence with their partners. Coaches may add restrictions and set how to defend the pick and roll. 10 minutes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U16|compete|GUARDS: Same Hand, Same Foot Finishes","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"guards","group":"GUARDS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"GUARDS: Same Hand, Same Foot Finishes","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Small groups in a line on each side of the basket; the first two players in each line have a ball; use more baskets. The first player dribbles to the basket and finishes with the hand on the side they approach (right hand on the right, left on the left), jumping off the foot on the same side as the shooting hand (right foot for a right-hand finish). Complete the reps, then switch sides. The plan notes this finish may feel awkward at first.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U16|compete|GUARDS: Rebound, Sprint & Shoot","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"guards","group":"GUARDS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"GUARDS: Rebound, Sprint & Shoot","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two lines under the basket, each player with a ball; a coach. The first player tosses the ball to the coach, jumps and touches the backboard, sprints wide to touch where half court meets the sideline, then sprints back to the same-side corner to catch from the coach and take the designated shot. The player in the other line starts right after. Guards run the floor wide and are ready to shoot on the catch.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/"},{"key":"U16|compete|GUARDS: Pick & Roll Situational Shooting","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"guards","group":"GUARDS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"GUARDS: Pick & Roll Situational Shooting","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every player has a ball, inside half court on one side; a chair or cone is the screener and the coach plays the screener's defender. The player dribbles in, signals for the ball screen, sets it up and comes off it, expecting the on-ball defender to go over the top and get caught. The coach hedges, drops back or plays between the two. The player reads the coach and takes the appropriate shot.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U16|compete|GUARDS: Pick & Roll Reads-On Ball","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"guards","group":"GUARDS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"GUARDS: Pick & Roll Reads-On Ball","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every guard has a ball, high on the wing; a cone or chair is the screener and the coach is the on-ball defender. If the coach goes over the screen, the guard dribbles off it for a pull-up jump shot. If the coach goes under, the guard can stop behind the screen and shoot, or fake a re-screen and come off the other way for a jump shot. Work both sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U16|compete|GUARDS: Pick & Roll Reads – Second Defender","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"guards","group":"GUARDS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"GUARDS: Pick & Roll Reads – Second Defender","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Every guard has a ball, high on the wing; a cone or chair is the screener and the coach plays the screener's defender. If the coach hard-hedges, the guard splits for a shot. If the coach sits back, the guard comes off the screen and shoots, or freezes the coach and attacks the basket.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U16|compete|BIGS: Catch it High, Keep it High","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"BIGS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"BIGS: Catch it High, Keep it High","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs, one ball, several pairs per basket. The shooter stands in the paint around the block. The partner tosses the ball high; the shooter jumps, catches with arms outstretched, keeps the ball high on landing, then immediately jumps back up and shoots. The partner rebounds and repeats until the shooter makes 5, then they switch. The ball must not come down on the catch.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U16|compete|BIGS: Dribble. Bump. Balance.","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"BIGS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"BIGS: Dribble. Bump. Balance.","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Pairs, one ball. The attacker starts with the ball under the backboard, with the defender directly behind, between the attacker and the basket. The attacker takes one dribble, bumps the defender back, balances on two feet and goes up strong to finish. Repeat to the set number of makes, then switch roles; work both sides. Key points from the plan: create space with a power dribble and a strong bump, stay low, strong and balanced, and set a good angle for the shot.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 1](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/"},{"key":"U16|compete|BIGS: Rebound, Sprint & Seal","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"BIGS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"BIGS: Rebound, Sprint & Seal","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Post players in a line with a ball at one basket; a coach. The first player bounces the ball off the backboard, rebounds, makes a good outlet pass to the coach, sprints to half court and back to the paint. The next player defends in the paint, holding a ball overhead with both hands. The first player seals the defender, catches the coach's pass and finishes. The defender then does the backboard toss and the pattern repeats. Run it from both sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 7](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/7/"},{"key":"U16|compete|BIGS: Defending Post Cross Screen","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"BIGS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"BIGS: Defending Post Cross Screen","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One offensive and one defensive post on each block; the coach has the ball on the wing. First teach how to defend a big-to-big post screen without switching. The ball-side player sets a cross screen for the opposite big. Run several reps of defending the screen before adding the pass. The offence rotates to defence and two new attackers come in; with few posts, rotate one spot at a time.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U16|compete|BIGS: Pick & Roll Reads-Rotation","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"BIGS (position-specific)","kind":"activity","name":"BIGS: Pick & Roll Reads-Rotation","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Posts in a line on the baseline; one player in the corner with a defender; the coach in the opposite slot. The first post screens for the coach, who goes away from the corner player and passes to the rolling post. On the catch, the roller reads the corner defender: if the defender helps, pass to the corner for a shot; if not, finish at the basket. Rotate: roller to corner shooter, corner shooter to defender, defender to the end of the line.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 4](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/4/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Pass & Up & Under Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"Pass & Up & Under Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"One player on each block, with a line on the wing in front of each block; the first players in each line have a ball. The first player makes a post entry pass to the post on the block. The post catches, makes an up-and-under move, rebounds and goes to the back of the line. The passer runs to the block, posts up and gets ready for the next pass. Then switch sides.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U16|compete|4-5-4 Drop Step Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"4-5-4 Drop Step Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 3 at a basket with 2 balls, one on each block. One player rebounds and quickly puts each ball back on its block. The shooter goes block to block making a drop step and finish each time. After the 4th drop step, the player on the other side switches in and does 5, then the last player does 4. The plan says the 4-5-4 count keeps the changeovers quick and smooth.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Spin 5 Series","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"Spin 5 Series","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A ball each, starting on the baseline along the lane line; coach at the top. In turn, players pass to the coach, flash to the same-side elbow and make the move, until each has done all 5: turn and rip baseline with 1 dribble, quick spin back to the middle; the same with 2 hard dribbles and a big spin (both finish with no more dribbles); rip baseline with 2 hard dribbles, half spin toward the middle, turn back baseline for a shot; catch with the back to the basket, quick spin off an imagined defender for a shot of choice; one dribble with the back to the basket, quick spin into a shot of choice. Then the other elbow.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Post Pass Shooting Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"Post Pass Shooting Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line at the top with a couple of balls; one player on the post and one on the wing. The top player passes to the wing; the wing passes to the post and cuts; the post passes to the cutter for an open jump shot. The shooter rebounds and goes to the back of the line. The post flashes to the wing and takes a pass from the next player at the top, while the previous top player sprints to the baseline, then back to the block to post up. The pattern repeats to the set makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 12](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/12/"},{"key":"U16|compete|High Low Passing Drill","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"High Low Passing Drill","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The coach on the wing; a post player and a defender on the ball-side block; a line on the opposite block, each player with a ball. The first player in line passes to the coach. The coach looks into the post, where the defender is fronting, which signals the opposite-block player to flash to the high post. The coach passes to the high post, who makes a high-low pass to the post player to finish. Rotate: flasher to block defender, defender to block attacker, attacker to the back of the line. 4 each side.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|compete|2 on 2 From High Low Flash","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"2 on 2 From High Low Flash","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The coach on the wing; an attacking post and a defender on the ball-side block; an attacker and a defender on the opposite block; the others wait in pairs. The coach looks into the post, where the defender is fronting, and the opposite-block player flashes to the high post. The coach passes to the high post and the game is live for one possession. After the shot or turnover, players rotate. Games to 4.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Low-post decision 3 on 3","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"post","group":"Post play","kind":"activity","name":"Low-post decision 3 on 3","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"3 on 3 on one basket for 10 minutes; a team that scores keeps the ball. A basket counts only after a correct pass into the low post: from below the extended free-throw line, with the post defender behind (later also three-quarter fronting on the side away from the passer). Perimeter defenders may not intercept the pass into the post. A pass from above the line, or into a fronted post, is a wrong decision; a basket scored without a correct post pass does not count, and the other team gets the ball. Next step: the post with the ball passes out if a perimeter defender helps and plays 1 on 1 if not; perimeter players move to an open passing lane when their defender helps. +1 for each correct decision, −1 for a wrong one.","source":"[FIBA, Basketball for Young Players (2000)](<https://kki.is/library/Skrar/07.CAPITULO%207%20(Ingles).pdf>) ch.8, about p.298–304","sourceUrl":"https://kki.is/library/Skrar/07.CAPITULO%207%20(Ingles).pdf"},{"key":"U16|compete|Digging in the Post","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Post defence","kind":"activity","name":"Digging in the Post","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A line on the wing; the coach on the same-side block. The first player in line defends the next. The attacker makes a post entry pass to the coach, and the defender digs down to force a pass back out. After 3 reps the defender goes to the back of the line, the attacker becomes the defender and the next player attacks. Digging, as the plan defines it: the wing defender jabs and fakes down into the post, facing the court with long arms, one hand digging and the other trying for deflections.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/"},{"key":"U16|compete|1 on 1 From Post Cross Screen","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Post defence","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 From Post Cross Screen","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Partners. Two pairs start on offence and defence at the blocks; the coach has the ball on the wing. The ball-side player sets a cross screen for the other big, and defenders may not switch. Play is live when the coach passes into the post. After each possession the defence becomes the offence, the offence rotates off and a new pair defends. Games to 4.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 5](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/), 8, 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/5/"},{"key":"U16|compete|3 on 3 Post Entry & Play","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Post defence","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 3 Post Entry & Play","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3: one on offence, one on defence, extra teams behind the offence. The offence starts with a player at the top of the key with the ball, a post and a same-side wing. The wing gets open and makes a post entry pass; all passes are live and can be stolen. After the entry, guards may cut, screen or relocate while the ball is live, mostly staying on the same side. Defenders may dig in the post when it fits. Games to 3.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 8](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/8/"},{"key":"U16|compete|Load to the Paint","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and closeouts","kind":"activity","name":"Load to the Paint","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Groups of 4 or 5 on the baseline; the coach has one ball. The first group runs in a small circle in the lane until the coach tosses the ball up, and all call \"shot.\" The players allowed to crash the offensive boards run to touch the backboard and sprint back; the guards, or others not allowed to crash, run back as soon as the ball is tossed. All sprint to load the paint, then turn and close out to the nearest perimeter spot calling \"Ball! Ball! Ball!\" The coach points in a couple of directions and players slide, then clap and hustle to the baseline at that end as the next group starts.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 11](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/11/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/11/"},{"key":"U16|compete|1 on 1 – Stunt & Recover","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and closeouts","kind":"activity","name":"1 on 1 – Stunt & Recover","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The coach has the ball on the wing; a line at the top of the key. The first player in line defends and the next attacks. The coach drives toward the middle and the defender stunts; the coach kicks to the attacker and the defender closes out. They play one 1 on 1 possession with a limit of 3 dribbles. Then the attacker becomes the defender and the defender leaves. Stunt, as the plan defines it: a defender one pass away fakes a hand and foot at the driver, then closes out on their own player when the ball is passed out.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 2](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/2/"},{"key":"U16|compete|3 on 3 from Closeout","age":"U16","block":"compete","type":"defence","group":"Help defence and closeouts","kind":"activity","name":"3 on 3 from Closeout","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"A player on each wing and one at the top; three lines on the baseline opposite them; the coach with one ball. When the coach passes to a perimeter player, the first three baseline players close out and play 3 on 3. Each team gets one shot. Then the next baseline group comes on. The plan stresses good closeouts and good defence. Games to 5.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U16|game|Additive Transition","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"Additive Transition","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Two teams, one at each end, full court. The advantage team starts 2 on 1 toward the other end. After a shot or turnover, the lone defender gets a teammate and they come back 2 on 2. Each later shot or turnover adds one player to the attacking side: 3 on 2, back 3 on 3, down 4 on 3, back 4 on 4, down 5 on 4, back 5 on 5. After the 5 on 5 possession the teams swap and the new advantage team restarts. First team to the set points wins.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 9](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/)","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/9/"},{"key":"U16|game|3 on 3 Dictated – Screen the Screener","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 Dictated – Screen the Screener","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3; use both baskets with 4 or more teams. After every change of possession the ball goes back to the top of the key; a team that scores keeps the ball; no check ball. The coach starts with the ball on the wing. A guard on the ball-side block screens across the lane for a post coming to the ball side; then a player near the free-throw line screens for the guard, who comes off to the top. Then the ball is live. Games to 3 makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U16|game|3 on 3 Dictated – Elbow Pass","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"3 on 3 Dictated – Elbow Pass","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Teams of 3; use both baskets with 4 or more teams. After every change of possession the ball goes back to the top of the key; a team that scores keeps the ball; no check ball. Play starts with a pass from a guard at the top to the elbow. A second guard is on the same-side wing, so the two guards can run several cuts and screens on that side. Then the ball is live. Games to 3 makes.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 3](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/), 11","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/3/"},{"key":"U16|game|5 on 5 Full Court Man to Man","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"games","group":null,"kind":"game","name":"5 on 5 Full Court Man to Man","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"Balanced teams of 5, full court. Each player matches up with and guards one opponent full court. The coach stresses full-court pressure, with off-ball players staying on the line of the ball. Games to 5.","source":"[Jr. NBA MVP Plan 6](https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/), 12","sourceUrl":"https://jr.nba.com/basketball-practice-plans/mvp/page/6/"},{"key":"U16|game|24-second clock, 14 on a rebound","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U16","kind":"rule","name":"24-second clock, 14 on a rebound","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"FIBA shot clock; reset to 14 seconds after an offensive rebound.","source":"[USA Basketball rules, grades 9–12](https://www.quickscores.com/downloads/mwrhuachucayouth_USA_Basketball_Youth_Guidelines__Rules_and_Standards.pdf)","sourceUrl":"https://www.quickscores.com/downloads/mwrhuachucayouth_USA_Basketball_Youth_Guidelines__Rules_and_Standards.pdf"},{"key":"U16|game|Zone allowed, or not","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U16","kind":"rule","name":"Zone allowed, or not","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"FIBA allows zone from U16; New Zealand bans it at U16. Canada: bring in zone and presses only after basic skills hold up in games.","source":"Mini p.34; [Basketball New Zealand U16](https://nz.basketball/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/BBNZ-Regs-Bk-3-Competition-as-of-5-Mar-2026.pdf); [Canada Basketball ADM](https://westvancouverschools.ca/docs/CanadaBasketballADM.pdf) p.46","sourceUrl":"https://nz.basketball/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/BBNZ-Regs-Bk-3-Competition-as-of-5-Mar-2026.pdf"},{"key":"U16|game|Four 8-minute quarters","age":"U16","block":"game","type":"rules","group":"Rule options added at U16","kind":"rule","name":"Four 8-minute quarters","nameZh":null,"howItRuns":"The length used in Ireland's U16 schools competition and China's junior high league; full FIBA is four 10-minute quarters.","source":"[Basketball Ireland schools](https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/2026/PPSC%20HANDBOOK%202026-2027.pdf); [Shaanxi notice](https://finance.sina.com.cn/wm/2026-05-25/doc-inhzchzh3486712.shtml)","sourceUrl":"https://ireland.basketball/uploads/ed/2026/PPSC%20HANDBOOK%202026-2027.pdf"}]}
